"""emed_stem_overrides.py - rewritten stems for image-dependent EMED questions.

174 questions rewritten. Each stem now states the imaging/clinical finding in
words (recovered from the question's own explanation) WITHOUT naming the
diagnosis, so the reasoning step being tested is preserved.

Applied by uq.py at load time: {bank_name: {question_index: new_stem}}
"""

STEM_OVERRIDES = {
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Cardiology - Hypertension (MCQ)": {
        "9": "A 24-year-old man is brought to the Emergency Department after coming off his motorcycle at speed and striking his left flank on a kerb. He has no significant medical history and takes no regular medications. On examination he is alert, with a pulse rate of 96/min, blood pressure 118/74 mmHg, respiratory rate 18/min, SpO2 98% on room air and temperature 36.8°C. There is bruising and tenderness over the left flank. Urinalysis shows frank haematuria.\n\nHe remains haemodynamically stable and a contrast-enhanced CT of the abdomen is performed. This demonstrates a laceration of the lower pole of the left kidney measuring more than 1 cm in depth, with a contained perinephric haematoma. The laceration does not extend into the renal pelvis or collecting system, and there is no urinary extravasation on delayed imaging. The renal vessels enhance normally.\n\nWhich one of the following is the grade of renal trauma in this case?",
        "3": "A 79-year-old woman presents to the General Practice clinic for review after recent treatment for a chest infection. She is now symptom-free. She has a medical history of hypertension and osteoarthritis of her knees. Her regular medications are perindopril and paracetamol. She is a non-smoker and drinks about two glasses of wine a week. On examination she appears well with vital signs within normal limits, and the physical examination is unremarkable.\n\nA chest X-ray shows a well-defined soft-tissue mass in the superior mediastinum, extending upward from the thoracic inlet and lying alongside the trachea. The trachea is deviated to the right. The lung fields are clear, with no consolidation, no collapse and no pleural abnormality.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely explanation for the changes seen on the X-ray?",
        "6": "An 85-year-old woman presents after vomiting a small quantity of fresh blood. When she woke up this morning she felt nauseated and then vomited. She has never had anything like this before. Her only other current health issues are hypertension and osteoarthritis, for which she takes perindopril and paracetamol. The physical examination is unremarkable.\n\nAn endoscopy is performed. In the proximal stomach there is a large, well-circumscribed, rounded mass bulging into the lumen. The mucosa covering it is largely intact and of normal appearance, with no surrounding irregularity, heaping or shouldering. At the apex of the lesion there is a small central umbilication with an area of recent ulceration.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "23": "An 85-year-old woman presents to the General Practice clinic with a two-month history of a painless lump in her right breast. She has a medical history of hypertension, osteoarthritis, and type 2 diabetes. Her regular medications include metformin, amlodipine, and paracetamol. On examination, she appears well. Her pulse rate is 72/min, blood pressure 138/82 mmHg.\n\nThere is a firm, irregular, fixed mass in the upper outer quadrant of the right breast. The skin directly over the lump is drawn inwards, forming a dimple, and the dimpling becomes more pronounced when she raises her arms above her head. The nipple is normal, with no scaling, no discharge and no inversion. The skin is not thickened and there is no pitting of the pores, no ulceration and no visible mass protruding through the skin.\n\nWhich one of the following signs is present?",
        "31": "An 82-year-old man presents to his general practitioner for a routine assessment. His medical history includes hypertension and pulmonary fibrosis, both adequately treated. He has no other ongoing health issues, but mentions that over the last few weeks he has been experiencing some perianal itching.\n\nOn examination of the perineum there is a flat, darkly pigmented lesion in the perianal skin, approximately 12 mm across. It is asymmetrical, with an irregular, notched border and variation in colour from mid-brown to near-black across its surface. It is not fluctuant, not tender, and there is no surrounding erythema, no induration and no discharge. The patient does not know how long it has been there.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "57": "During a routine assessment, a general practitioner notices a lesion on an 87-year-old man's left upper eyelid. He reports it has been present for many years and does not bother him. It is painless but has now started to itch. It has not significantly changed in appearance over the years. He has several similar lesions on his back.\n\nOn examination the lesion is a well-demarcated, brownish-black plaque with a waxy, warty surface. It has a characteristic appearance of being 'stuck on' to the surface of the skin, and its edges are sharply defined. There is no ulceration, no rolled or pearly border, no telangiectasia and no bleeding.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "60": "A 28-year-old man presents to his general practice with a lump on his eyelid which has been mildly irritating but mostly painless. It has been present for three days.\n\nOn examination there is a discrete, firm, round nodule within the upper eyelid, away from the lid margin. The overlying skin is not red or warm, the lesion is not tender to palpation, and there is no discharge, no preseptal cellulitis and no involvement of the lash line. Visual acuity and eye movements are normal, and the globe is white and quiet.\n\nWhich one of the following is the best initial management?",
        "54": "A 51-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department with progressively worsening headache over three months, The pain is diffuse but worse on the right side, exacerbated by coughing and lifting. He reports associated nausea, vomiting, seizures, and left arm weakness. He has no significant medical history and takes no regular medications. He worked in synthetic rubber manufacturing for 30 years On examination, he appears unwell. His pulse rate is 88/min, blood pressure 145/85 mmHg, respiratory rate 16/min, SpO2 98% on room air, and temperature 36.8 C. Neurological examination reveals power of 3/5 in the left upper limb with normal tone and reflexes. The remainder of the examination is unremarkable. aoe LSA.) het ay LK \\ ores ape 5 wens Se? ad ont pis faust Cenk j 4 + ated 4, 3 acess (o> ae / \\ Pi. eee Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "66": "A 55-year-old man presents to the General Practice clinic for a general health review after presenting with elevated blood pressure during his previous two visits in the past six months. He was initially advised on controlling his hypertension with weight loss and diet modification before starting medical therapy, but he has since been unable to lose any weight. Since then, he was started on a calcium channel blocker. His medical history includes hypertension and hypercholesterolaemia. His current medications are amlodipine 5 mg daily and simvastatin. On examination, he appears well. His pulse rate is 76/min, blood pressure 155/90 mmHg, respiratory rate 12/min, SpO2 98% on room air, and temperature 36.5 C. His BMI is 33 kg/m? in the previous visit). His blood pressure from his previous two visits was 148/86 mmHg (six months ago) and 149/90 mmHg (three months ago) Blood laboratory results show raised triglycerides 3.0 mmol/L (0.5-1.7) and raised LDL cholesterol 3.0 mmol/L (<2.6) Which one of the following is the most appropriate management for this patient's hypertension?",
        "67": "A 21-month-old girl presents to the Emergency Department with a three-day history of cough, runny nose and lethargy. She was previously well, has no diagnosed medical conditions and is fully immunised. On examination she is febrile at 38.6°C with a respiratory rate of 44/min and mild subcostal recession. Auscultation reveals reduced air entry on the right.\n\nHer chest X-ray shows an area of increased opacity in the right mid-zone which obliterates the right heart border, while the right hemidiaphragm remains clearly visible. The lung volumes are otherwise preserved and there is no pleural effusion, no pneumothorax and no cardiomegaly.\n\nWhich one of the following best describes the abnormality on the chest X-ray?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Cardiology - Cardiac Arrhythmia (MCQ)": {
        "3": "A six-year-old girl is brought to the Emergency Department after a sudden onset of palpitations and shortness of breath. She has no significant medical history and takes no regular medications. On examination, she appears anxious with a heart rate of 240/min and blood pressure 100/60 mmHg.\n\nHer ECG shows a regular, narrow-complex tachycardia at 240/min with no discernible P waves. The onset was abrupt, and the rate does not vary with respiration or vagal distraction.\n\nWhich one of the following best describes the underlying mechanism of this arrhythmia?",
        "4": "A seven-year-old girl presents to the Emergency Department following a syncopal episode. She was running with friends when she suddenly collapsed, but spontaneously regained consciousness. This is her first syncopal episode, though her parents report that she sometimes experiences episodes of palpitations. She has no significant medical history and takes no regular medications. There is no family history of unexplained syncope or sudden cardiac death. On examination, she appears well with vital signs within normal limits.\n\nHer 12-lead ECG shows sinus rhythm with a short PR interval, a widened QRS complex, and slurred upstrokes at the start of the QRS complex, most clearly seen in leads V1–V4.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis based on the ECG findings?",
        "6": "A 54-year-old woman is brought into the Emergency Department, having been retrieved from her home in an unconscious state. She was found lying on the floor with a large number of pills and a bottle of vodka beside her. The medication packets were not found. On examination she is sedated and barely rousable, with a Glasgow Coma Scale score of E2 V2 M4. Her blood pressure is 90/65 mmHg, pulse 80/min, temperature 37.0°C and SpO2 94% on room air.\n\nShe is intubated, an intravenous line inserted, bloods drawn and a crystalloid bolus commenced. Her ECG shows sinus rhythm at 80/min with a normal QRS duration of 90 ms, but a markedly prolonged corrected QT interval of 520 ms. There is no terminal R wave in aVR, no bradycardia and no evidence of ischaemia.\n\nOverdose of which one of the following drugs is most likely to have caused these ECG findings and clinical symptoms?",
        "7": "A 35-year-old man is brought into the Emergency Department by ambulance after being found by his sister unconscious. He was found surrounded by vomit in his bathroom soon after taking a large number of pills. Collateral history from his sister reveals a medical history of depression, chronic back pain and migraines. His medications list includes fluoxetine, amitriptyline, paracetamol, oxycodone, propranolol and occasional sumatriptan. He does not drink alcohol, is a lifelong non-smoker and uses no recreational drugs.\n\nOn examination, he is drowsy but alert to voice. His pulse rate is 134/min, blood pressure 99/57 mmHg, respiratory rate 11/min, SpO2 99% on room air and temperature 37.1°C. On neurological examination he moves all four limbs on command; his pupils are responsive to light and dilated bilaterally. There is no evidence of mechanical injury. His peripheries are warm to the touch. His blood glucose is 5.2 mmol/L (3.2-5.5).\n\nHis ECG shows sinus tachycardia at 134/min with a broad QRS complex of 130 ms and a tall terminal R wave in lead aVR with a deep S wave in lead I.\n\nWhich one of the following medications is he most likely to have taken an overdose of?",
        "9": "A 28-year-old woman presents to the Emergency Department with a two-hour history of sudden-onset palpitations. She has no chest pain or shortness of breath. She has no significant medical history and takes no regular medications.\n\nOn examination she is alert, blood pressure 118/76 mmHg, and there are no signs of heart failure. Her ECG shows a regular narrow-complex tachycardia at 180/min with no discernible P waves and a normal QRS duration. There is no ST elevation and no delta wave.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate initial management?",
        "17": "An 80-year-old woman presents to the General Practice clinic with a three-month history of fatigue, dizziness, and two episodes of syncope. She has a medical history of hypertension and osteoarthritis. She takes no rate-limiting medications.\n\nOn examination her pulse is 38/min and irregular. Her ECG shows periods of marked sinus bradycardia alternating with sinus pauses of up to 4 seconds, and intermittent runs of atrial tachycardia followed by prolonged pauses on termination. There is no evidence of complete heart block and no acute ischaemic change.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?",
        "19": "A concerned 29-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department. He anxiously tells you that while on a run he noticed palpitations and that his fitness tracker had been recording his heart rate as high as 220.\n\nOn examination he is alert and haemodynamically stable, with a blood pressure of 122/78 mmHg. His ECG shows a regular narrow-complex tachycardia at 200/min with no discrete P waves visible and a normal QRS duration. Vagal manoeuvres have already been attempted without success.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate next treatment?",
        "20": "A 68-year-old man presents with a history of shortness of breath and productive cough with white sputum for the last 2 years. He reports that the shortness of breath was brought on by exertion but recently it has worsened and now occurs at rest. He has a 45 pack-year smoking history.\n\nOn examination he has a barrel-shaped chest with bilaterally decreased breath sounds, widespread expiratory wheeze and distant heart sounds. A chest X-ray shows hyperinflated lung fields with more than six anterior ribs visible, flattened hemidiaphragms and a narrow cardiac silhouette.\n\nWhich one of the following findings would be most useful in confirming the diagnosis?",
        "11": "A term newbom presents with signs of respiratory distress after birth and is transferred to the neonatal intensive care unit. Clinical examination reveals pallor, cyanosis of the lips and tongue, weak peripheral pulses, cold extremities, rapid breathing with flared nostrils, and bobbing of the head. Auscultation of the chest reveals a prominent S2. Urgent echocardiography confirms the diagnosis of hypoplastic left heart syndrome. Immediate initiation of continuous prostaglandin E1 infusion occurs, followed by the Norwood procedure. Nine days after the surgery, an ECG shows the following abnormality. Which one of the following is the most appropriate definitive management?",
        "14": "A 17-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department after being found collapsed at a party. His friends report he was well until two hours ago when he became increasingly drowsy and confused. He has no significant medical history and takes no regular medications. On examination, he appears unwell. His pulse rate is 86/min, blood pressure 82/60 mmHg, respiratory rate 6/min, SpO2 94% on room air, and temperature 36.0 C. His Glasgow Coma Scale score is 13/15. He has bilateral pinpoint pupils and intermittent myoclonic jerks. The remainder of his examination is normal. Which one of the following classes of agents most likely explains the history and physical examination findings?",
        "21": "68-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department with sudden onset left-sided crushing chest pain. He has a past medical history of type 2 diabetes mellitus and a previous myocardial infarction two years ago. On examination, his blood pressure is 70 mmHg systolic (no diastolic reading is achieved). He is confused with cool and clammy hands and has a raised jugular venous pressure with Kussmaul's sign present. His lungs are clear to auscultation. a Ute eae ee dea ruearaest penn tina sas! usd oenu esa | Poet PAMyi Hy { jan [ANE reer Geee oat iemei |i pARKSNOUPSLUERED oat avags anne iets iersaavotaeeaes | a tana fam (daa | ia iaeeta | ise mee Some jnEa AGH AUMPARDE GUY Reo SWE MUES eT LEM eee a = ae Bib ea (e { eet |e Nae fli ade (=o Le |e | Mean Wes ca Which one of the following is the most important next step in management?",
        "25": "A 50-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department with palpitations. Two hours ago he was discharged from the endoscopy suite following an elective endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography. On examination his pulse is 170/min, blood pressure 118/74 mmHg, and he is alert and not in pain. There are no signs of heart failure.\n\nHis ECG shows a regular broad-complex tachycardia at 170/min with a uniform QRS morphology in every beat, a QRS duration of 160 ms, and evidence of atrioventricular dissociation. He remains haemodynamically stable.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate management?",
        "26": "A 41-year-old man presents to a rural hospital with a two-week history of fever, night sweats, chest pain and joint pain. He has no known medical conditions and takes no regular medications. He has a 20 pack-year smoking history, drinks alcohol heavily. He has recently returned from a one week trip to Bali where he underwent a dental procedure. On examination, he appears unwell. His pulse rate is 110/min, blood pressure 110/70 mmHg, respiratory rate 20/min, SpO2 98% on room air, and temperature 38.3 C. The patient has multiple painless, erythematous macules on his palms. On auscultation, a new systolic murmur is noted at the left sternal border. Investigations reveal a haemoglobin of 78 g/L (135-175) and white cell count of 15 x 10*9/L (4.0-11.0). Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "29": "A 75-year-old woman presents to the General Practice clinic after a fall while walking to the toilet at home the previous night. She has no history of falls and denies any loss of consciousness or serious injuries from the fall. Her medical history includes a myocardial infarction two years ago, hypertension, and insomnia. Her medications include furosemide, metoprolol extended-release, hydrochlorothiazide, and perindopril. She has also recently been started ona short-term medication for insomnia. On examination, she appears well with vital signs within normal limits. She expresses concern about further falls, worrying about sustaining a more serious injury in the future. Which one of the following is the most appropriate initial target for deprescribing to reduce her risk of further falls?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Dermatology - Rashes / Other Dermatoses (MCQ)": {
        "17": "A four-year-old boy presents to the General Practice clinic with his mother, complaining of a rash that has been present for the past two weeks. It is painless and does not appear to bother him. He also has some excoriated areas over his elbow and knee creases. He has no significant medical history and takes no regular medications. His mother has a history of cold sores, and his older brother currently has cold-like symptoms.\n\nOn examination, he appears well with vital signs within normal limits. Over his thighs and axillae there are approximately 15 discrete, dome-shaped, pearly, skin-coloured papules measuring 2–4 mm in diameter, several of which have a central umbilication. There is no surrounding erythema, no vesicles and no crusting.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate management?",
        "11": "A 27-year-old man presents with a two-week history of multiple painless nodules on his fingers. There is no itching, pruritus or discharge and he has never had anything like this before. His brother, who lives with him, has also noticed similar nodules.\n\nOn examination there are two firm, rough, hyperkeratotic papules on the dorsum of the fingers, each 4-5 mm across, with a verrucous (cauliflower-like) surface and small black dots visible within them. They are not tender, not fluctuant, and there is no surrounding erythema, no vesicles and no scaling elsewhere.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate treatment?",
        "19": "A 10-year-old boy presents with a three-day history of fever, cough and malaise. He has now developed increasingly painful lesions on his legs and a few spots around his mouth. He is not taking any medications except paracetamol for fever and has no history of drug or other allergies.\n\nOn examination he is febrile. On the extensor surfaces of both legs there are numerous well-demarcated round lesions, each with a dusky centre surrounded by a pale ring and an outer ring of erythema. There are several shallow erosions on the lips and buccal mucosa. Less than 5% of the body surface area is involved and there is no skin sloughing, no Nikolsky sign and no widespread desquamation. Chest auscultation reveals scattered crepitations, and a chest X-ray shows patchy bilateral interstitial infiltrates.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "26": "A 70-year-old man presents with a four-day history of an itchy rash on his face. Many years ago he had some similar episodes that completely resolved spontaneously after 7-10 days. His medical history includes several squamous cell carcinomas removed from his face, hayfever and coeliac disease.\n\nOn examination there is a cluster of small tense vesicles on an erythematous base, grouped around the right naso-labial fold and extending onto the vermillion border of the upper lip. Some have crusted. The eruption does not cross the midline onto a dermatomal distribution, and there is no ulceration, no induration and no involvement of the eye.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "27": "A 33-year-old woman presents with a two-day history of fever, sore throat, runny nose, cough and headache. She has now noticed a rash on her face spreading to the trunk and limbs, accompanied by mild joint pain.\n\nOn examination there is a diffuse, fine, erythematous maculopapular rash over the face, trunk and limbs, which blanches completely on pressure. There are no vesicles, no pustules, no sandpaper texture and no perioral pallor. There is tender lymphadenopathy in the posterior auricular and suboccipital chains. She is not immunised.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "34": "A 40-year-old building worker presents to the General Practice clinic with a painful toe. This has been giving him trouble for the last ten days, and he has not been able to wear his work boots. His general practitioner has prescribed antibiotics, and he has been using topical antiseptics, without improvement. He reports the same problem has recurred several times over the past two years.\n\nOn examination the lateral nail fold of the great toe is swollen, erythematous and tender, with a bead of pus at the nail edge and exuberant granulation tissue overgrowing the fold. The lateral edge of the nail plate is curved and embedded into the soft tissue of the fold. The toe is otherwise well perfused with normal sensation.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate management?",
        "13": "A 75-year-old woman presents to her local hospital with a one-week history of worsening productive cough, dyspnoea, and fevers. She has a medical history of COPD, hypertension, and three prior myocardial infarctions with four stents in situ. Her regular medications include amlodipine. She has a 35 pack-year smoking history and is an active smoker.\n\nOn examination she appears unwell with a pulse rate of 100/min, blood pressure 140/90 mmHg, respiratory rate 24/min, SpO2 90% on room air, and temperature 37.8°C. She is started on intravenous antibiotics, prednisolone, and nebulisers. She improves symptomatically and is discharged for rehabilitation after one week, and is now back to baseline functioning according to the physiotherapist.\n\nHowever, she has remained hypoxic, with oxygen saturation 90% on room air, dropping to 87% on mobilising. She saturates at 98% on 2 L/min oxygen via nasal cannulae. On examination there are scattered wheezes and scant bibasal crepitations. Dual heart sounds are heard with no added murmurs. The apex beat is displaced to the anterior axillary line. JVP is not raised. There is no hepatosplenomegaly, peripheral oedema, ascites or caput medusae. Calves are soft and non-tender.\n\nHer ECG shows sinus rhythm with deep pathological Q waves in leads V1 to V4 and poor R-wave progression across the praecordial leads, together with T-wave inversion in the lateral leads. There is no acute ST-segment elevation, no right-axis deviation, no right bundle branch block and no S1Q3T3 pattern.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely cause of her hypoxia?",
        "5": "A 32-year-old man presents to the General Practice clinic with a three-day history of itchy skin lesions that developed during a recent business trip to Paris. He stayed at a hotel for five nights and noticed the rash on the morning of his fourth day. He has no significant medical history and takes no regular medications. He has not made any changes to his personal care products or diet during the trip. He practised yoga on the carpet each morning and night.\n\nOn examination he appears well with vital signs within normal limits. There are multiple erythematous papules on his arms, back, neck and lower legs, several arranged in short linear clusters of three. Some lesions show evidence of excoriation. No vesicles, bullae or pustules are present. The remainder of the examination is unremarkable.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely finding if the patient's hotel room were to be inspected?",
        "31": "A 35-year-old man presents with painless lesions on his palms that have been present for about a week. He does not have involvement of his lips or mouth. He reports a two-week history of feeling generally unwell with a mild fever. He is sexually active with male partners.\n\nOn examination there is a symmetrical, non-itchy, coppery-red maculopapular eruption over both palms and extending onto the soles, with fine collarette scaling. There are no vesicles, no target lesions with a dusky centre, and no oral or lip erosions. There is painless generalised lymphadenopathy.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "40": "A 16-year-old girl presents with a four-week history of an itchy red patch on her arm. She appears embarrassed about the lesion and states that another student in the boarding house has something similar.\n\nOn examination there is a well-demarcated, annular erythematous plaque approximately 4 cm across, with a raised, scaly, advancing edge and central clearing. There are no vesicles, no honey-coloured crust and no burrows.\n\nWhich one of the following investigations will best confirm the diagnosis?",
        "44": "A 67-year-old man presents to the General Practice clinic with a two-week history of a moderately pruritic rash on his trunk. The rash developed one week after he was discharged from hospital following a cardioplasty for severe achalasia. He has a medical history of hypertension and hypercholesterolaemia. His medications include aspirin, atorvastatin, and nifedipine. He denies any new medications or changes to his home environment. On examination, he appears well with vital signs within normal limits. The rash consists of multiple erythematous, edematous papules in clusters on his trunk only. Some lesions show signs of excoriation. There are no vesicles, bullae, or scaling present. Which one of the following is the most appropriate initial management for this patient?",
        "46": "A 52-year-old woman presents to the General Practice clinic with a four-month history of worsening bloating, slight abdominal distension and increased urinary frequency. She has been using a hot water bottle against her abdomen most evenings for months to ease the discomfort.\n\nOn examination the skin of the lower abdomen shows a reticular, net-like pattern of brown-red pigmentation with fine telangiectasia, in the distribution where she applies the heat. It does not blanch. There is no vesicular or dermatomal eruption, no palpable purpura and no bruising of the flanks. Abdominal examination reveals a firm mass arising from the pelvis.\n\nThe skin change is a clue to which one of the following underlying diagnoses?",
        "48": "A 35-year-old woman presents to the General Practice clinic with a three-month history of episodic flushing, abdominal pain, and diarrhoea. She also reports multiple reddish-brown spots on her trunk and limbs that become raised and itchy in seconds when rubbed. She has no significant medical history and takes no regular medications. On examination, she appears well with vital signs within normal limits, Multiple reddish-brown macules are present on her trunk and limbs. Gentle stroking of these lesions produces localised whealing and erythema. A recent bone marrow biopsy has confirmed systemic mastocytosis. Which one of the following is the most appropriate first-line treatment?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - ENT - Hearing Loss (MCQ)": {
        "1": "An eight-year-old boy presents to the General Practice clinic with a several-week history of right ear discharge. He reports having ear pain prior to the onset of discharge. He has no significant medical history and takes no regular medications. On examination, he appears well. His pulse rate is 84/min and temperature 36.6°C. There are scars of a healing scabies infestation over the forearms.\n\nOtoscopic examination of the right ear reveals purulent discharge in the ear canal and, once this is cleared, a central perforation of the tympanic membrane with a moist, inflamed middle-ear mucosa visible through the defect. Rinne's test shows bone conduction greater than air conduction on the right, and Weber's test lateralises to the right.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate treatment?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Gastroenterology - Cholecystitis / Biliary Colic (MCQ)": {
        "9": "An intra-operative choledochogram (cholangiogram) is performed during a cholecystectomy to examine the anatomy of the biliary tree and identify any pathology, such as a stone in the common bile duct. The cystic duct is cannulated and contrast injected to outline the biliary system.\n\nIn this patient, the contrast study shows the cystic duct draining directly into the right posterior hepatic duct, rather than joining the common hepatic duct. Contrast flows freely into the duodenum, the intrahepatic ducts opacify normally, and there are no filling defects.\n\nWhat is the anatomical defect?",
        "0": "A 32-year-old woman presents to the Emergency Department with a four-day history of vague abdominal discomfort and nausea. Her medical history is not immediately available. A plain abdominal X-ray is ordered as part of the assessment.\n\nThe supine film shows a radio-opaque ring-shaped device encircling the upper part of the stomach, just below the gastro-oesophageal junction, connected by a fine radio-opaque tube running up towards the left upper abdominal wall, where it terminates in a small subcutaneous port. There is no bowel dilatation, no free gas and no discrete radio-opaque calculi in the right upper quadrant.\n\nWhich one of the following best explains the appearance on this X-ray?",
        "10": "A 64-year-old man presents to the General Practice clinic with a two-day history of left lower quadrant pain that has since resolved. He has no significant medical history and does not take any regular medications. On examination he appears well with normal vital signs and a soft abdomen.\n\nA CT scan performed during the acute episode shows uncomplicated left-sided colonic diverticulosis. Incidentally, the gallbladder wall shows extensive, patchy mural calcification. There are no gallstones, no gallbladder wall thickening or pericholecystic fluid, and the biliary tree is not dilated. He has no biliary symptoms.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate management of the incidental finding?",
        "14": "A 52-year-old teacher presents with a two-week history of constant dull right subcostal pain. She is normally in good health and has recently returned to Australia from a two-year secondment in Istanbul, where she kept dogs. She does not use any medications.\n\nAn abdominal ultrasound shows a large, well-marginated, rounded cystic lesion in the right lobe of the liver, with a thick echogenic wall and multiple smaller daughter cysts visible within the parent cyst, separated by internal septations. The lesion is sharply demarcated from adjacent parenchyma, and contains no internal gas and no debris-fluid level.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "13": "A 65-year-old man presents to his General Practitioner after an episode of rapid-onset, sharp right upper quadrant pain this morning that lasted about 20 minutes and then subsided. He is now entirely asymptomatic and systemically well, with a soft, non-tender abdomen and normal observations.\n\nA CT scan of the abdomen incidentally shows a fusiform dilatation of the infrarenal abdominal aorta measuring 4.3 cm in maximum diameter, with no periaortic stranding, no contrast extravasation and no retroperitoneal haematoma. The aneurysm is asymptomatic and unrelated to his transient pain.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate management of the aortic finding?",
        "21": "A 44-year-old woman presents with a six-hour history of intermittent colicky abdominal pain, nausea and a single episode of vomiting. The pain is worst in her upper abdomen. She has no significant medical history.\n\nA plain abdominal X-ray shows a single, rounded, laminated area of calcification in the right upper quadrant, lying lateral to the vertebral column and above the level of the iliac crest. It has a faceted margin. It does not lie along the line of the ureter, is not within the appendix, and has no central lucency.\n\nThe area of calcification is most likely to be which one of the following?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - General - Miscellaneous (MCQ)": {
        "63": "A five-month-old girl presents to the General Practice clinic with her parent, who is concerned about a mark on the child's abdomen. The lesion has been present since birth, fully formed, and has not changed in size or appearance since. She has no significant medical history and takes no regular medications. Her immunisations are up to date.\n\nOn examination, she appears well with vital signs within normal limits. On the abdomen there is a solitary, well-circumscribed, raised red-purple vascular plaque approximately 3 cm in diameter, with a warm surface and fine overlying telangiectasia. It is non-tender and non-ulcerated. There are no other similar lesions.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate management?",
        "67": "An airway assessment is being undertaken. The patient is asked to sit upright with the mouth fully open and to protrude the tongue.\n\nOn inspection, the soft palate and the base of the uvula are visible. The tonsillar fauces and pillars cannot be seen, and the body of the uvula is obscured by the base of the tongue.\n\nWhat Mallampati grading is this patient?",
        "69": "A seven-year-old girl presents to the Emergency Department with a 30-minute history of sudden onset left-sided chest pain that occurred while she was watching television. She denies any palpitations, shortness of breath, or syncope. Her medical history is unremarkable, but there is a family history of sudden cardiac death. She takes no regular medications and has no known allergies. On examination, she appears well with vital signs within normal limits. Cardiovascular examination reveals no abnormalities.\n\nA 12-lead ECG shows a normal P wave before every QRS complex with a constant, normal PR interval and narrow QRS complexes. The R–R interval varies cyclically, shortening during inspiration and lengthening during expiration. There are no ectopic beats and no dropped QRS complexes.\n\nWhich one of the following best describes the ECG findings?",
        "1": "A 62-year-old man presents to the General Practice clinic with sudden onset right arm pain after weightlifting at the gym. While performing an upper body exercise, he felt a sharp pain and an audible 'pop' in his right arm.\n\nOn examination there is bruising over the anterior aspect of the upper arm, and the belly of the biceps muscle is bunched up as a prominent, rounded mass in the lower arm, sitting distal to its usual position. The contour of the upper arm is flattened above this. Elbow flexion and supination are weak but preserved.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate next step to confirm the diagnosis?",
        "2": "A 35-year-old man presents to the General Practice clinic with a six-month history of pain, numbness, and tingling in his right arm and hand. The symptoms worsen when he raises his arm above shoulder level. He developed headaches four months ago.\n\nOn examination there is mild wasting of the hypothenar eminence. Sensation is reduced along the medial aspect of the forearm and hand. Radial pulses are present and symmetrical, with no colour change of the hand on elevation. Reproduction of his symptoms occurs when the arms are abducted to 90 degrees and externally rotated for 60 seconds. Neck movements are full and painless, and there is no dermatomal cervical radicular pattern.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate initial management?",
        "5": "A 16-year-old boy presents to the General Practice clinic with a two-month history of persistent left knee pain. He plays basketball competitively for his high school team and the pain now impairs his ability to play. The pain worsens with running and jumping and settles with rest. He is in the middle of a growth spurt.\n\nOn examination there is a tender, firm, bony prominence over the tibial tuberosity, with overlying soft-tissue swelling. Pain is reproduced by resisted knee extension. The patellar tendon itself is not tender along its length, the joint line is not tender, there is no effusion, and there is no prepatellar swelling. There is no history of acute injury.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "8": "A 40-year-old man is retrieved from the scene of a motor car accident, in which he was the driver involved in a head-on collision. He is fully conscious, alert and orientated and complaining of abdominal pain. He has an unremarkable medical history.\n\nHis initial blood pressure was 120/80 mmHg but has fallen to 85/55 mmHg with a pulse of 130/min despite two litres of crystalloid. A CT scan shows a large laceration involving the posterior aspect of the liver with a surrounding haematoma and a large volume of free intraperitoneal fluid. He remains persistently hypotensive.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate management?",
        "11": "An 85-year-old man presents for a routine health check.\n\nWhen he looks straight ahead, the left eye is deviated outwards relative to the right. The doctor asks the patient to look sharply to the right; the left eye fails to move medially past the midline, while the right eye abducts fully. The patient reports horizontal double vision on looking to the right. Abduction of the left eye is full, there is no ptosis, and the pupils are equal and reactive.\n\nWeakness of which one of the following muscles best explains this finding?",
        "54": "A 68-year-old woman presents to her General Practitioner with a two-week history of a mildly itchy rash under her breasts. She is otherwise in good health, has no significant medical history and takes no regular medications.\n\nOn examination there are well-demarcated, flat, red-brown patches with fine wrinkled scale in both inframammary folds. There are no satellite pustules or papules beyond the border of the patch, no central clearing, no raised active edge and no vesicles. Under Wood's lamp examination the affected skin fluoresces coral-pink.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate treatment?",
        "57": "A 57-year-old woman presents with a painless swelling on the left side of her neck. This has been present for several years and is slowly increasing in size. It did not bother her until it recently started to discharge foul-smelling material intermittently.\n\nOn examination there is a 3 cm smooth, firm, mobile subcutaneous swelling in the left side of the neck. The overlying skin is intact and tethered to the lesion, and a small central punctum is visible on its surface. It is not pulsatile, does not transilluminate, and there is no associated lymphadenopathy or overlying scarring.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "59": "A 63-year-old man is about to undergo a colonoscopy after returning a positive screening faecal occult blood test. The patient has no gastrointestinal symptoms.\n\nOn inspection of the anus as the procedure begins, two vascular cushions are seen partially prolapsing through the anal verge on straining. They reduce fully when pushed back manually, but do not reduce spontaneously. They are not thrombosed, not tender, and the overlying mucosa is intact.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate management?",
        "82": "A 60-year-old man presents to the General Practice clinic with pain in his right arm. He felt a 'pop' in his right arm whilst moving heavy furniture yesterday. He states it was immediately painful and he was unable to continue lifting heavy objects. He is right-hand dominant and works in an office.\n\nOn examination there is ecchymosis over the anterior upper arm and the biceps muscle belly is bunched distally into a rounded mass, with flattening of the upper arm contour above it. Elbow flexion power is 4+/5 and supination is mildly weak but functional. The distal biceps tendon is intact and palpable in the antecubital fossa.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate management?",
        "86": "A 78-year-old woman is being assessed for transfer from her home to residential care. She has lived by herself for the past five years and her son reports that she is now finding it difficult to look after herself. She has not been able to attend to her own foot care for some time.\n\nAs part of the assessment, a physical examination is performed. The nail of the right great toe is grossly thickened, opaque and yellow-brown, and is markedly elongated, curving over on itself into a thick, spiralled, horn-like deformity resembling a ram's horn. There is no surrounding erythema, no discharge, no embedding of the nail edge into the nail fold, and no splitting or layering of the nail plate.\n\nWhich one of the following best describes this nail abnormality?",
        "88": "A 20-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department with a four-day history of left axillary swelling that has been slowly increasing in size. He was prescribed oral cefalexin by his General Practitioner. He has a history of recurrent abscesses in the axillae and groin over several years.\n\nOn examination there are multiple inflamed, tender nodules and fluctuant abscesses in the left axilla, together with several discharging sinus tracts and thickened, rope-like scarring from previous episodes. There are open comedones with a double-headed appearance in the surrounding skin. Similar scarring is present in the right groin.\n\nWhich one of the following best describes the underlying pathological process?",
        "56": "A 61-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department with a three-day history of sudden onset of brief and excruciating facial pain attacks on the left cheek and forehead. He has never had any previous facial pain problems. He has also expressed concerns about chewing when he eats. On examination, his blood pressure is 130/90 mmHg, pulse 90/min, temperature 37.3 C, respiratory rate 18/min and SpO2 98% on room air. There is @ loss of corneal reflex for this patient. Which of the following abnormalities is most likely to also be occurring in this patient?",
        "70": "A 45-year-old Aboriginal man presents to the General Practice clinic for a routine health check. He has no significant medical history and takes no regular medications. During the consultation, he mentions feeling disconnected from his community since moving to the city for work two years ago. He expresses a desire to reconnect with his culture and traditional practices. On examination, he appears well with vital signs within normal limits. Which one of the following best describes a cultural determinant of health that may positively impact this patient's wellbeing?",
        "72": "An unidentifiable man with substantial lacerations to his arms and legs is brought into a trauma centre by the emergency retrieval service after being called to the site of an altercation. No alcohol or illicit substances are found nearby. The paramedics decided to scoop and run as the emergency department is only a couple of minutes away. On arrival assessment, the patient is in a pool of blood and appears drowsy, unable to complete full sentences. The peripheries are cold and clammy whilst central capillary refill time is 4.5 seconds, The radial pulse is thready and rapid. His pupils are equal and reactive bilaterally. On removal of his clothing, there is a long laceration in his left popliteal fossa which is haemorrhaging a large volume of bright red blood. Direct bleed site pressure is unable to contain the bleeding. Vital signs: respiration rate 28/min, temperature 36.5 C, heart rate 124/min, blood pressure 82/51 mmbg, oxygen saturation 95%. Which one of the following is the most appropriate next intervention?",
        "74": "A 62-year-old man is admitted to hospital with community-acquired pneumonia. Despite some improvement with oxygen and empirical intravenous antibiotics, he remains very weak and is only able to mobilise between his hospital bed and the toilet. He has a medical history of hypertension and asthma. His medications include amlodipine and salbutamol as needed, On examination, he appears breathless. His blood pressure is 130/92 mmHg, pulse 96/min, respiratory rate 24/min, temperature 39.5 C, and SpO2 89% on room air. Initial blood investigations show normal liver and renal function. With his limited mobility, some thought must be given to the risk of venous thrombosis and the need for some form of thromboprophylaxis. Which one of the following would be the most appropriate?",
        "75": "53-year-old man is admitted to the rehabilitation ward following a left below-knee amputation two weeks ago, The amputation was performed due to critical limb ischaemia and non-healing diabetic foot ulcers. He describes intermittent shooting pain and burning sensations in his amputated foot. He has a medical history of poorly controlled type 2 diabetes mellitus with an HbA\\1c of 98 mmol/mol (11.1%), hypertension, hypercholesterolaemia, and peripheral vascular disease. He is an active smoker with a 30 pack-year history, His medications include metformin, gliclazide, insulin glargine, perindopril, and atorvastatin. On examination, he appears well. His vital signs are within normal limits. His surgical wound is healing well without signs of infection. He is currently using a wheelchair for mobility and requires moderate assistance with transfers. He reports feeling anxious about his future independence. Which one of the following interventions has shown the most benefit in improving functional outcomes?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Haematology - Anaemia (MCQ)": {
        "17": "A five-year-old boy presents to the Emergency Department with a four-hour history of a spontaneous painful penile erection. He has been unwell for the past five days with shortness of breath, chest pain and fever. He and his family recently migrated from Nigeria. He has no significant medical history and does not take any regular medications.\n\nOn examination, he appears pale with a pulse rate of 120/min, blood pressure 100/60 mmHg, respiratory rate 24/min, SpO2 96% on room air, and temperature 38.2°C. The penile shaft is rigid and tender. Other examination findings are unremarkable.\n\nInvestigations reveal:\n- Haemoglobin: 80 g/L (110-135)\n- White cell count: 11 x10^9/L (4-10)\n- Platelets: 350 x10^9/L (150-400)\n\nHis peripheral blood film shows numerous elongated, crescent-shaped red cells with pointed ends, together with target cells and occasional nucleated red cells. Howell-Jolly bodies are present.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely underlying cause of his current presentation?",
        "20": "A previously healthy 30-year-old man presents with a four-day history of easy fatigue and bone pains. He gives no medical history except for a recent urinary tract infection one week ago for which he was prescribed ciprofloxacin.\n\nOn examination he is jaundiced and pale. Investigations show a normocytic-to-macrocytic anaemia with a raised reticulocyte count, elevated unconjugated bilirubin, markedly raised LDH and a low haptoglobin. The direct antiglobulin (Coombs) test is positive for IgG. The blood film shows spherocytes and polychromasia, with no bite cells, no Heinz bodies and no sickled forms.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "22": "A 66-year-old man presents to the General Practice clinic with increasing fatigue and pallor over the past three months. He has also noted mild abdominal pain and constipation, against a family history of bowel cancer.\n\nInvestigations show anaemia with a raised reticulocyte count, elevated unconjugated bilirubin, raised LDH and low haptoglobin. The direct antiglobulin (Coombs) test is positive. The blood film shows numerous spherocytes with polychromasia, and no schistocytes or fragmented red cells.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely cause of his anaemia?",
        "26": "A 68-year-old man presents to the General Practice clinic with a three-month history of tongue pain and burning, particularly when eating spicy foods. He follows a strict vegan diet. He has no significant medical history. He also describes tingling in both feet.\n\nOn examination the tongue is smooth, glossy, and beefy red, with complete loss of the filiform papillae. There are no white plaques that can be scraped off, no white lacy striae, and no map-like migrating patches with serpiginous white borders. There is reduced vibration sense in both feet.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "19": "A 60-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department with a one-hour history of central crushing chest pain, shortness of breath and sweating. He has a medical history of hypertension and hypercholesterolaemia, and is a current smoker.\n\nHis ECG shows ST-segment elevation of 3 mm in leads V2 to V5 with reciprocal changes inferiorly. Troponin is markedly elevated. Coronary angiography demonstrates an abrupt, complete occlusion of the proximal left anterior descending artery with a hazy, irregular filling defect at the site.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely underlying cause of his presentation?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Haematology - Leukaemia (MCQ)": {
        "10": "A four-year-old girl presents to the General Practice clinic with her parent for routine childhood vaccinations. She appears well with vital signs within normal limits. Her parent reports that a 'birthmark' on her abdomen appeared at around two weeks of age, enlarged over the following few months, and has been slowly shrinking and paling since.\n\nOn examination there is a solitary raised, bright red, well-circumscribed, lobulated lesion approximately 2 cm in diameter on the abdomen. It blanches incompletely with pressure, is non-tender, and is not ulcerated. There are no other similar lesions elsewhere on her body. She has no significant medical history and takes no regular medications.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "1": "A 76-year-old woman presents to the General Practice clinic with a one-month history of a rapidly expanding growth over her left upper gum margin. She has also noted fatigue, night sweats, and weight loss during this one-month period.\n\nOn examination there is a firm, non-tender, violaceous, fleshy mass arising from the left upper alveolar margin, which is expanding rather than being a diffuse overgrowth of otherwise normal gingival tissue. Investigations show pancytopenia with an elevated lactate dehydrogenase. Bone marrow biopsy shows a hypercellular marrow with more than 20% myeloblasts.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "7": "A 56-year-old woman presents with a four-month history of fatigue and dyspnoea. She reports weight loss of three kilograms over this timeframe. She also reports a persistent dull pain in several areas including the central chest and lower back. She has a 40 pack-year smoking history.\n\nInvestigations show a prominent normocytic anaemia. A blood film shows a leucoerythroblastic picture with nucleated red cells and immature granulocytes. Bone marrow biopsy shows infiltration by nests of large cells with abundant eosinophilic cytoplasm, intercellular bridges and keratin pearls. Serum protein electrophoresis shows no paraprotein and serum calcium is normal.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "11": "A 52-year-old man presents to the General Practice clinic with a three-month history of increasing fatigue, night sweats, and unexplained bruising. He has no significant medical history and takes no regular medications. He has worked as a petroleum refinery operator for the past 25 years. On examination, he appears pale. His pulse rate is 92/min, blood pressure 118/74 mmHg, respiratory rate 16/min, SpO2 98% on room air, and temperature 37.2 C. He has scattered bruises on his arms and legs. Blood tests reveal: * haemoglobin 85 g/L (130-170) * white cell count 32 x 10*9/L (4.0-11.0) platelets 45 x 10 9/L (150-400) * blood film shows numerous myeloblasts with Auer rods (>20%) Which one of the following occupational exposures is most likely to have contributed to this presentation?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Respiratory - Pneumothorax (MCQ)": {
        "9": "A 34-year-old man is intubated and mechanically ventilated in the Intensive Care Unit following a high-speed motor vehicle collision. He sustained multiple left-sided rib fractures. Over the past two hours the nursing staff have noticed progressive facial swelling.\n\nOn examination there is gross swelling of both eyelids, to the point where he cannot open his eyes, together with swelling of the face and neck. The swollen tissues feel like they are crackling under the fingers on palpation. His peak airway pressures have risen and air entry is reduced on the left.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely explanation for this physical sign?",
        "6": "A 25-year-old woman is brought to a rural hospital after sustaining a stab wound to her left chest during a physical altercation. The nearest tertiary hospital is 200 km away. The emergency consultant is currently managing a critical patient and will not be available for 20 minutes. On examination she is anxious, with a blood pressure of 106/70 mmHg, pulse 110/min, respiratory rate 28/min and SpOz 94% on room air. On inspection of her chest a wound measuring approximately 5 cm in diameter is visible below her left scapula, with air movement noted through the wound as she breathes. On auscultation there are decreased breath sounds on the left side of her chest. Which one of the following is the most appropriate first step in management?",
        "8": "A 40-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department with a five-hour history of sudden-onset shortness of breath and chest discomfort. He has no cough, nausea, dizziness or lightheadedness. He has cystic fibrosis.\n\nOn examination his respiratory rate is 26/min and SpO2 92% on room air. There is hyper-resonance and reduced breath sounds over the left hemithorax. A chest X-ray shows a left-sided pneumothorax with a visible visceral pleural edge and a rim of 3.5 cm at the level of the hilum. The trachea is central and he is haemodynamically stable.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate management?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Endocrinology - Thyroid Nodule / Cancer (MCQ)": {
        "5": "A 57-year-old woman presents with a two-month history of a painless midline swelling in her neck. The swelling moves upwards when she protrudes her tongue. An ultrasound shows a 3 cm cystic swelling above, and separate from, a normal thyroid gland. The patient is not particularly keen on any treatment.\n\nIf this lesion is left untreated, which one of the following is the most likely outcome?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Cardiology - Heart Failure (MCQ)": {
        "1": "A 72-year-old woman is referred by her General Practitioner to the Emergency Department due to concerns about systemic symptoms from a suspected urinary tract infection. She has a history of asthma and some intermittent indigestion but is otherwise well.\n\nAs part of her septic workup, a chest X-ray is performed. This shows a rounded retrocardiac opacity containing a gas–fluid level, projected behind the cardiac shadow. The cardiac silhouette itself is of normal size and contour, the mediastinum is not widened, the lung fields are clear, and there is no free mediastinal gas and no pleural fluid.\n\nWhich one of the following findings is present on this chest X-ray?",
        "3": "A 57-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department with a one-week history of severe shortness of breath, unable to complete full sentences. This is on the background of increasing breathlessness on exertion over the past three months. He has also noticed some muscle wasting and diminished appetite during this time. He has no significant medical history and takes no regular medications. He is a long-term smoker.\n\nOn examination he is cachectic. There is stony dullness to percussion and absent breath sounds over the left lower and mid zones. A chest radiograph demonstrates a large left-sided pleural effusion with a meniscus sign, opacifying the lower two thirds of the left hemithorax. There is no air bronchogram and no upper-lobe diversion or septal lines.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely underlying diagnosis?",
        "45": "A 72-year-old woman presents with a four-week history of increasingly 'puffy' legs. She has no other associated symptoms and was feeling well until this began, apart from some 4 kg of weight loss over the last two months. Her medical history includes hypertension, ischaemic heart disease and congestive cardiac failure. She is a non-smoker and does not drink alcohol.\n\nOn examination there is bilateral pitting oedema extending to both thighs. Over the anterior abdominal wall there are markedly distended, tortuous superficial veins running in the epigastric distribution, with flow directed upwards towards the chest. The jugular venous pressure is not elevated, the chest is clear, and there is no hepatomegaly, ascites or splenomegaly.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "19": "An 85-year-old Indigenous woman presents to the Emergency Department of a rural hospital with three days of progressive shortness of breath on a background of two months of fatigue. She has a medical history of diabetes mellitus, hypertension, and childhood acute rheumatic fever. She takes metformin and perindopril regularly. She does not smoke or drink alcohol. On examination, she appears unwell. Her pulse rate is 92/min, blood pressure 138/78 mmHg, respiratory rate 22/min, SpO2 94% on room air, and temperature 36.7 C. There is an apical pan- systolic murmur on auscultation, which is unchanged from previous examination in hospital. There are crepitations at both lung bases and her hands are cool to touch. No peripheral oedema is noted. Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "20": "Management plans are being discussed for an 82-year-old man with end-stage congestive cardiac failure. On the most recent medical review the patient is dyspnoeic despite maximal medical therapy, including furosemide 120mg BD, which is the maximal dose tolerated. Trials of thiazide diuretics and |V furosemide have been unsuccessful, complicated by his declining renal function. He has a history of chronic renal impairment: most recent creatinine clearance 25 mL/min. After discussion with the patient and his family the plan is to proceed with focus on comfort measures and management. His current are: furosemide 120mg BD, perindopril 5mg mane, bisoprolol 10mg mane, spironolactone 25mg mane, atorvastatin 20mg daily and aspirin 150mg daily. On examination he is tachypnoeic at rest with SpO2 97% on room air. Bi-basal crackles are noted and heart sounds are dual with no murmurs. He also has pitting oedema to his knees. Which one of the following is the most appropriate management of his dyspnoea?",
        "36": "A 45-year-old man presents to the General Practice clinic with a three-month history of progressive shortness of breath on exertion and fatigue. He has no significant medical history and does not take any regular medications. He reports drinking 9-10 beers daily for the past eight years since his divorce. On examination, he appears tired. His pulse rate is 90/min, blood pressure 130/85 mmHg, respiratory rate 18/min, $pO2 97% on room air, and temperature 36.7 C. Cardiovascular examination reveals a displaced apex beat and a soft systolic murmur at the apex. Chest X-ray shows cardiomegaly but no lung disease . Echocardiogram reveals a dilated left ventricle (left ventricular end-diastolic volume 2.2 SD above the normal level) with reduced ejection fraction with EF ~ 35%. ECG showed normal QRS amplitude complexes with normal conduction intervals. Which one of the following is the most likely cause of this patient's cardiomyopathy?",
        "37": "53-year-old Aboriginal woman presents to the General Practice clinic for review of her recent routine blood tests. She has a medical history of congestive cardiac failure secondary to theumatic heart disease, mitral valve replacement, type 2 diabetes mellitus and depression. Her regular medications include perindopril, bisoprolol, spironolactone, warfarin, metformin and semaglutide. She smokes five cigarettes per day and does not drink alcohol. She reports mild shortness of breath when walking up hills or stairs but denies chest pain. On examination, she appears well. Her pulse rate is 72/min, blood pressure 125/80 mmHg, respiratory rate 16/min, SpO2 98% on room air, and temperature 36.7 C. She has pitting oedema to the mid-shins which she reports has been stable for the past few months. Her recent blood tests reveal * Haemoglobin 130 g/L (115-160) + Mean cell volume 80 fL (80-100) + Mean cell haemoglobin 25.5 pg (27.5-33) * Iron 12 umol/L (9-30) * Transferrin 2.5 g/L (2.0-3.6) * Transferrin saturation 22% (15-45) * Ferritin 65 pg/L (10-85) Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?",
        "41": "An otherwise well 54-year-old man presents with a six-month history of a painless swelling in the right side of his scrotum. He has no history of trauma or other symptoms.\n\nOn examination there is a smooth, soft, non-tender swelling in the upper pole of the right scrotum, lying above and separate from the testis, which is itself normal in size and consistency. The swelling transilluminates brightly. It is possible to get above it. There is no cough impulse, no bag-of-worms feel on standing, and no hard irregular testicular mass.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "43": "A 68-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department after waking from sleep acutely short of breath and subsequently calling an ambulance. He is not normally one to regularly visit a doctor.\n\nOn examination he is sitting bolt upright, with a respiratory rate of 30/min and SpO2 89% on room air. There are widespread fine inspiratory crackles to the mid-zones, a raised JVP and a third heart sound. His chest X-ray shows cardiomegaly with upper-lobe venous diversion, interstitial septal (Kerley B) lines and bilateral perihilar alveolar shadowing. He is in sinus rhythm.\n\nWhich one of the following medications is most likely to improve his long-term survival?",
        "44": "55-year-old man presents with a three month history of pain in his right calf when he walks more than 100 metres. The pain ceases when he rests. He has no other symptoms and his medical history is unremarkable. He is overweight and has a 30 pack-year smoking history. On examination his blood pressure is 150/100 mm Hg and his pulse rate is 90/min. Both lower limbs and feet are warm and well perfused. The right femoral pulse is diminished in volume and the right pedal pulse cannot be felt. The ankle-brachial index is 0.9 on the left and 0.7 on the right. There are no other abnormal physical findings Apart from advice on smoking cessation and checking his serum lipids, which one of the following is the most appropriate initial plan of management?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Cardiology - Hypercholesterolaemia (MCQ)": {
        "5": "An 82-year-old man presents with a six-month history of dysphagia and regurgitation. He has a medical history of poorly controlled heartburn and hyperlipidaemia. His medications include a proton pump inhibitor and simvastatin, which he typically forgets to take. He is a non-smoker, walks 2 km every day and can walk up two flights of stairs without any problems. Physical examination is unremarkable.\n\nA contrast swallow shows a short, smooth, tapered narrowing of the distal oesophagus, sitting immediately above a hiatus hernia, with dilatation of the oesophagus proximal to it. The mucosal outline through the narrowed segment is smooth and regular, with no shouldering, no irregular filling defect and no bird-beak appearance. The oesophageal body shows normal peristalsis.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate management?",
        "35": "A 50-year-old man presents to the General Practice clinic with a two-year history of a lump behind his left earlobe. The lump has been slowly growing and occasionally discharges malodorous, white material. He has no significant medical history and takes no regular medications.\n\nOn examination, he appears well with vital signs within normal limits. There is a 2 cm firm, mobile, non-tender subcutaneous nodule behind the left earlobe. It is dome-shaped, moves freely over the underlying tissue but is tethered to the overlying skin, and there is a small central punctum visible on its surface. There is no surrounding erythema, warmth or fluctuance, and no regional lymphadenopathy.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "12": "A 65-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department with a three-hour history of dull, pressure-like chest pain radiating to the left shoulder. He has a medical history of hypertension and hypercholesterolaemia. His medications include amlodipine and atorvastatin. On examination, he appears distressed with a pulse rate of 58/min, blood pressure 110/70 mmHg, respiratory rate 20/min, SpO2 96% on room air, and temperature 36.8°C.\n\nHis ECG on presentation shows sinus bradycardia at 58/min with ST-segment elevation in leads II, III and aVF, with reciprocal ST depression in leads I and aVL. He is diagnosed with a ST-elevation myocardial infarction, managed with percutaneous coronary intervention, and is now stable.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely complication to result within the first 24 hours?",
        "33": "A 55-year-old Caucasian man presents to his General Practitioner for his yearly check-up. He has a six-year history of hypertension, managed with an ACE inhibitor, and hyperlipidaemia, which he manages via dietary control. On examination, he appears generally well. His current BMI is 28 - compared to a BMI of 32 twelve months ago. His blood pressure at the GP today is 142/88mmHg. Prior to his appointment, the patient had bloods taken for lipid studies, which show the following: * Total cholesterol: 7.1 mmol/L (3.0 - 5.5) * Triglycerides: 0.6 mmol/L (<2.0) + HDL: 1.0 mmol/L (0,7 - 1.9) * LDL: 3.8 mmol/L (<3.5) * Total/HDL ratio: 3.0 The patient, in collaboration with his GP, decides to commence a medication to better manage his hyperlipidaemia. Which of the following medications would be most appropriate to prescribe?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Dermatology - Non-melanoma Skin Cancer (MCQ)": {
        "1": "A 46-year-old woman presents to the General Practice clinic with a one-year history of slowly enlarging lesions on both shins. She reports no pain or itching from the lesions but is concerned about their appearance. She is a surfer, and has had two basal cell carcinomas excised from her nose two years ago. She has type 2 diabetes.\n\nOn examination there are well-demarcated plaques over both anterior shins. They have a waxy, shiny, yellow-brown centre which is atrophic and slightly depressed, with prominent telangiectasia visible through the surface, and a raised violaceous border. They are not tender, not indurated, and there is no ulceration, no scale and no thickened or bound-down skin.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "2": "A 41-year-old woman presents with two lesions on her nose. They have been present for many years and have not changed.\n\nOn examination there are two smooth, dome-shaped, skin-coloured papules on the nose, each about 4 mm across, with a soft, uniform surface. There is no ulceration, no crusting, no bleeding, no surface telangiectasia, no pearly or rolled edge and no variation in pigmentation.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "3": "A 66-year-old man undergoes a right hemicolectomy for a carcinoma of the caecum. He makes a steady recovery and on the seventh day after the operation his wound is noticed to be reddened. His temperature spikes on one occasion to 38.5°C.\n\nOn examination there is a localised area of erythema and swelling over the middle third of the laparotomy wound, which is fluctuant and tender on palpation, with a small amount of turbid discharge expressible from between two skin staples. There is no spreading cellulitis, no crepitus and no skin necrosis.\n\nWhich one of the following organisms is most likely to be responsible?",
        "5": "A 64-year-old construction worker presents to the General Practice clinic with concern about a small lesion on his upper lip that has been present for four months. He has no significant medical history and takes no regular medications. It bled once when he cut it accidentally while shaving, but it is otherwise asymptomatic.\n\nOn examination there is a solitary, bright red, dome-shaped papule on the upper lip, approximately 4 mm in diameter, with a smooth surface. It blanches with pressure. There is no ulceration, no pearly rolled edge, no pigment variation, no induration and no surrounding erythema.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "6": "An 18-year-old woman wants lesions on her hand removed. She initially had only one, but reports multiple others arising over the past few years. She reports similar lesions around her toes. She is irritated by them and picks at them absent-mindedly, and is also self-conscious about their appearance.\n\nOn examination there are approximately eight firm, rough, hyperkeratotic papules on the dorsum of the hand and around the nail folds, ranging from 3-8 mm, with a verrucous surface and pinpoint black dots within them. They disrupt the normal skin lines. There is no surrounding erythema and no ulceration.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate treatment?",
        "7": "A 65-year-old man presents to the General Practice clinic with a skin lesion on his left nasolabial fold that has been present for several months and is slowly growing in size. He reports occasional bleeding from the lesion. His medical history includes hypertension and hypercholesterolaemia.\n\nOn examination there is a 9 mm raised nodule on the left nasolabial fold with a pearly, translucent surface, a rolled edge, and fine branching telangiectasia running across it. There is a small central area of ulceration and crusting.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate management?",
        "9": "A 69-year-old woman presents to her General Practitioner with a painless lesion on her lower lip, which first appeared one month ago. This has gradually grown, with no other change in appearance and no associated itch or pain. She has a medical history of osteoarthritis. She spent 30 years working outdoors on a farm and is a non-smoker.\n\nOn examination there is an indurated, keratotic, ulcerated plaque on the vermillion of the lower lip, approximately 12 mm across, with a firm, everted edge and an adherent crust. The surrounding lip shows diffuse pallor, loss of the sharp vermillion border, and scaly roughening.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely predisposing cause?",
        "10": "A 70-year-old man presents to his general practitioner with a hard lesion that has been growing on his ear for the past two years. He has worked as a farmer for many years and was not one to wear a hat.\n\nOn examination there is a firm, dry, keratinous projection arising from the helix of the ear, standing approximately 8 mm proud of the skin surface, conical in shape and yellow-brown in colour. It is strongly rooted to the skin and does not move independently of it. It is not pigmented, not ulcerated, and not bleeding, and there is no surrounding erythema.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "12": "An 80-year-old man presents to the General Practice clinic with a two-month history of a bleeding lesion on his right lower leg. He has no significant medical history and takes no regular medications. He lives independently and previously worked as a farmer for 40 years.\n\nOn examination there is a 2.5 cm firm, indurated, exophytic nodule on the right shin with a central ulcerated crater, a keratotic rim, and contact bleeding on gentle palpation. The base is fixed to underlying tissue.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most important next step in his assessment?",
        "16": "A 68-year-old man presents with a painful, ulcerated, exophytic nodular lesion on his left forearm. It has been progressively increasing in size for the last two years, is painful, and bleeds spontaneously. He lives in a remote area of the Northern Territory with few medical resources. He has a history of smoking and long-term outdoor sun exposure.\n\nOn examination the lesion is a 4 cm raised, firm, fungating nodule with a central ulcer, heaped keratotic edges and surrounding induration. There is no undermined violaceous border, no surrounding satellite lesions and no regional lymphadenopathy.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "19": "A 45-year-old man presents with a painless lesion on his lip that has been increasing in size for the last two months. He has a 20 pack-year smoking history. He is otherwise well with no significant medical history.\n\nOn examination there is a firm, indurated lesion on the vermillion of the lower lip with raised, rolled, everted edges and a central ulcerated base with adherent keratin. It is not tender, does not blanch, and there is no vesicle formation and no surrounding grouped lesions.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "23": "A 61-year-old man presents with a nodule on his back, which has been present for 12 months. He thinks it is slowly getting larger. He has no other health issues.\n\nOn examination there is a 1 cm red, translucent nodule on his back with a pearly, rolled edge and fine surface telangiectasia. There is a small central area of ulceration. Nothing else abnormal is found.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate management?",
        "17": "An 80-year-old woman presents with a lesion on her lower lip. The lesion has been slowly increasing in size and sometimes bleeds. She does not remember it being associated with any pain or itch. She has had some 'sores' removed before but cannot remember what the doctor called them. She worked as a farmhand for 30 years and has fair skin.\n\nOn examination there is an indurated, red, crusted lesion on the vermillion of the lower lip, approximately 15 mm across, with a firm, everted, keratotic edge and a central ulcerated base that bleeds on contact. There is no pearly, translucent rolled border and no surface telangiectasia. There is no pigmentation, no vesiculation and no waxy 'stuck-on' plaque.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Dermatology - Cellulitis / Skin Infection (MCQ)": {
        "12": "A previously well 55-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department with a one-day history of worsening scrotal pain. The pain is constant and came on gradually. He mentions that he has been feeling increasingly fatigued for the past few months and has had increasing thirst and urinary frequency.\n\nOn examination he is febrile at 38.9°C with a pulse of 120/min and blood pressure 95/60 mmHg. The scrotum and perineum are grossly swollen, with a dusky, blackened area of skin over the left hemiscrotum. There is crepitus on palpation of the scrotal skin, and a foul-smelling discharge. The pain is markedly out of proportion to the visible skin changes, and the overlying skin is anaesthetic to light touch.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate immediate management?",
        "15": "A 76-year-old man presents with a two-day history of a tender rash on his face. He denies any history of similar eruptions. His medical history includes type 2 diabetes, hypertension and benign prostatic hyperplasia. His current medications include tamsulosin, metformin, glipizide and amlodipine.\n\nOn examination there is a bright red, warm, tender area of erythema across both cheeks and the bridge of the nose in a butterfly distribution. The affected skin is raised above the surrounding skin, and the border is sharply demarcated with a clearly palpable edge. There is no vesiculation, no pustules, no necrosis and no crepitus. He is febrile at 38.2°C but haemodynamically stable.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate treatment?",
        "13": "A 70-year-old man presents with a wound over his right greater trochanter. He has had this wound for several years but it has not bothered him until recently. He says it has 'changed' in the last week or so, becoming significantly pruritic and tender. He lives alone in poor conditions and has limited mobility.\n\nOn examination there is a chronic pressure ulcer over the right greater trochanter with a sloughy base. On close inspection of the superomedial aspect of the wound, several small, pale, cylindrical larvae are visible moving within the tissue. The surrounding skin is mildly erythematous but there is no spreading cellulitis, no crepitus, no skin necrosis and no systemic upset. He is afebrile and haemodynamically stable.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "11": "An 81-year-old man presents with a six-day history of pain and redness on his left leg. He feels generally unwell and feverish. There has been no obvious trauma to the leg.\n\nOn examination there is a warm, tender, erythematous area over the left shin. The border of the erythema is indistinct and not raised above the surrounding skin, and the affected area is diffusely swollen. There is no crepitus, no skin necrosis, no bullae and no pain out of proportion to the findings. There is no chronic pigmentation, no lipodermatosclerosis and no ulceration. He is febrile at 38.3°C.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Dermatology - Melanoma (MCQ)": {
        "0": "A 35-year-old man undergoes removal of a right chest melanoma on a background of previous skin cancers. The surgeon also dissects the right axillary lymph nodes draining the chest to prevent further metastasis.\n\nOn post-operative examination, the surgeon observes impaired abduction of the right arm beyond 90 degrees. When the patient is asked to push against a wall with outstretched arms, the medial border of the right scapula lifts away from the chest wall. Sensation over the medial upper arm is intact, and shoulder extension and internal rotation are preserved.\n\nInjury to which one of the following nerves is most likely responsible?",
        "1": "A 25-year-old man presents to his general practice complaining of pain in the second toe of his right foot. The pain commenced 24 hours ago during football training and has gradually increased in intensity since then. He is unable to remember if he sustained trauma to the area. Paracetamol has helped only minimally, and the toe now throbs constantly.\n\nOn examination the nail of the second toe shows a dark purple-black discolouration underlying approximately 60% of the nail plate, which does not blanch with pressure. The toe is exquisitely tender over the nail. The nail plate itself is intact and adherent, the nail fold is not erythematous, and there is no pus, no proximal spread and no deformity suggesting fracture.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate management?",
        "6": "A 25-year-old Caucasian man presents to his GP with a lesion on his back after his friend noticed it while at the beach. He is unsure when the lesion first appeared. He denies any previous history of skin lesions or malignancy. He does not take any regular medications.\n\nOn examination there is a small, round, uniformly pigmented brown macule on the back, approximately 5 mm across, with a regular, symmetrical border. It is surrounded by a sharply defined, symmetrical rim of completely depigmented white skin, approximately 5 mm wide, giving a target-like appearance. The central lesion shows no colour variation, no irregular notching, no ulceration and no asymmetry.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "10": "A 32-year-old woman presents to the General Practice clinic with a painless swelling on the dorsum of her left index finger. This has been present for six weeks and has grown rapidly. It bleeds profusely on minor contact.\n\nOn examination there is a bright red, friable, dome-shaped, pedunculated nodule approximately 8 mm across, with a moist, glistening surface and a collarette of scale at its base. It bleeds readily on gentle palpation. There is no pigmentation, no irregular border and no colour variegation. It is not compressible and has no palpable thrill or bruit.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Gastroenterology - GORD (MCQ)": {
        "17": "A 27-year-old man presents with complete dysphagia after trying to swallow a large lump of meat, which he felt got stuck. An endoscopy is performed and a food bolus disimpacted.\n\nThe oesophageal mucosa shows multiple fixed concentric rings running the length of the oesophagus, giving it a corrugated appearance, with fine linear furrows and scattered white punctate exudate. There is no ulceration, no stricture at the gastro-oesophageal junction and no varices.\n\nOn further questioning, he reports a long history of food sticking, and a background of asthma and eczema.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate first-line treatment?",
        "21": "A 71-year-old man presents to the General Practice clinic to review a gastrointestinal concern he has had for the last twelve months. He has no significant medical history and takes no regular medications. He is a non-smoker and drinks alcohol occasionally. Physical examination is unremarkable.\n\nA fluoroscopic contrast swallow study shows a large outpouching arising from the wall of the oesophagus, with contrast pooling within the cavity and an air–fluid level visible. Contrast is retained in the pouch after the oesophagus has emptied. There is no stricture, no mucosal irregularity and normal peristalsis in the oesophageal body.\n\nWhich one of the following symptoms is he most likely to report?",
        "26": "A 28-year-old man presents to the General Practice clinic for review of quitting smoking. You notice a rash on his forearms which he then notes has been there for three weeks. He reports feeling generally unwell about six weeks ago and recalls a painless ulcer on his penis that healed on its own.\n\nOn examination there is a symmetrical, non-itchy, coppery-red maculopapular eruption over the trunk and forearms, extending onto the palms and soles, with fine scale. There is generalised painless lymphadenopathy. The lesions are not teardrop-shaped, there is no herald patch, and there is no Christmas-tree distribution along the skin cleavage lines.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Gastroenterology - Inflammatory Bowel Disease (MCQ)": {
        "16": "A 32-year-old man presents to the General Practice clinic with a one-month history of crampy lower abdominal pain, bloody diarrhoea, and 6 kg weight loss. He has no significant medical history apart from an appendicectomy five years ago. He does not take any regular medications. He also reports a painful perianal lesion.\n\nColonoscopy shows deep, serpiginous, linear ulcers in the terminal ileum and ascending colon, with intervening areas of entirely normal-looking mucosa, giving the surface a cobblestoned appearance. The rectum is spared. There is no continuous circumferential friability and no loss of vascular pattern in the distal colon.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "18": "A 64-year-old man presents with a six-month history of a painless abdominal swelling, which is slowly increasing in size and is most noticeable when he stands up. He reports no weight loss or change in bowel habits. He has a medical history of chronic Hepatitis C infection.\n\nOn examination there is a soft, reducible swelling immediately adjacent to the umbilicus, with a palpable defect in the abdominal wall at its neck. It expands and becomes more prominent on standing and on coughing, and reduces when he lies flat. The overlying skin is normal, with no ulceration, no induration and no discharge. There is shifting dullness on percussion of the abdomen.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "13": "A 35-year-old man presents to the General Practice clinic with a two-day history of pain over both pre-auricular regions. He has been at home for the past two weeks recovering from a flare-up of his known Crohn's disease, which had required a two-week hospital admission and commencement of corticosteroids. He also has a medical history of depression and is currently attempting to quit cigarette smoking. His regular medications include azathioprine, sertraline for depression, and nicotine replacement therapy. On examination, he appears unwell. His pulse rate is 100/min, blood pressure 130/80 mmHg, respiratory rate 18/min, SpO2 98% on room air, and temperature 38.2 C. There is warmth and tenderness on palpation of the pre-auricular regions. Pus is expressed from the parotid ducts. There is no evidence of a dental abscess Which one of the following is the most appropriate management?",
        "17": "A 62-year-old man presents with a three month history of episodic colicky abdominal pain. When it occurs the pain is generalised and is accompanied with nausea and abdominal bloating. These episodes may last several hours before resolving spontaneously. He has lost 5 kg in weight over the last six months, but has no other current medical problems. His medical history includes hypertension and an open appendicectomy. On examination his blood pressure is 128/82 mmHg, pulse 84/min and temperature 37.2 C. His abdomen is soft and there are no identifiable masses on palpation and bowel sounds are normal. A CT scan is performed and two representative slices are shown (image 1, 2). \\ ipt- \\ Seay Les Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "19": "A 60-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department with a one-hour history of central crushing chest pain, shortness of breath and sweating. He has a medical history of hypertension and hypercholesterolaemia, and is a current smoker.\n\nHis ECG shows ST-segment elevation of 3 mm in leads V2 to V5 with reciprocal changes inferiorly. Troponin is markedly elevated. Coronary angiography demonstrates an abrupt, complete occlusion of the proximal left anterior descending artery with a hazy, irregular filling defect at the site.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely underlying cause of his presentation?",
        "20": "A previously well 65-year-old woman presents with a two-day history of colicky abdominal pain and vomiting. She has not been able to tolerate any oral intake for the last 24 hours. She has a history of chronic kidney disease on haemodialysis.\n\nOn examination her blood pressure is 110/70 mmHg and pulse 100/min. Her BMI is 31. Her abdomen is distended but soft. The bowel sounds are increased and tinkling. In her right groin there is a tender, irreducible swelling immediately below and lateral to the pubic tubercle, with no cough impulse. The overlying skin is normal.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "22": "A 32-year-old woman presents to the General Practice clinic with a ten-year history of intermittent, generalised abdominal pain, bloating and loose stools. The pain is worse immediately prior to defecation, which then relieves the pain. There is no visible blood in her stools. She gets an episode of such pain about once a week and there do not appear to be any apparent triggers. Her sleep pattern is not disturbed by her symptoms. She has no other gastrointestinal symptoms, is otherwise in good health, does not take any regular medications and has no significant medical history. She consumes gluten regularly in her diet. On examination, she appears well with vital signs within normal limits. Abdominal examination is unremarkable with no tenderness or masses. Recent blood tests including full blood count, C-reactive protein, liver function tests, and tissue transglutaminase antibodies are all within normal ranges. Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "24": "He presented with a seven day history of acute onset diarrhoea accompanied with cramping abdominal pain, fatigue, and a fever of 37.8C. He has had six loose bowel actions per day with frank blood. Initial investigations show a CRP of 135, erythrocyte sedimentation rate of 42, with mild leukocytosis. A stool sample is negative for clostridioides difficile toxins. An abdominal X-ray excludes toxic megacolon. A sigmoidoscopy reveals markedly erythematous and inflamed colonic mucosa with decreased typical superficial vascular markings. There are superficial ulcers with streaks of blood along the mucosal lining of the descending and sigmoid colon. He was commenced on intravenous hydrocortisone however his symptoms failed to improve after 72 hours. Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Gastroenterology - Hernia (MCQ)": {
        "0": "A 69-year-old woman presents with a six-month history of early satiety. She has no other symptoms and is otherwise well. Physical examination is unremarkable.\n\nA CT scan shows a large defect in the diaphragm through which abdominal contents have herniated into the posterior mediastinum, sitting behind the heart. The herniated sac is large and contains gas-filled and soft-tissue structures.\n\nWhich one of the following pairs of structures is most likely to be found within this hernia?",
        "1": "A contrast study was performed in a previously well 47-year-old woman after she had a CT scan for an episode of epigastric pain. The CT had suggested an area of abnormality in the upper digestive tract, and more detailed imaging was requested.\n\nThe contrast study shows the duodenum failing to cross the midline. The duodenojejunal junction, which normally lies to the left of the vertebral spinous process at the level of the duodenal cap, instead lies over the body of the vertebra to the right of the midline, and the proximal jejunal loops all sit on the right side of the abdomen. There is no filling defect, no ulcer crater and no outpouching from the duodenal wall.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "4": "A 74-year-old man presents to the General Practice clinic with a three-month history of a painless swelling in the left side of his scrotum. He has a medical history of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease with two hospital admissions for infective exacerbations in the past year.\n\nOn examination there is a smooth, non-tender, fluctuant swelling surrounding the left testis. It transilluminates brightly when a light is held against it. It is possible to get above the swelling on palpation, and it is separate from and does not extend into the spermatic cord. There is no cough impulse, no overlying skin change and no regional lymphadenopathy.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely underlying cause?",
        "7": "A 28-year-old man presents to his General Practice clinic with a two-week history of worsening peri-umbilical pain. He reports intermittent umbilical discharge over the preceding months. He has no fever, chills, nausea, vomiting, or urinary symptoms. He has no significant medical history. His BMI is 34 kg/m².\n\nOn examination the umbilicus is erythematous and tender, with macerated skin, accumulated debris within the umbilical pit, and a small amount of malodorous discharge. There is no palpable abdominal wall defect, no cough impulse, no irreducible mass and no discharge of urine. The surrounding abdominal wall is soft and non-tender.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "21": "A 46-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department with a two-day history of increasing periumbilical pain. He has no other symptoms. His medical history includes type 2 diabetes, osteoarthritis of both knees and intermittent heartburn. His medications include metformin. He is obese and a long-term smoker with a chronic cough.\n\nOn examination there is a tense, exquisitely tender swelling adjacent to the umbilicus. It is irreducible, has no cough impulse, and the overlying skin is erythematous and dusky. He is febrile at 38.4°C with a pulse of 108/min. The abdomen is otherwise soft with localised guarding over the swelling.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate management?",
        "5": "A mother brings her six-month-old son to the General Practice clinic concerned about a lump that has appeared in his right groin region. She first noticed the lump two days ago and since then has noticed that it has increased in size. The infant has been crying intensely over the last 24 hours, and the lump fluctuates in size with crying and movement. The mother did not notice any diarrhoea or constipation associated with the new lump. The mother attended all the previous antenatal check-up and screening with no abnormal results. The baby was born prematurely at 35-weeks gestation via elective caesarean section, with no complications during birth. He has no other significant medical history and takes no regular medications. His vaccinations are all up to date. On examination, the infant is crying and waving his arms. He is currently afebrile, and his vital signs are all within the normal ranges. His abdomen is soft and there is a tender immovable 2cm lump immediately lateral to the right pubic tubercle overlying the superficial skin, located superolateral to the right testicle. The left testis is situated in its normal position in the scrotum, whilst the right testis can be palpated at the apex of the scrotum. Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?",
        "6": "An 82-year-old woman presents with a two-week history of anorexia and vomiting. She has started to vomit up recognisable particles of food eaten the previous day. She has lost 6 kg in weight.\n\nA CT scan of the abdomen shows a markedly dilated, food-filled stomach with a narrowed distal gastric outlet. Separately, gas is seen within the biliary tree, and there is a surgical anastomosis between the common bile duct and the duodenum from previous surgery. There is no small bowel obstruction and no gallstone in the bowel lumen.\n\nWhich one of the following operations has this patient previously undergone?",
        "16": "A 92-year-old woman presents to the Emergency Department with a 24 hour history of generalised colicky abdominal pain and distension. Her symptoms are becoming more severe. Her medical history includes a laparotomy and colectomy ten years earlier for a carcinoma of the colon. Her abdomen is distended, but soft. Increased bowel sounds are evident. A CT scan is arranged, with the request stating abdominal pain - cause unknown. ' (image 1, 2). 5 nase yn, nen 07:03:10 cue & ros 3/12/2013 07:03:10 TILT: ~ Aut Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "23": "A 65-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department with a two-day history of a painful swelling in his right groin. The pain has become increasingly severe over the last few hours. He thinks there may have been a swelling there for a few months previously, but he only noticed it when he coughed or strained. He has a medical history of myocardial ischaemia and insertion of two drug-eluting stents two months earlier. His regular medications include atorvastatin, perindopril, clopidogrel and aspirin. On examination, he appears unwell. His pulse rate is 110/min, blood pressure 140/90 mmHg, respiratory rate 18/min, SpO2 98% on room air, and temperature 37.2 C. His abdomen is soft and not distended. There is a tender, irreducible 6 cm swelling immediately above the right inguinal ligament. Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Gastroenterology - Upper GI Bleed (MCQ)": {
        "18": "A 65-year-old man presents to his General Practitioner with a two-month history of episodic passage of black, tarry stools. Over the same time period he has experienced some weight loss and fatigue. He does not report any abdominal pain or vomiting.\n\nEndoscopy shows a well-defined, rounded mass on the anterior wall of the body of the stomach, protruding into the lumen. The mucosa covering it is intact and of normal appearance apart from a small central ulcerated area at its apex, from which there is oozing. The gastric folds elsewhere are normal and pliable, with no diffuse thickening, no rigidity and no varices.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "7": "55-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department with a two day history of upper abdominal pain and the recent passage of dark, sticky and offensive smelling stools. There has been no nausea, vomiting nor diarrhoea. His history includes hypertension, osteoarthritis of the knees, and a previous ischaemic stroke 1 year ago. The only medications he takes are enalapril and aspirin. On examination his blood pressure is 155/90 mmHg, pulse 70/min, temperature 37.2 C, respiratory rate 20/min and SpQ2 97% on room air. He has epigastric tenderness without guarding and no abdominal distension. Digital rectal examination confirms the presence of melaena stool, The rest of the physical examination is unremarkable. Laboratory tests reveal: * Haemoglobin 102 g/L (135-175) Haematocrit: 0.30 (0.38-0.50) PLT: 190 x 10*9/L (150-450) * Urea: 10.2 mmol/L (2.5-7.5) * Cr: 93 pmol/L (60-110) * Bilirubin: 6 pmol/L (0-20) * Albumin: 40 g/L (35-50) Which one of the following is the initial step in management?",
        "21": "A 30-year-old woman presents to the Emergency Department with a one-month history of nausea and vomiting. She vomits undigested food several hours after a meal. She has no other health issues and does not take any medications. She has been worried about her weight and put herself on an unsupervised keto diet to lose weight. Over the last three months, she managed to lose 20 She appears cachectic and malnourished. Abdominal examination is unremarkable apart from a succussion splash. An abdominal CT scan is performed (Image 1, 2). Her serum amylase and lipase levels are within normal limits. Liver function test results are also within normal limits. Pas BAe ss: - i{ s Cee Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Gastroenterology - Bowel Obstruction (MCQ)": {
        "11": "An 82-year-old woman presents to the Emergency Department with a two-day history of abdominal pain, distension, and absolute constipation. She underwent a left hip arthroplasty two weeks ago and is making a steady recovery. Her medical history includes hypertension and a previous hysterectomy.\n\nOn examination the abdomen is distended and tympanic, with high-pitched tinkling bowel sounds. An abdominal X-ray shows multiple dilated loops of bowel in a central distribution, with valvulae conniventes crossing the full width of the bowel wall, and several air–fluid levels on the erect film. The colon is collapsed distally, with no gas in the rectum. There is no coffee-bean sign and no caecal dilatation.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "12": "A 72-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department with a one-day history of increasing central abdominal pain and abdominal distension, accompanied by constipation for the past three days. His medical history includes a sigmoid colectomy for carcinoma eight years ago.\n\nOn examination the abdomen is grossly distended and tympanic, with localised tenderness in the right iliac fossa. An abdominal X-ray shows a massively dilated, gas-filled loop of large bowel arising from the right iliac fossa and extending towards the left upper quadrant, with a single long air–fluid level and haustral markings preserved on one wall. The caecum measures 12 cm in diameter. There is tenderness and guarding over the distended segment.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate management?",
        "14": "A 53-year-old man presents with painful discolouration of the fingers of both hands. He also complains of itching over the affected skin. His medical history is unremarkable. He does not smoke or drink. It is winter and he has been doing a lot of work outside in the garden.\n\nOn examination there are several tender, dusky red-purple, swollen papules and plaques over the dorsal aspects of the fingers of both hands. They are warm and itchy, and the affected skin is slightly oedematous. There is no sharply demarcated triphasic colour change of the digits, no digital ulceration, no sclerodactyly and no nailfold infarcts. Peripheral pulses are normal.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate initial management?",
        "21": "A 50-year-old woman presents to the Emergency Department with a two-week history of intermittent colicky abdominal pain. She has experienced a recent change in her bowel habit, with episodes of diarrhoea and constipation. Her history includes hypertension and a previous colonoscopic polypectomy. She had a pigmented skin lesion excised from her back several years ago.\n\nAt laparotomy, a segment of small bowel is seen to have telescoped into the lumen of the adjacent distal segment, with the invaginated bowel and its mesentery drawn inside. At the leading point there is a firm, darkly pigmented mural nodule.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely underlying cause?",
        "5": "A 25-year-old woman presents to the Emergency Department with a 6-hour history of pain which came on rapidly in her lower abdomen. She does not feel nauseated and has not vomited. The pain is maximal in the right iliac fossa with localised guarding.\n\nAn ultrasound of the abdomen shows a blind-ending, non-compressible tubular structure in the right iliac fossa measuring 9 mm in diameter, with surrounding echogenic fat and a small volume of free fluid. Both ovaries appear normal with no adnexal mass and no torsion.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "15": "A 76-year-old woman presents with a two-day history of generalised, colicky abdominal pain, distension and constipation. Her medical history includes Parkinson's disease and hypertension. She has previously had an open hysterectomy.\n\nOn examination the abdomen is distended and tympanic with high-pitched bowel sounds. An abdominal X-ray shows multiple dilated loops of bowel in a central distribution, with valvulae conniventes crossing the full width of the bowel wall. The colon is collapsed distally. There is no gas in the biliary tree, no coffee-bean loop, and no caecal dilatation.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely cause?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Gastroenterology - Bowel Perforation / Acute Abdomen (MCQ)": {
        "4": "A 35-year-old man is brought into the Emergency Department after a domestic incident during which he was stabbed with a kitchen knife. On examination, he is fully conscious, alert and oriented. His pulse rate is 75/min, blood pressure 120/70 mmHg, respiratory rate 15/min.\n\nThere is a 4 cm laceration in the left upper quadrant of the anterior abdominal wall, through which a substantial length of fatty, vascular omentum is protruding and lying on the skin surface. There is localised tenderness and voluntary guarding around the wound.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate management?",
        "10": "A 21-year-old woman presents to the Emergency Department with a two day history of epigastric pain, She is normally in good health and does not take any medication apart from ibuprofen for dysmenorthoea. Her last period finished three days ago and she was taking ibuprofen immediately prior to that. On examination she looks well, with a blood pressure of 110/70 mmHg, pulse rate of 90/min and temperature of 37.2C. She is tender in the right upper quadrant and epigastrium, but her abdomen is otherwise soft. A CT scan is performed (/mages 1, 2, 3). ~ TILT:0 eat Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "11": "A 46-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department with a four hour history of sudden onset and rapidly worsening upper abdominal pain. His medical history includes type 2 diabetes and a work-related back injury sustained three weeks ago. His medications include metformin and regular use of diclofenac for his back pain. His back pain has been more severe recently. On examination he is morbidly obese, with a blood pressure of 115/80 mmHg, pulse rate 110/min, temperature 37.4 C and respiratory rate 18/min. Because of his body habitus, abdominal examination is limited, but there is tenderness in the epigastrium and the rest of the abdomen appears to be soft. Which one of the following is the most appropriate diagnostic investigation?",
        "12": "63-year-old woman presents to the Emergency Department with a two-day history of left iliac fossa pain, constipation and a general feeling of malaise and anorexia. She has no significant medical history and takes no regular medications. On examination, she appears well. Her pulse rate is 80/min, blood pressure 125/70 mmHg, respiratory rate 16/min, SpO2 98% on room air, and temperature 37.6 C. There is mild and localised tenderness in the left iliac fossa. She would rather not be admitted to hospital unless necessary, Investigations reveal: + White cell count: 9.5 x10 /L (4.0-11.0) + C-reactive protein: 5 mg/L (<5). * Electrolytes and renal function: normal. * Abdominal CT scan: scattered sigmoid diverticulae with thickening of the wall of the sigmoid and pericolic fat stranding Which one of the following is the most appropriate plan of management?",
        "20": "A 75-year-old woman presents to the Emergency Department with a four-day history of left iliac fossa pain, nausea and vomiting. She has a medical history of hypertension and hypercholesterolaemia. She is febrile at 38.5°C with a raised white cell count.\n\nA CT of the abdomen and pelvis shows sigmoid diverticulosis with wall thickening and pericolic fat stranding, together with a well-defined, rim-enhancing fluid collection measuring 6 cm adjacent to the sigmoid colon. There is no free intraperitoneal gas and no generalised faecal peritonitis.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate management?",
        "22": "A 32-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department following a motor vehicle accident. He has severe abdominal and chest pain. He has no significant medical history and takes no regular medications. On examination, he appears anxious. His pulse rate is 118/min, blood pressure 86/68 mmHg, respiratory rate 25/min, SpO2 98% on room air, and temperature 36.8 C. There is a scalp laceration that has stopped bleeding. His trachea is midline and bilateral breath sounds are heard on chest auscultation. Heart sounds are normal with no murmurs. Jugular veins are collapsed, Abdominal examination reveals some tenderness to both upper quadrants, but no rigidity, guarding, or rebound tenderness. There is no obvious bruising. The rest of the examination is unremarkable. Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step in evaluation?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Gastroenterology - Cholangitis / Choledocholithiasis (MCQ)": {
        "0": "A 28-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department following two weeks of progressive malaise, increasing abdominal distension and jaundice. He has no significant medical or surgical history and does not take any regular medications. He is a non-smoker and drinks two standard drinks a week.\n\nOn examination he is jaundiced, with tender hepatomegaly and tense ascites. Doppler ultrasound of the liver shows absent flow in the hepatic veins, with the intrahepatic portions of the veins not visualised, and a hypertrophied caudate lobe. The portal vein is patent, the biliary tree is not dilated, and there are no gallstones.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "1": "A 67-year-old woman with known gallstones is admitted with a two-day history of jaundice and right upper quadrant pain. She has no other significant medical history and takes no regular medications. On examination, her pulse rate is 96/min and blood pressure 130/90 mmHg. She is clinically jaundiced. She reports pale stools and dark urine.\n\nAbdominal ultrasound shows a dilated common bile duct measuring 12 mm, containing an echogenic focus with posterior acoustic shadowing at its lower end. The intrahepatic ducts are dilated. The liver parenchyma is normal.\n\nWhich one of the following is most likely to be found on urinalysis?",
        "5": "A 65-year-old man presents with a four-day history of pale stools, dark urine and itching. He has no other symptoms. His medical history includes chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and benign prostatic hyperplasia.\n\nOn examination he is clinically jaundiced, with no hepatomegaly and no palpable abdominal mass. Liver function tests show a markedly raised alkaline phosphatase and gamma-glutamyl transferase, with a conjugated hyperbilirubinaemia and only mildly elevated transaminases, confirming an obstructive picture. No imaging has yet been performed.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate next investigation?",
        "3": "A 48-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department with a two-day history of constant, dull lower abdominal pain. He has no associated symptoms and no significant medical history.\n\nOn examination he is afebrile with a soft abdomen and mild localised tenderness in the left iliac fossa. There is no guarding and no rebound. A CT scan of the abdomen shows a small ovoid lesion adjacent to the descending colon with a thin ring of enhancement and a central density equivalent to fat, with a little surrounding stranding. There is no diverticular wall thickening, no abscess collection, no free gas and no bowel obstruction.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate management?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Gastroenterology - Appendicitis (MCQ)": {
        "0": "A previously well 52-year-old man presents with a five-day history of lower abdominal pain. The pain was initially intermittent and has now become constant. He has no other symptoms apart from intermittent nausea. On examination, his blood pressure is 115/75 mmHg, pulse 96/min and temperature 38.1°C. There is a tender, ill-defined fullness in the right iliac fossa with localised guarding.\n\nA CT scan shows an inflammatory mass in the right iliac fossa, immediately posterior to the caecum, with surrounding fat stranding and a thickened appendix at its centre. There is no discrete drainable collection, no free gas and no bowel obstruction.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate management?",
        "2": "A 35-year-old man presents with a two-week history of increasing right upper quadrant pain which is now associated with intermittent fever and sweats. He feels generally unwell. He returned from a trip to South-East Asia two months ago, where he had an episode of bloody diarrhoea.\n\nA CT of the abdomen shows a single large, rounded, low-attenuation lesion in the right lobe of the liver with a peripherally enhancing rim. There are no gallstones and the gallbladder wall is not thickened. The pancreas is normal, the appendix is normal, and there is no colonic diverticular disease.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely underlying cause?",
        "3": "A 16-year-old girl presents to the emergency department feeling unwell with a fever. Five days previously she had been discharged from the hospital following a laparoscopic appendicectomy. She had presented a week ago with acute abdominal pain and was found to have perforated appendicitis. The patient initially recovered well from the surgery and was discharged on 2nd postoperative day. Over the past three days, she has developed a high-grade fever associated with chills and abdominal pain. On examination her blood pressure is 120/70 mmHg, pulse 100/min, temperature 38.7 C and respiratory rate 18/min, There is lower abdominal examination tenderness and a boggy sensation on rectal palpation. Investigations reveal white blood cell count 15 x 10 9/L (4.0-11.0), C-reactive protein 90 mg/L (O to 5 mg/L), and blood cultures are pending. nce wean Which one of the following is the most appropriate plan of management?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Hepatology - Alcoholic Liver Disease / Cirrhosis (MCQ)": {
        "8": "A 55-year-old man presents to the General Practice clinic for review of his benign prostatic hyperplasia. He was diagnosed two years ago after experiencing urinary frequency, urgency and a weakened stream. His only medication is a combined dutasteride/tamsulosin tablet. He drinks two standard drinks a week and has a BMI of 26 kg/m².\n\nOn examination there is bilateral, symmetrical, firm, rubbery glandular tissue palpable concentrically beneath both nipples, which is mildly tender. There are no stigmata of chronic liver disease, no testicular masses, and no features of adrenal excess.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely cause of this finding?",
        "12": "A 68-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department with a two-week history of painless swelling of his face, neck and arms. He also reports a bluish discolouration on his chest. He has a 40 pack-year smoking history. He has no significant medical history.\n\nOn examination the face, neck and both upper limbs are oedematous and plethoric. There are distended, tortuous superficial veins visible over the anterior chest wall, and the jugular veins are distended and non-pulsatile. The swelling worsens when he raises his arms above his head. There is no facial asymmetry, no unilateral arm swelling, and no peripheral oedema of the legs.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "6": "A 76-year-old woman presents to the General Practice clinic with a one-month history of anorexia and weight loss. She reports her skin and eyes have become yellow over the past two weeks. She has no significant medical history and takes no regular medications. She is a non-smoker and drinks minimal alcohol.\n\nOn examination she appears jaundiced but is not in distress. Her vital signs are within normal limits. Her abdomen is soft and non-tender. The jaundice is painless.\n\nA CT scan of the abdomen shows a mass in the head of the pancreas, with dilatation of both the common bile duct and the pancreatic duct, and a distended, palpable gallbladder. The intrahepatic ducts are dilated. The liver parenchyma is smooth with no nodularity, no splenomegaly and no varices. There is no gastric wall thickening and no renal mass.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "20": "A 45-year-old man presents to his GP for an annual health check. He is generally well, and his history reveals regular alcohol consumption of 3-4 standard drinks most days of the week.\n\nOn examination of the chest there are two small, symmetrical, pigmented papules situated on the milk line below and medial to each nipple. Each has a small central areola. There is no firm, rubbery glandular tissue palpable concentrically beneath either nipple, no waxy 'stuck-on' plaque, no irregular pigmented macule, and the sternum is not depressed.\n\nWhich one of the following best describes this finding?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Hepatology - Viral Hepatitis (MCQ)": {
        "0": "A 42-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department with a one-week history of feeling generally unwell, with fevers and sweats. Two days ago he began to get pain in his upper abdomen. He has no significant medical history except for an episode of diarrhoea one month ago while travelling overseas.\n\nOn examination he is febrile at 38.8°C with tender hepatomegaly. A CT scan of the abdomen shows a single, large, rounded low-attenuation lesion in the right lobe of the liver with a peripherally enhancing rim. There are no gallstones, the biliary tree is not dilated, and there are no other lesions elsewhere in the liver.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate initial treatment?",
        "6": "A 44-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department with a three-day history of yellowing of the skin and confusion. He has mild abdominal pain in the right upper quadrant and anorexia.\n\nOn examination, there is severe jaundice of the sclera and skin. He is confused, with a coarse flapping tremor of the outstretched hands. There is tender hepatomegaly, spider naevi over the upper chest, and palmar erythema. He is febrile at 37.9°C. He drinks approximately 15 standard drinks per day. Liver function tests show an AST:ALT ratio greater than 2, with both transaminases below 300 U/L, and a markedly raised bilirubin. Abdominal ultrasound shows no biliary dilatation and no gallstones.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "17": "A 48-year-old woman presents with a three-week history of right upper quadrant discomfort and fatigue. She is now experiencing generalised itching, and a dry mouth and eyes. Her medical history includes diabetes (for which she takes metformin), viral hepatitis and hypothyroidism.\n\nOn examination there are yellowish plaques over both upper eyelids. Liver function tests show a markedly raised alkaline phosphatase and gamma-glutamyl transferase with only mildly raised transaminases, and a raised serum cholesterol. Anti-mitochondrial antibodies are present at high titre. Anti-smooth muscle and anti-nuclear antibodies are negative, and serum IgG is normal.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "14": "A 46-year-old man presents to the hospital with a two-week history of right upper quadrant abdominal pain, anorexia, and jaundice. He admits to drinking about 8-10 beers daily for the past 15 years. He has no significant medical history and does not take any regular medications. On examination, he appears ill. His pulse rate is 90/min, blood pressure 100/80 mmHg, respiratory rate 18/min, SpO2 98% on room air, and temperature jaundiced and has mild tenderness in his right upper abdomen with hepatomegaly. Investigations reveal: + AST: 180 U/L (10-40) * ALT: 70 U/L (7-56) * Bilirubin: 88 mol/L (2-24) + HBsAg: negative * Anti-HBe: negative + Anti-HBs: positive + Ultrasound: hepatomegaly and fatty infiltration. Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?",
        "20": "A 28-year-old Aboriginal woman presents to the General Practice clinic with her 6-month-old son for a routine check-up and vaccinations. The woman is from a remote community in the Northern Territory. She has no significant medical history and takes no regular medications. Her son has been developing normally and has no medical concerns. On examination, the child appears well with vital signs within normal limits. The mother asks about the recommended vaccination schedule for her child, as she has heard it might be different for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander children. Which one of the following best describes the current Australian Immunisation Schedule recommendations for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander children?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Paediatrics - Paediatric Gastroenterology (MCQ)": {
        "2": "A 35-year-old previously healthy man presents to the Emergency Department with a four-hour history of rapid-onset severe, cramp-like abdominal pain and bloating. He feels nauseated and has vomited once.\n\nDuring the physical examination the physician notices multiple small, flat, dark blue-brown pigmented macules clustered on the vermillion border of the lips, around the mouth, and on the buccal mucosa. His abdomen is distended with high-pitched bowel sounds. He reports that his father and sister have similar pigmentation and that his father had bowel surgery in his thirties.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Endocrinology - Type 2 Diabetes (MCQ)": {
        "5": "A 71-year-old woman presents to the Emergency Department with sudden onset loss of vision in her left eye occurring two hours ago. She has a medical history of type 2 diabetes and hypertension. Her medications include gliclazide and captopril. The visual loss was painless.\n\nFundoscopy of the left eye shows widespread flame-shaped and dot-blot haemorrhages scattered across all four quadrants of the retina, with marked venous dilatation and tortuosity, optic disc swelling, and multiple cotton-wool spots — giving a 'blood and thunder' appearance. There is no cherry-red spot at the macula, no retinal pallor, and no retinal tear or elevation.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "6": "A 60-year-old man presents with a three-month history of a painless lesion on his right foot. He has tried various topical treatments without effect. He remembers having a plantar wart many years ago. He has type 2 diabetes.\n\nOn examination there is a punched-out ulcer with a thick, heaped rim of callus on the plantar aspect of the right foot, adjacent to the third and fourth metatarsal heads, over a pressure point. The base is clean and granulating. The lesion is entirely painless. Monofilament testing shows absent sensation over the forefoot. Both dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial pulses are strongly palpable, and the foot is warm and well perfused.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate next investigation?",
        "7": "A 71-year-old woman presents with a two-week history of gradually deteriorating vision in her left eye. Her symptoms started with flashing lights and then a cloudy diminution in vision. Her medical history includes type 2 diabetes.\n\nFundoscopy of the left eye shows an elevated, billowing sheet of retina with a wrinkled, corrugated surface, ballooning forward into the vitreous, with an associated horseshoe-shaped tear at its superior edge. The underlying choroid is out of focus relative to the elevated retina. There are no widespread haemorrhages, no venous tortuosity and no cherry-red spot.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "27": "A 57-year-old man presents with a five-year history of numbness and tingling in his right little finger. This is particularly worse during sleep and he is sometimes awoken in the middle of the night due to the discomfort.\n\nOn examination of the right hand, the fourth and fifth fingers are held in a fixed position with hyperextension at the metacarpophalangeal joints and flexion at the interphalangeal joints. There is wasting of the hypothenar eminence and of the first dorsal interosseous muscle, with visible guttering between the metacarpals. Sensation is reduced over the little finger and the ulnar half of the ring finger. Thenar bulk and thumb abduction are normal. There is no palpable fibrous cord in the palm.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "30": "A 58-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department with a two-day history of flashing lights and six hours of visual loss. He has not had any recent eye trauma. He has a medical history of type 2 diabetes mellitus and hypertension.\n\nOn examination, visual acuity in the affected eye is reduced to counting fingers. Fundoscopy shows an elevated, mobile, corrugated sheet of retina billowing into the vitreous cavity, with an associated peripheral retinal tear. The red reflex is diminished but the view of the retina is not obscured by blood. There is no vitreous haze of red cells and no anterior chamber cellular reaction.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "36": "An 81-year-old man presents to the General Practice clinic with a two-day history of double vision and unsteadiness when walking. He has a medical history of hypertension and type 2 diabetes mellitus. His regular medications include metformin and lisinopril.\n\nOn examination, the right eye fails to abduct past the midline on attempted gaze to the right, while adduction of the right eye is full. Horizontal diplopia is worst on looking to the right and resolves on looking to the left. There is no ptosis, the pupils are equal and reactive, and elevation, depression and adduction of both eyes are otherwise normal.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "49": "A 45-year-old man presents to his General Practice clinic with three days of pain and swelling around his left eye. He has a medical history of type 2 diabetes and atopic eczema. His medications include metformin, glipizide and topical hydrocortisone.\n\nOn examination there is a tense, exquisitely tender, erythematous, fluctuant swelling localised to the medial canthus of the left eye, below and medial to the inner corner of the eyelid, overlying the lacrimal sac. Pressure over the swelling expresses purulent material from the punctum. There is epiphora. Eye movements are full and painless, visual acuity is normal, and there is no proptosis.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate management?",
        "55": "A 63-year-old woman presents to the clinic with a complaint of thick, curved and discoloured toenails on her right foot. She reports that the nails have been gradually thickening over the past several years and have become increasingly difficult to trim. She lives alone and has poor eyesight. She has not been able to reach her feet to cut her nails for some years.\n\nOn examination the nail of the right great toe is grossly thickened, opaque, yellow-brown, and markedly elongated, curving over on itself into a thick, spiralled, horn-like deformity. The surrounding nail folds are not erythematous, there is no discharge, and the nail is not embedded into the soft tissue. There is no subungual debris or onycholysis of the other nails.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate management?",
        "10": "A 94-year-old woman is brought to see her General Practitioner by her daughter who is concerned about a reddened scaly area on her mother's left forearm. This has been present for at least three months. Her mother has a medical history of dementia and hypertension, and is frail.\n\nOn examination there is a well-demarcated, flat, erythematous plaque approximately 2 cm across on the left forearm, with an adherent, rough, scaly surface. The border is sharply defined and irregular. There is no induration, no ulceration, no rolled pearly edge and no nodularity. Biopsy shows full-thickness atypia of the epidermis with an intact basement membrane.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate treatment?",
        "39": "He was found confused with a capillary blood glucose of 2.8 mmol/L (3.9-7.8). He has a medical history of type 2 diabetes, hypertension, atrial fibrillation, heart failure, chronic kidney disease (estimated glomerular filtration rate 45 mL/min/1.73m?), osteoarthritis, and depression. His medications include metformin 1000 mg twice daily, gliclazide modified release 60 mg daily, sitagliptin 50 mg daily, perindopril, bisoprolol, apixaban, furosemide, duloxetine, paracetamol, and cholecalciferol. His most recent glycated haemoglobin (HbA1c) was 8.2% (4.0-6.0) three months ago. He reports difficulty managing his multiple daily medications and often forgets his evening doses. On examination, he appears frail but has recovered from his hypoglycaemic episode. His vital signs are within normal limits. He has reduced grip strength and walks with a frame. Which one of the following is the most appropriate principle for managing his diabetes?",
        "47": "A 52-year-old caucasian man presents to his general practitioner for a routine health checkup because he is worried that he might be suffering from diabetes as his elder brother has recently been diagnosed with type 2 diabetes. On examination his blood pressure 140/90 mmHg, pulse 90/min and temperature 37.2 C. Physical examination is unremarkable. Which one of the following would be the most appropriate first-line laboratory test to screen for diabetes in this patient?",
        "56": "A 45-year-old Aboriginal man presents to the General Practice clinic with poorly controlled type 2 diabetes. He has recently moved to the city for work and lives alone. He reports feeling isolated and disconnected from his community. He has a medical history of hypertension and takes metformin and perindopril. On examination, he appears well with vital signs within normal limits. His HbAtc is 9.5% (80 mmol/mol). Which one of the following is the LEAST appropriate next step to improve this patient's overall health and wellbeing?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Endocrinology - Thyroid - Hyperthyroidism / Graves (MCQ)": {
        "14": "A 30-year-old woman presents to the General Practice clinic with a three-week history of increased anxiety, tremulousness, insomnia, palpitations, and increased bowel frequency. Six weeks ago she was admitted following a failed suicide attempt, during which she underwent a contrast-enhanced CT scan.\n\nOn examination she has a fine tremor, warm moist palms and a resting tachycardia of 108/min. There is no goitre, no thyroid bruit, no thyroid tenderness and no eye signs. Thyroid function tests show a suppressed TSH with elevated free T4 and free T3. A radioiodine uptake scan shows markedly reduced uptake throughout the gland. TSH-receptor antibodies are negative.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Endocrinology - Calcium / Parathyroid (MCQ)": {
        "0": "A previously well 68-year-old man presents with two months of back pain and bilateral lower leg weakness. A detailed history does not provide any other information apart from a loss of libido over the last few years.\n\nOn examination there is palpable tenderness over the lower lumbar spine, with reduced power in both lower limbs. A lateral radiograph of the lumbar spine shows crush fractures of the third and fourth lumbar vertebrae, with generalised reduction in bone density but no lytic or sclerotic focal lesions. Serum calcium and parathyroid hormone are within normal limits, and serum protein electrophoresis shows no paraprotein. Morning serum testosterone is markedly low with an inappropriately low LH and FSH.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely underlying cause of his fractures?",
        "8": "A 52-year-old woman presents to the General Practice clinic with a six-month history of worsening depression, anxiety, and difficulty concentrating. She reports occasional panic attacks and insomnia. She has no significant medical history and takes no regular medications. On examination, she appears well with vital signs within normal limits. Mental state examination reveals a depressed mood, psychomotor retardation, and mild cognitive impairment. She scores 24/30 on the Mini-Mental State Examination. Routine blood tests show a serum calcium level of 2.8 mmol/L (2.1-2.6), sodium 138 mmol/L (135-145), potassium 3.8 mmol/L (3.5-5.0), magnesium 0.9 mmol/L (0.7-1.0), phosphate 0.8 mmol/L (0.8-1.5), and a slightly elevated thyroid-stimulating hormone of 5.2 mIU/L (0.4-4.0). Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Respiratory - Pneumonia (MCQ)": {
        "6": "A 4-year-old boy is brought to the Emergency Department by his mother because of a 3-hour history of chest pain and mild shortness of breath. Immediately prior to this, he was unsupervised in his playroom. He has been well recently, but his mother reports a sudden bout of coughing and choking.\n\nOn examination there is reduced chest expansion on the left with absent breath sounds and dullness to percussion over the left hemithorax. A chest X-ray shows complete opacification (whiteout) of the left hemithorax with no air bronchograms. The trachea and mediastinal structures are shifted towards the left, and the left hemidiaphragm is elevated.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "17": "A 56-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department with a six-day history of upper respiratory tract symptoms, myalgia, and fever. He has been on a cruise ship for seven days. In the past two days, after initially starting to improve, he has developed a productive cough with yellow sputum and a recurrence of high fever.\n\nOn examination he is febrile at 39.1°C with a respiratory rate of 28/min and SpO2 90% on room air. There are coarse crackles and bronchial breathing at the right base with dullness to percussion. A chest X-ray shows dense right lower lobe consolidation with air bronchograms. Influenza A PCR is positive.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate treatment?",
        "21": "An 81-year-old man with a 50 pack-year smoking history and prior alcohol excess suffers a rapid deterioration 24 hours following a colectomy and end colostomy for a perforated, obstructing carcinoma. He has received large volumes of intravenous crystalloid intraoperatively and postoperatively.\n\nOn examination his JVP is elevated, there are fine bibasal crackles, and he has developed peripheral oedema. His chest X-ray shows increased opacity in both lower zones with upper-lobe venous diversion and interstitial septal lines, and the cardiac silhouette is enlarged. He is afebrile with a normal white cell count. There is no focal consolidation with air bronchograms and no pleuritic pain.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely cause of his deterioration?",
        "22": "A 28-year-old woman presents to the Emergency Department with a three-day history of fever, productive cough with yellow sputum, and progressive shortness of breath. She is 12 weeks pregnant with her first child and has had an uncomplicated pregnancy thus far. She has no significant medical history and does not take any regular medications apart from pregnancy vitamins. She works as a primary school teacher. On examination, she appears unwell. Her pulse rate is 110/min, blood pressure 110/70 mmHg, respiratory rate 24/min, SpO2 94% on room air, and temperature 38.5 C. Chest examination reveals crackles in the right lower zone. Heart sounds are normal. The fetal heart rate is 150/min Blood tests show a white cell count of 15.0 x 1049/L (4.0-11.0) with neutrophilia and C-reactive protein of 120 mg/L (<5). The patient expresses concern about having a chest X-ray due to potential radiation effects on her developing baby. Which one of the following is the most appropriate advice regarding imaging for this patient?",
        "24": "68-year-old woman is being reviewed on the surgical ward 36 hours after an elective total hip replacement. The duty intern has been asked to see her because she has developed a bothersome cough. She reports feeling generally unwell but denies any specific pain beyond her surgical site. She has no significant medical history. Her medications include aspirin 100 mg daily for venous thromboembolism (VTE) prophylaxis, and she is using sequential compression devices (SCDs). She takes no other regular medications. She smokes five cigarettes per day, with a 12-pack-year smoking history. On examination, she appears uncomfortable due to the cough but is otherwise stable. Her pulse rate is 88/min, blood pressure 130/80 mmHg, respiratory rate 18/min, temperature 37.4 C, and SpO2 95% on room air. Her surgical wound appears clean and dry. Chest auscultation reveals decreased breath sounds at the left base with no crepitations or wheeze audible. Investigations including an ECG, full blood examination and electrolytes, urea and creatinine are normal. Which one of the following is the most likely cause of this patient's cough?",
        "25": "The procedure is uneventful and the patient is prescribed immunosuppressants to prevent rejection of the donor kidney. Prior to the procedure he was found to be PPD-negative and given the pneumococcal vaccine. He was also found to be HIV negative. Six months later, the patient develops exertional dyspnoea and mild-moderate non-productive cough. On examination there are diffuse crackles bilaterally. On examination his blood pressure is 115/75 mmHg, pulse 79/min, temperature 37.2 C, respiratory rate 14/min and SpO2 94% on room air. A chest X-ray shows diffuse, bilateral symmetrical interstitial infiltrates extending from the perihilar region. An induced saline sputum sample shows clusters of cysts that stain with methenamine silver. Which is the following medications is most likely to have been prescribed in order to prevent this?",
        "26": "A 10-day-old boy presents to the Emergency Department with a three-day history of eye discharge. He was born at term via normal vaginal delivery with no complications. His mother had limited antenatal care and did not receive routine screening tests during pregnancy. He has no other medical conditions and takes no medications. On examination, he appears well with vital signs within normal limits. There is watery to mucopurulent discharge from both eyes with mild conjunctival injection and lid swelling. The corneas appear clear. Which one of the following is the most appropriate initial management?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Respiratory - Asthma (MCQ)": {
        "4": "A 68-year-old man is admitted to hospital with a community-acquired pneumonia. His medical history includes hypertension, asthma and hyperlipidaemia. His medication list includes rosuvastatin, salbutamol and enalapril, which was recently commenced. His serum potassium is 7.1 mmol/L (3.5-5.0).\n\nHis ECG shows tall, peaked, narrow-based T waves in the precordial leads, a prolonged PR interval, and broadening of the QRS complex.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate immediate treatment?",
        "9": "A 30-year-old man presents to the General Practice clinic with worsening asthma symptoms over the past three weeks. He reports increasing shortness of breath with associated chest tightness. He has had asthma for 20 years. His only medication is salbutamol metered dose inhaler (MDI!) as needed, which he has been taking two puffs (100 microg) three to four times per day. He has no other significant medical history. He feels well in himself and cannot identify any triggers for this deterioration. On examination, he appears well with vital signs within normal limits. His inhaler technique is correct on demonstration. His peak expiratory flow rate is 280 L/min (predicted >420 L/min). Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?",
        "12": "A 24-year-old woman presents to the General Practice clinic with a five-year history of episodic breathing difficulty and cough. She reports that she primarily develops symptoms during and immediately following upper respiratory tract infections, though occasional episodes also occur during periods of stress at work. During flare periods she is functionally limited, often having recurrent coughing attacks while attempting to consult with clients at her job asa dietitian. Episodes are characterised by difficulty breathing in, severe dry coughing attacks, throat tightness, and a high-pitched noise during inspiration. She was previously diagnosed with asthma but showed no improvement with inhaled or nebulised salbutamol. She has no significant medical history and takes no regular medications. On examination, she appears well with vital signs within normal limits. Chest examination reveals normal air entry with no wheeze. Spirometry shows normal flow volume loops when asymptomatic, with no change on bronchodilator challenge. Bronchial provocation testing is negative. Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?",
        "14": "25-year-old woman presents to the Emergency Department with a four-hour history of progressively worsening shortness of breath and wheezing. She has a history of asthma since childhood, She uses a salbutamol inhaler as needed but has been using it more frequently over the past week due to a viral upper respiratory tract infection. She has no other significant medical history and takes no regular medications. On examination, she appears distressed and is using accessory muscles to breathe. Her pulse rate is 125/min, blood pressure 140/85 mmHg, respiratory rate 38/min, SpO2 91% on 15 L/min oxygen via non-rebreather mask, and temperature 37.2 C, Chest auscultation reveals diffuse expiratory wheezes with poor air entry bilaterally. She has received three doses of nebulised salbutamol and ipratropium bromide over the past hour with minimal improvement. She has also received intravenous hydrocortisone. Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Respiratory - Lung Cancer (MCQ)": {
        "0": "A 76-year-old woman presents with a two-month history of a rapidly enlarging lump in her neck. This enlargement has been painless. Over the last week she has experienced hoarseness and difficulty in swallowing. She has had an unintentional weight loss of 6 kg.\n\nOn examination there is a hard, irregular, fixed mass in the anterior compartment of the neck, which moves upwards on swallowing but not on tongue protrusion. It is not fluctuant and does not transilluminate. There is associated cervical lymphadenopathy. Flexible laryngoscopy shows a left vocal cord palsy with a normal-appearing larynx.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "7": "A previously well 62-year-old man presents with a one-month history of a hoarse voice. He has a 35 pack-year smoking history and drinks three standard drinks a day.\n\nThe only abnormality on physical examination is a drooping of the right upper eyelid with a constricted right pupil that reacts normally to light. Both pupils dilate poorly in the dark, and the anisocoria is more marked in dim light. There is reduced sweating over the right side of the face. Eye movements are full and visual acuity is normal.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate next investigation?",
        "14": "A 62-year-old man presents to the General Practice clinic with a six-month history of a hoarse voice following radiotherapy for squamous cell carcinoma of the lung. He has no other significant medical history and takes no regular medications.\n\nOn examination there is drooping of the right upper eyelid and a constricted right pupil which reacts normally to light. Extraocular movements are full in all directions and there is no diplopia. There is reduced sweating over the right side of the face and neck.\n\nInjury to which one of the following structures is most likely responsible?",
        "8": "A 53-year-old man presents to the General Practice clinic with a three-month history of persistent cough. He has a 30 pack-year smoking history and continues to smoke. He has no significant medical history and does not take any regular medications. He works as a carpenter and has no relevant travel history. On examination, he appears well with vital signs within normal limits. Respiratory examination reveals normal breath sounds with no added sounds. A low-dose screening CT of the chest reveals a solitary pulmonary nodule measuring 6mm in diameter adjacent to the bifurcation of the right pulmonary arteries. The nodule has smooth margins and no calcification. No other abnormalities are detected. Which one of the following is the most appropriate management?",
        "11": "A 40-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department with a five-hour history of sudden-onset shortness of breath and chest discomfort. He has no cough, nausea, dizziness or lightheadedness. He has cystic fibrosis.\n\nOn examination his respiratory rate is 26/min and SpO2 92% on room air. There is hyper-resonance and reduced breath sounds over the left hemithorax. A chest X-ray shows a left-sided pneumothorax with a visible visceral pleural edge and a rim of 3.5 cm at the level of the hilum. The trachea is central and he is haemodynamically stable.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate management?",
        "17": "A 72-year-old woman presents to the Emergency Department with a two week history of worsening breathlessness and facial swelling. She has a medical history of metastatic non-small cell lung cancer, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD), type 2 diabetes mellitus, and hypertension. Her regular medications include salbutamol inhaler as required, Symbicort inhaler, metformin, and amlodipine. On examination, she appears distressed with audible stridor and a swollen face. Her pulse rate is 110/min, blood pressure 145/90 mmHg, respiratory rate 28/min, SpO2 88% on room air, and temperature 37.2 C. Examination of the chest reveals mild expiratory wheeze bilaterally. She has some swelling of her upper limbs. Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?",
        "18": "A76-year-old male presents with a two month history of painless double vision. It is generally better in the morning and worsens throughout the day. His symptoms have progressed over the last two weeks. Additionally, he needs to hold his eyes open with his hands in order to read a book. He has no facial, arm or leg weakness and can walk and swallow normally. He has bilateral ptosis with no gaze palsy or ophthalmoplegia. His pupillary reflex is intact bilaterally. There is no relative afferent pupillary defect. His facial movements have normal power bilaterally. He has no bulbar palsy, no hoarse voice, and an intact gag reflex Given the most likely diagnosis, which one of the following is the most appropriate initial treatment?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Respiratory - Tuberculosis (MCQ)": {
        "1": "A 71-year-old man presents with a three-week history of coughing up blood. He reports at least five to six episodes every 2-3 days of coughing up bright red blood, approximately one to two tablespoons on each occasion. He also has mild shortness of breath and has lost 7 kg over three months. He has a 50 pack-year smoking history.\n\nA chest X-ray shows a 4 cm spiculated mass lesion in the right upper zone, with associated right hilar enlargement. There is no cavitation, no upper-lobe fibronodular change and no pleural effusion. There is no anterior mediastinal mass.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Respiratory - Pulmonary Embolism (MCQ)": {
        "4": "A 76-year-old woman, who has a 42 pack-year smoking history, presents to the Emergency Department with shortness of breath that has progressively become worse over the past two days. She does not have a cough, haemoptysis or any associated chest pain. She has active malignancy and is receiving chemotherapy.\n\nOn examination her pulse is 112/min, blood pressure 118/72 mmHg, respiratory rate 26/min and SpO2 91% on room air. The chest is clear on auscultation, with no wheeze and no crackles. Her chest X-ray is unremarkable. She is haemodynamically stable and her renal function is normal.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate investigation?",
        "13": "A 40-year-old woman presents to the Emergency Department with sudden onset shortness of breath and chest pain four days after an elective gastric resection for carcinoma. The pain is on the right side of her chest and worse with inspiration. She has no significant medical history and takes no regular medications. On examination, she appears distressed, Her pulse rate is 120/min, blood pressure 98/60 mmHg, respiratory rate 32/min, SpO2 84% on room air (94% on Hi-Flow with FiO2 of 0.6), and temperature 36.8 C. Her jugular venous pressure is elevated and heart sounds are normal. Chest examination reveals reduced air entry on the right side. Initial troponin is 85 ng/L (0-26) and ECG shows sinus tachycardia with right heart strain. A CT pulmonary angiogram is performed (Image 1, 2). Soh Hid ns) csancer 1. Which one of the following is the most appropriate initial management?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Respiratory - COPD (MCQ)": {
        "1": "An 82-year-old man presents to the General Practice clinic with a two-day history of odynophagia. He reports an itching and burning sensation in his mouth and noticed some blood when brushing his teeth yesterday. He has no dysphagia or retrosternal pain. He has COPD and uses a regular inhaled corticosteroid inhaler, which he does not rinse his mouth after using.\n\nOn examination of the oral cavity there are creamy-white plaques over the tongue, palate and buccal mucosa. These can be scraped off with a tongue depressor, revealing an erythematous, mildly bleeding base underneath. There is no glossitis, no angular cheilitis and no ulceration.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely underlying cause?",
        "6": "A 65-year-old woman is being reviewed on the ward one day after surgical repair of a fractured neck of femur. For her pain management, she was ordered 5-10 mg oral oxycodone hourly as required and has been receiving doses of 10 mg oxycodone. She was also prescribed regular oral paracetamol 1 g every six hours. The nursing team have asked for the patient to be reviewed as she appeared to be drowsier than normal with a sedation score of two, and previously assessed as having moderate-to-severe intensity pain with minimal functional limitation. Her medical history includes chronic obstructive pulmonary disease diagnosed five years ago. On review, the patient is sleeping on the bed. Her respiratory rate is 13 breaths/min. She looks drowsy but is easily roused. She answers all the questions appropriately, however she is unable to stay awake during the consultation. Which one of the following is the most appropriate management?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Respiratory - Pleural Disease (MCQ)": {
        "0": "A 33-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department with sudden-onset right-sided pleuritic chest pain and severe shortness of breath following an episode of coughing. He has no significant medical history and takes no regular medications. He is tall and thin.\n\nOn examination he is in respiratory distress with a respiratory rate of 32/min and SpO2 89% on room air. There is hyper-resonance to percussion and absent breath sounds over the entire right hemithorax. A chest X-ray shows a large right-sided pneumothorax with complete lung collapse and a visible visceral pleural edge, with a rim of more than 4 cm at the level of the hilum. The trachea remains central.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate management?",
        "3": "A 47-year-old man presents to the General Practice clinic with a two-month history of increasing breathlessness. He has no other cardiorespiratory symptoms. His symptoms have started to interfere with his job at a quarry, where he has worked for 20 years.\n\nOn examination there is dullness to percussion and reduced breath sounds at the right base. A chest X-ray shows a small right-sided pleural effusion blunting the costophrenic angle, with associated irregular pleural thickening along the right lateral chest wall. There is no obvious parenchymal mass and no mediastinal shift.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate next investigation?",
        "5": "54-year-old woman presents to the Emergency Department with a seven-day history of fever, productive cough, and left-sided pleuritic chest pain. She has a past medical history of rheumatoid arthritis, and is currently on methotrexate and prednisone, She does not take any other regular medications. On examination, she appears unwell with a pulse rate of 118/min, a blood pressure of 96/60 mmHg, respiratory rate of 28/min, $pO2 92% on room air, and temperature of 39.5 C, Chest examination reveals decreased air entry, increased vocal fremitus, and dullness to percussion at the left lung base. A chest X-ray shows a moderate left-sided pleural effusion with air-fluid levels. Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "6": "He was the restrained passenger in the front seat and is orientated to person, place and time. IV en route. The patient is in cervical spine precautions. There is no neck pain or neurological symptoms. There is bruising over the region of the seat belt in both the chest and lower abdomen, On auscultation of the chest there is equal air entry to both bases. Abdominal examination shows the previously mentioned seat belt bruising and palpation reveals mild diffuse tenderness, maximal in the lower abdomen, with no guarding. Which one of the following is the most appropriate initial investigation?",
        "8": "A 71-year-old woman presents to the General Practice clinic with a one-week history of right lower quadrant pain. She has a medical history of hypertension and osteoarthritis. She has no respiratory symptoms and is entirely well from a cardiorespiratory point of view.\n\nA CT scan performed to investigate the abdominal pain incidentally shows a defect in the posterolateral aspect of the left hemidiaphragm, through which a small amount of retroperitoneal fat has herniated into the thorax. There is no bowel within the hernia, no strangulation, no obstruction, and no pleural effusion or pneumothorax. She is asymptomatic from this finding.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate management of the incidental finding?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Respiratory - Interstitial Lung Disease (MCQ)": {
        "2": "A 54-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department with right upper quadrant pain. He has no significant medical history and takes no regular medications. On examination, he appears well with vital signs within normal limits. His abdomen is soft with mild right upper quadrant tenderness.\n\nA CT scan performed to investigate the abdominal pain incidentally includes the lung bases and lower thorax. It demonstrates symmetrical bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy, together with peripheral honeycombing, patchy ground-glass opacity and areas of traction bronchiectasis in the upper zones. There is no pleural effusion, no cavitation and no calcified lymph nodes.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "4": "A 23-year-old man presents to his oncologist for a follow-up appointment during treatment for a non-seminomatous germ cell tumour. He complains of insidious and progressive breathlessness on exertion. He has no other significant medical history, He is a non-smoker and currently does not drink alcohol. On examination, he appears mildly short of breath. His pulse rate is 78/min, blood pressure 125/76 mmbg, respiratory rate 19/min, SpO2 92% on room air, and temperature 37.4 C. Chest auscultation reveals bilateral basal inspiratory crackles. High-resolution CT of the chest shows interspersed areas with a ground-glass appearance in both lung bases. Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Cardiology - Acute Coronary Syndrome (MCQ)": {
        "1": "A 69-year-old man presents to hospital with dull chest pain that has persisted over the past 45 minutes. He has a marked bradycardia of 44 beats/min and a blood pressure of 84/62 mmHg.\n\nHis ECG shows ST-segment elevation in leads II, III and aVF with reciprocal ST depression in leads I and aVL, together with sinus bradycardia.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate immediate treatment?",
        "4": "A 58-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department at a small rural hospital with a one-hour history of central crushing chest pain radiating to his jaw, associated with nausea and vomiting. He has a medical history of hypertension.\n\nOn examination he is diaphoretic, with a pulse of 92/min, blood pressure 104/68 mmHg and SpO2 97% on room air. His ECG shows ST-segment elevation of 3 mm in leads V2 to V5. The nearest percutaneous coronary intervention centre is a three-hour transfer away.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate management?",
        "6": "A 15-year-old boy presents to the Emergency Department with a 12-hour history of central chest pain. He has no significant medical history and takes no regular medications. The pain varies slightly with respiration and posture, and is relieved by sitting forward. He had a viral illness two weeks ago.\n\nOn examination he is afebrile with normal vital signs. A scratchy, high-pitched rub is audible at the left sternal edge. His ECG shows widespread concave ST-segment elevation across the limb and precordial leads, with PR-segment depression, and PR elevation with ST depression in lead aVR. There is no reciprocal ST depression and no Q waves.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "17": "A 55-year-old woman presents to the Emergency Department with sudden onset of chest pain and shortness of breath following the unexpected death of her spouse. She describes the pain as 'crushing' and radiating to her left arm.\n\nOn examination her pulse is 104/min and blood pressure 108/70 mmHg. Her ECG shows ST-segment elevation across the anterior precordial leads. Troponin is elevated.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate immediate management?",
        "18": "A 25-year-old man presents with a 12-hour history of dull central chest pain. The pain does not radiate and there are no obvious alleviating or aggravating factors, or associated symptoms. He had a respiratory tract infection two weeks ago.\n\nHis ECG shows widespread concave-upward ST-segment elevation across most leads, with PR-segment depression, and ST depression with PR elevation in lead aVR. The changes do not conform to a single coronary artery territory, and there is no reciprocal ST depression, no Q waves, no peaked T waves and no delta wave.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "22": "A 54-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department with a one-hour history of severe, constant, tearing chest pain radiating to his neck and between the shoulder blades. He has a 12-year history of hypertension but takes his medication irregularly.\n\nOn examination there is a 25 mmHg difference in systolic blood pressure between his right and left arms. An early diastolic murmur is audible at the left sternal edge. His chest X-ray shows a widened mediastinum. He is haemodynamically stable with normal renal function.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate investigation?",
        "8": "A 62-year-old woman presents to the Emergency Department with three weeks of worsening breathlessness and peripheral oedema. She has no chest pain. She has severe chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) with an FEV1 of 35% predicted, diagnosed 15 years ago. She uses regular tiotropium and fluticasone/vilanterol inhaler. She continues to smoke 15 cigarettes per day and has had three hospital admissions in the past year for COPD exacerbations. On examination, she appears short of breath at rest. Her pulse rate is 92/min, blood pressure 135/85 mmHg, respiratory rate 24/min, SpO2 88% on room air, and temperature 36.5 C. Her jugular venous pressure is elevated at 8 cm. There is peripheral oedema to the mid-shin. Chest auscultation reveals reduced air entry bilaterally. Heart sounds are normal with a loud P2 Chest X-ray shows hyperinflated lung fields and prominent pulmonary arteries. ECG shows right axis deviation and right ventricular strain pattern. Blood tests reveal normal full blood count, electrolytes, and troponin. Which one of the following is the most likely cause of her deterioration?",
        "13": "A 70-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department with new-onset central crushing chest pain that came on suddenly while showering. He describes the pain as 7/10 in intensity, radiating to the jaw, with associated nausea and sweating.\n\nHis ECG shows ST-segment elevation in leads II, III and aVF with early Q-wave formation in the inferior leads, and reciprocal ST depression in leads I and aVL. Troponin is elevated.\n\nWhich one of the following scoring systems is most appropriate to risk-stratify this patient?",
        "23": "A 45-year-old woman presents to the Emergency Department with a three-day history of chest pain with worsening dyspnoea. She has no significant medical history and takes no regular medications. On examination, she appears unwell. Her pulse rate is 110/min, blood pressure 135/85 mmHg, respiratory rate 24/min, SpO2 92% on room air, and temperature 37.2 C. CT pulmonary angiography confirms the presence of a pulmonary embolus. She is commenced on intravenous unfractionated heparin, with resolution of symptoms in the following days. Five days later, she develops a fever associated with chest pain and dyspnoea. There is no clinically evident bleeding. Investigations reveal a platelet count of 50 x10 /L (150-450), a drop from a baseline of 350 x10 /L, Troponin is negative. Her electrocardiogram demonstrates sinus rhythm. She has normal renal function. Which one of the following is the most likely cause of her chest pain?",
        "24": "A 60-year-old man is brought into the Emergency Department of a rural hospital with a one hour history of severe central chest pain. He describes the pain as a tight pressure and it is making him sweat and gasp for air. His medical history include hypertension, for which he takes irbesartan, He has a 25 pack-year smoking history. On examination, his blood pressure is 150/90 mmHg and pulse 110/min. Oral aspirin, sublingual nitroglycerin and IV morphine is given for pain. The nearest PCI capable hospital is 2.5 hours away. | | Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?",
        "27": "A 60-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department with a one-hour history of central crushing chest pain, shortness of breath and sweating. He has a medical history of hypertension and hypercholesterolaemia, and is a current smoker.\n\nHis ECG shows ST-segment elevation of 3 mm in leads V2 to V5 with reciprocal changes inferiorly. Troponin is markedly elevated. Coronary angiography demonstrates an abrupt, complete occlusion of the proximal left anterior descending artery with a hazy, irregular filling defect at the site.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely underlying cause of his presentation?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Cardiology - Atrial Fibrillation (MCQ)": {
        "2": "A 66-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department with a nine-hour history of right-sided headache with dull neck pain on the same side. The pain is increasing in severity and he has never had a headache or neck pain like this before.\n\nOn examination there is partial drooping of the right upper eyelid and a constricted right pupil which reacts normally to light. The anisocoria is more marked in dim light. There is reduced sweating over the right side of the face. Extraocular movements are full and there is no diplopia. The remainder of the neurological examination is normal.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate investigation?",
        "9": "A 77-year-old man presents after an episode of right-sided clouding of vision four hours ago, lasting 20 minutes. His medical history includes myocardial infarction and insertion of a drug-eluting stent one year ago.\n\nFundoscopy of the right eye shows diffuse pallor and whitening of the retina, with a bright cherry-red spot at the macula. The retinal arterioles are attenuated with segmentation of the blood column. There are no scattered haemorrhages, no venous tortuosity and no retinal elevation.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate investigation?",
        "21": "A 15-year-old boy presents to the Emergency Department with a history of a syncopal event. He has no other symptoms. He has no significant medical history and does not take any regular medications. On examination he appears well with normal vital signs.\n\nHis ECG shows an rsR' pattern in leads V1-V2 with a QRS duration of 130 ms, a positive QRS in lead I with a negative QRS in leads II and aVF, and a PR interval of 240 ms.\n\nWhich one of the following best describes the ECG findings?",
        "4": "A 30-year-old woman, 15 weeks pregnant, presents to the Emergency Department with palpitations. She has no other symptoms and her pregnancy is progressing well without complications. She has no significant medical history and takes no regular medications.\n\nOn examination she is haemodynamically stable. Her ECG shows sinus rhythm in which every second beat is a broad, bizarre QRS complex with no preceding P wave, followed by a compensatory pause — so that normal and ectopic beats alternate one-for-one. The QRS duration of the conducted beats is normal, with no rsR' pattern and no broad notched R waves. The rhythm of the underlying sinus beats is regular.\n\nWhich one of the following best describes the ECG findings?",
        "12": "A 76-year-old man presents to the General Practice clinic with a one-week history of palpitations and shortness of breath on exertion. He has no significant medical history and takes no regular medications.\n\nOn examination, he appears well. His pulse is 128/min and irregularly irregular. His ECG shows an irregularly irregular narrow-complex rhythm with absent P waves and a chaotic, undulating baseline, confirming a new diagnosis of atrial fibrillation.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate next investigation?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Cardiology - Valvular Heart Disease (MCQ)": {
        "6": "A 39-year-old man presents to general practice with complaints of shortness of breath, chest pain, and palpitations during exercise. He has had previous vague chest discomfort on exertion. He has no medical history. His father died suddenly at the age of 42.\n\nOn examination there is a harsh ejection systolic murmur at the left sternal edge which becomes louder on standing and on Valsalva, and softer on squatting. His ECG shows voluminous, tall QRS complexes meeting voltage criteria for left ventricular hypertrophy, with deep, narrow Q waves in the inferolateral leads and widespread T-wave inversion.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate next investigation?",
        "12": "A 13-month-old boy presents to the General Practice clinic with his mother. She reports that he has had shortness of breath and difficulty feeding since birth, which have progressively worsened. The child's lips and fingertips appear dusky. He has Down syndrome.\n\nOn examination there is a hyperdynamic praecordium with a loud pansystolic murmur at the lower left sternal edge and a loud, single second heart sound. His ECG shows a superior QRS axis (left axis deviation) with first-degree heart block and biventricular hypertrophy. A chest X-ray shows cardiomegaly with increased pulmonary vascular markings. There is no boot-shaped heart, no egg-on-a-string appearance and no rib notching, and femoral pulses are strong and symmetrical.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "9": "A 22-year-old man presents to the General Practice clinic for a routine check-up. He has no significant medical history and takes no regular medications. On examination, he appears well. His pulse rate is 72/min, blood pressure 150/60 mmHg, respiratory rate 14/min, SpO2 98% on room air, and temperature 36.6 C. He is notably tall at 198 cm, with long, slender limbs and fingers. His arm span exceeds his height by 8 cm. He has mild scoliosis and a high-arched palate. Cardiovascular examination reveals a murmur. Which one of the following is the most likely murmur associated with this patient's condition?",
        "14": "A 73-year-old man presents with a 3-month history of chest pain and shortness of breath when exercising lifting weights or running on a treadmill. His history includes hypertension and gallstones. He is compliant with the verapamil for his blood pressure control. He is a non-smoker and drinks about four glasses of wine at weekends. His BMI is 26, On examination his blood pressure is 165/55 mmHg, pulse 85/min, temperature = 37.3 C and respiratory rate 17/min. There is a 4/6 crescendo-decrescendo murmur heard at the right upper sternal border with radiation to the carotid arteries, weak and delayed carotid pulses, and an S4 gallop. An ECG reveals sinus rhythm without arrhythmia. (normal 3.0-4.0), One year ago he had a coronary angiogram which showed a 20% lesion in the mid-left anterior descending (LAD) artery, a 30% lesion in the right coronary artery (RCA) and no significant stenosis in the left circumflex artery (LCx). Which one of the following is the most appropriate management strategy?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Cardiology - Cardiac Tamponade / Pericardial Disease (MCQ)": {
        "0": "A 55-year-old man is retrieved from the scene of a head-on motor vehicle crash in which he was the driver. Police estimate the collision occurred at approximately 60-70 km/h. On review, he is able to protect his airway and is haemodynamically stable.\n\nHis chest X-ray shows widening of the mediastinum to greater than 8 cm, with loss of definition of the aortic knuckle, depression of the left main bronchus and deviation of the nasogastric tube to the right. The lung fields are clear, with no pneumothorax, no apical cap and no pleural fluid. The cardiac silhouette is not globular.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - ENT - Neck Lump (MCQ)": {
        "0": "A 72-year-old man presents to his General Practitioner with a six-month history of a painless swelling in his neck. It has been slowly getting larger. He has no other health issues. He smokes 15 cigarettes per day and drinks heavily.\n\nOn examination there is a 3 cm hard, non-tender, fixed mass in the upper deep cervical chain at the level of the angle of the mandible. It does not move on swallowing or on tongue protrusion, does not transilluminate and is not fluctuant or pulsatile. There is no overlying skin change. Examination of the oral cavity is unremarkable.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate investigation?",
        "4": "A 56-year-old woman presents with a firm swelling under the left side of her jaw, which has been present for 10 weeks. The swelling is slowly getting larger, is not painful and does not change in size with meals.\n\nOn examination there is a 2.5 cm firm, non-tender, poorly mobile swelling in the left submandibular triangle. It lies superficial to and separate from the submandibular gland, which is itself normal on bimanual palpation. There is no expression of pus from the submandibular duct, no fluctuance, no overlying erythema and no punctum. It does not transilluminate.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "1": "A 35-year-old woman presents with a two-day history of swelling under her tongue. It is slightly uncomfortable, but she does not experience any increased pain when eating. She smokes one cigarette a day, has a glass of wine most nights, and is otherwise well with no regular medications.\n\nOn examination there is a soft, painless, fluctuant swelling on the floor of the mouth, lateral to the midline, with a smooth surface and a translucent bluish hue. It transilluminates. The overlying mucosa is intact, with no ulceration, no induration and no bleeding. The submandibular duct orifice is patent with clear saliva expressible, and the gland itself is not enlarged or tender. There is no dental caries and no cervical lymphadenopathy.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Genetics - Inheritance Patterns (MCQ)": {
        "2": "A 28-year-old woman presents with a one-month history of fatigue, joint pain and irritability. She also reports that she has become quite irritable lately for no clear reason. Prior to this she had been in good health.\n\nOn slit-lamp examination there is a golden-brown ring of pigment deposited at the periphery of the cornea, at the limbus, in both eyes. Liver function tests are deranged with a raised bilirubin and transaminases. Serum caeruloplasmin is low and 24-hour urinary copper excretion is markedly elevated. Serum ferritin and transferrin saturation are normal.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate treatment?",
        "1": "A two-year-old boy presents to the General Practice clinic with his parent due to multiple changing skin lesions on his face and neck. His parent reports he develops severe sunburn even with minimal sun exposure. He did not have any similar issues before the age of 6 months. He has a history of mild developmental delay but no other medical conditions and takes no regular medications. His parent mentions that his older sister had similar symptoms and died from metastatic melanoma at age eight. His parents migrated from Japan last year. On examination, he appears well with vital signs within normal limits. There are multiple hyperpigmented macules, areas of skin atrophy and telangiectasia. His eyes appear irritated and photophobic with sunglasses on inside the room. Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Haematology - Bleeding / Coagulopathy (MCQ)": {
        "6": "A 12-year-old girl is brought by her parent to see her general practitioner as she suffers from recurrent nosebleeds. This has been recurring over the past few years, however they self-resolve without any hospitalisation required. She has recently started menstruating and reports very heavy periods. She bruises easily. There is no history of haemarthrosis or deep muscle bleeding. Her mother has similar symptoms.\n\nInvestigations show a normal platelet count, a normal prothrombin time, and a prolonged activated partial thromboplastin time. Bleeding time is prolonged.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "12": "A 35-year-old woman presents to the Emergency Department with a three-month history of increasing fatigue and generalised weakness, associated with a fever over the last week. She has No significant medical history and does not take any regular medications. On examination, she appears unwell. Her pulse rate is 95/min, blood pressure 112/74 mmHg, respiratory rate 15/min, SpO2 98% on room air, and temperature 38 C. Examination of the oral cavity shows multiple petechiae. Her liver edge is palpable, and her spleen can be felt 3. cm below the costal margin Investigations reveal: * Haemoglobin 90 g/L (115-165) + White cell count 30 x 109/L (4.0-11.0) * Platelet count 39 x 10*9/L (150-450) Blood film shows numerous promyelocytes with heavy granulation and multiple Auer rods. Bone marrow karyotype showed translocation between chromosomes 15 and 17. Which one of the following complications is this patient most at risk of developing?",
        "16": "A 38-year-old woman presents to the Emergency Department with a one-week history of easy bruising, fatigue, and confusion. She has no significant medical history and takes no regular medications. On examination, she appears unwell. Her pulse rate is 110/min, blood pressure 145/90 mmHg, respiratory rate 18/min, SpO2 98% on room air, and temperature 37.2 C. She is pale and lethargic with scattered petechiae and ecchymoses on her arms and legs. Neurological examination reveals mild disorientation and confusion. Investigations reveal: + Haemoglobin: 110 g/L (125-160) * Platelet count: 120 x109/L (150-450) + LDH: 320 U/L (105-230) * Peripheral blood smear: Schistocytes present * Serum creatinine: 180 mol/L (50-120) + ADAMTSI3 activity: <10% (>50) Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Haematology - Lymphoma (MCQ)": {
        "1": "A 58-year-old man presents with a painless swelling on the left side of his face which has been present for four months. Recently it has been increasing in size. His medical history includes pulmonary tuberculosis, treated five years ago.\n\nOn examination there is a 3 cm firm, smooth, mobile, non-tender swelling situated in front of and below the left ear, overlying the angle of the mandible and lifting the earlobe. The facial nerve is intact, the overlying skin is normal, and there is no cervical lymphadenopathy and no fluctuance.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Infectious Disease - Sexually Transmitted Infection (MCQ)": {
        "9": "A 32-year-old man presents to his general practitioner with a three-week history of malaise. He has been feeling fatigued throughout the day. Over the last week he has noticed new rashes on his feet and palms. He is sexually active with multiple male partners and does not consistently use condoms.\n\nOn examination there is a symmetrical, non-itchy, coppery-red maculopapular rash involving the palms and the soles, with fine scale. There is generalised non-tender lymphadenopathy. He recalls a painless genital ulcer that healed spontaneously about two months ago. There are no vesicles, no target lesions and no mucosal sloughing.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate investigation?",
        "25": "A 28-year-old man presents to the General Practice clinic with a three-week history of painless growths in his genital area. He is otherwise well. He has no significant medical history and does not take any regular medications.\n\nOn examination there are multiple soft, fleshy, filiform and cauliflower-like verrucous papules clustered around the shaft of the penis and perianal skin. They are skin-coloured to pink, pedunculated, and have a rough papilliform surface. They are not moist, flat-topped or broad-based, there is no ulceration, no vesicles, and no surrounding induration.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "6": "A 20-year-old Aboriginal woman from a remote community in the Northern Territory presents to the General Practice clinic with a three-week history of a non-itchy rash. She also reports fatigue, mild fever and generalised aches. She has no significant medical history and takes no regular medications. She reports unprotected sexual intercourse with more than one partner in the past six months.\n\nOn examination she appears well. Her pulse rate is 82/min, blood pressure 125/75 mmHg, respiratory rate 16/min, SpO2 98% on room air, and temperature 37.8°C. There is a symmetrical, copper-coloured maculopapular rash on her trunk and extremities, including the palms and soles. There are several painless, enlarged lymph nodes in her neck and groin. Condylomata lata are noted around the anus. Blood and urine tests are being arranged.\n\nA positive result in which one of the following investigations is most likely to influence management of this patient's underlying condition?",
        "19": "An 18-year-old Indigenous woman presents to the Emergency Department with a 24-hour history of increasing right iliac fossa pain. She has anorexia and has vomited once. Her last menstrual period was four weeks ago and she has had regular intercourse since then with several partners. She uses condoms occasionally and is not taking any hormonal contraception. On examination, she appears flushed and unwell. Her temperature is 37.8 C, blood pressure 110/70 mmHg, pulse rate 105/min, respiratory rate 18/min, and SpO2 98% on room air. She has tenderness in the right iliac fossa with localised peritonism. Speculum examination shows a copious purulent discharge. Serum beta-hCG is negative. Which one of the following is the most appropriate next diagnostic step?",
        "23": "A 42-year-old man presents to his General Practice clinic with a one-month history of non-pruritic, non-painful skin lesions over his face and many other parts of his body. He is otherwise well. He is HIV positive with a low CD4 count.\n\nOn examination there are numerous discrete, dome-shaped, pearly, skin-coloured papules 3-6 mm across, many with a central umbilication from which a cheesy material can be expressed. They are scattered across the face, trunk and limbs. There are no burrows in the finger webs, no fleshy verrucous growths, no coppery-red palmar rash, and no firm white keratin-filled cysts.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "26": "A 35-year-old woman presents to the General Practice clinic for her cervical screening test (CST) results. She is currently using the Mirena intra-uterine device for contraception with no complications. She has been sexually active with three casual partners over the past two months and used condoms irregularly. Her last cervical screening test was done five years ago with no abnormal results. Her past medical history includes a chlamydia infection four years ago which was adequately treated. A routine cervical screening test was performed a week ago (clinician collected at patient's request) which shows the following results: Oncogenic HPV and genotype: * HPV 16/18: not detected * HPV (non 16/18): detected Liquid-based cytology (LBC) result: * Possible low-grade squamous intraepithelial lesion (LSIL) + Endocervical component: identified Which one of the following is the recommended next step in management?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Infectious Disease - Tropical / Zoonotic (MCQ)": {
        "0": "A 56-year-old woman presents to the General Practice clinic with a single episode of upper abdominal pain. The pain came on suddenly, lasted about 30 minutes, and then settled. She has no significant medical history. She grew up on a sheep farm and has several working dogs.\n\nAn abdominal ultrasound shows a large, well-defined cystic lesion occupying much of the right lobe of the liver. The lesion has a thick, calcified wall, and multiple smaller daughter cysts are visible within the parent cyst, separated by internal septations. The remainder of the liver parenchyma is normal, and there are no other lesions and no biliary dilatation.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "5": "A 55-year-old man from Turkey presents with a one-month history of right upper quadrant pain and lethargy. The pain is associated with nausea and vomiting. He does not drink alcohol. He grew up on a sheep farm and kept working dogs.\n\nA CT scan shows a 10 cm compartmentalised cystic lesion in the right lobe of the liver, containing multiple smaller daughter cysts separated by internal septations, with a thick, partly calcified wall. There is no peripherally enhancing rim of an abscess, no gas within the lesion, and the remainder of the liver is normal.\n\nInfection with which one of the following organisms is the most likely cause?",
        "11": "A 42-year-old man presents to the General Practice clinic concerned about his health after the death of his horse. He lives on a rural property in southeast Queensland with fruit trees that attract flying foxes. His horse developed acute onset gastrointestinal disturbance (\"colic\") and ataxia two days ago. The horse had not been vaccinated against any diseases in the past five years. The man reports that he administered medication via nasogastric tube to treat the colic and was in close contact with the horse's nasal and oral secretions during this process. Despite his efforts, the horse died 12 hours later. The man has no significant medical history and does not take any regular medications. He appears well with vital signs within normal limits. Physical examination reveals no abnormalities. Which one of the following diseases is this patient most at risk of developing?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Musculoskeletal - Vasculitis / Autoimmune (MCQ)": {
        "14": "A 32-year-old woman presents to the Emergency Department with weakness and tingling in her extremities for the past several days. She has had multiple episodes of nephrolithiasis in the past. She also reports dryness of the eyes and mouth.\n\nInvestigations show a serum potassium of 2.8 mmol/L (3.5-5.0), a low serum bicarbonate, an elevated serum chloride, and a normal anion gap. Urine pH is inappropriately alkaline at 6.5 despite the systemic acidosis.\n\nWhich one of the following sets of arterial blood gas results would be most consistent with her condition?",
        "16": "A 52-year-old woman presents to the General Practice clinic with a six-month history of dysphagia to solids and fluids. She has lost 4 kg in weight over this time. She has no significant medical history.\n\nOn examination the skin of the fingers is thickened and tight, restricting flexion, and the fingertips are tapered with several small pitted scars. There are multiple small dilated blood vessels visible on the face and hands. She reports that her fingers turn white, then blue, then red on exposure to cold. There is no proximal muscle weakness, no heliotrope rash and no skin thickening proximal to the elbows.\n\nWhich one of the following antibodies is most likely to be positive?",
        "9": "A 17-year-old woman presents to the General Practice clinic for evaluation of widespread pain and fatigue which has been troubling her over the last four years. The pain affects multiple joints including the knees and shoulders, and worsens as the day progresses. She has dislocated her right shoulder on two occasions, and these episodes were managed conservatively.\n\nOn examination she is able to passively flex her thumb so far that it lies flush along the flexor aspect of her forearm. Both elbows and knees hyperextend beyond 10 degrees, and she can place her palms flat on the floor with knees straight. Her skin is soft and velvety and can be stretched well away from the underlying tissue, and there are widened atrophic scars over both knees. There is no joint swelling, no synovitis, no rash and no muscle weakness. Inflammatory markers are normal.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate management?",
        "11": "A 28-year-old woman presents to the Emergency Department with sudden onset chest pain radiating to her back and shortness of breath at 32 weeks gestation. She has a family history of her mother dying suddenly during pregnancy at age 30. She has no significant medical history and takes only pregnancy vitamins. She works as an accountant. On examination, she appears distressed. Her pulse rate is 120/min, blood pressure 85/60 mmHg, respiratory rate 24/min, SpO2 94% on room air, and temperature 36.5 C. She has thin, translucent skin with visible veins. Her joints are hypermobile. Heart sounds are normal with no murmurs. The fetal heart rate is 160/min. Which one of the following Is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "17": "A 28-year-old woman presents to her rheumatologist for routine review. She has a medical history of systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) and takes hydroxychloroquine and mycophenolate 1000 mg twice daily for maintenance immunosuppression. She does not take any other regular medications, Her SLE has been well-controlled on this regimen for the past three years. She and her partner have decided they would like to try for a baby in the coming months. On examination, she appears well with vital signs within normal limits. There are no examination signs of active SLE. Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?",
        "18": "A 72-year-old woman presents to a small rural hospital with a 12-hour history of throbbing headache, worse on the left side. The pain has not responded to paracetamol or ibuprofen. She denies visual disturbances, photophobia, phonophobia, nausea, vomiting, or neurological symptoms. She reports shoulder joint ache, stiffness, and fatigue for several weeks. Her medical history includes hypertension, for which she takes atenolol. On examination, she appears uncomfortable. Her pulse rate is 90/min, blood pressure 150/70 mmHg, respiratory rate 16/min, and temperature 36.8 C. Neurological examination is unremarkable with equal and reactive pupils bilaterally. There is mild tenderness on palpation over her scalp, particularly in the temporal region. Which one of the following is the most appropriate initial management?",
        "20": "A 50-year-old man presents with a two day history of fever, chills and acute pain and swelling in his right knee. At the same time he noticed a rash comprising several small pustular lesions on his ankles. He does not think he has injured his knee. His medical history includes type 2 diabetes for which he takes metformin. He has poor compliance, with an HbAIC of 13% (3.5-6) recorded two months ago. On examination his blood pressure is 134/78 mmHg, pulse 72/min, temperature of 38.2 C, respiratory rate 18/min and SpO2 98% on room air. His right knee is warm and swollen joint, with severe limitation to passive and active range of movement, and a negative patellar tap. The rest of the examination is unremarkable Which one of the following initial investigations would be most appropriate?",
        "24": "An 65-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department with sudden and severe tearing chest pain that radiates to the scapula. The following tests are requested: renal function tests, D-dimer, ECG, CXR and transthoracic echocardiogram (TTE). A diagnosis of descending aortic dissection (Stanford type B) is made based on elevated D-dimer, widened mediastinum on a chest X-ray and dissection flap on the transoesophageal echocardiogram. There are no signs of end-organ malperfusion with normal kidney function. A CT angiogram is awaited. Which one of the following is the next best step in management?",
        "25": "A 21-year-old woman presents to the Emergency Department with painful ulcers on her trunk and extremities that have developed over two weeks. She was recently diagnosed with acute myeloid leukaemia, for which she has completed one cycle of chemotherapy. She takes no other regular medications. She appears unwell with a pulse rate of 110/min, blood pressure 115/70 mmHg, respiratory rate 20/min, SpO2 98% on room air, and temperature 38.2 C. On examination, there are multiple tender, punched-out ulcers with raised erythematous borders distributed across her trunk and limbs. Some lesions show central necrosis. Which one of the following is the most appropriate next investigation?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Musculoskeletal - Gout / Pseudogout (MCQ)": {
        "17": "A 70-year-old woman presents to the General Practice clinic with painless swelling of multiple finger joints that has gradually worsened over the past year. The swelling now interferes with her activities of daily living. She has chronic kidney disease and takes a thiazide diuretic.\n\nOn examination there are firm, irregular, nodular swellings over the distal and proximal interphalangeal joints of several fingers and over the olecranon. Several have a chalky, white-yellow material visible beneath thinned overlying skin, and one is discharging a white pasty substance. The joints are not warm, not tender and not symmetrically inflamed. There is no ulnar deviation, no nail pitting and no sclerodactyly.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "16": "A 54-year-old man presents to the General Practice clinic with immense pain in his hands and wrist which started a couple of hours ago. He complains of similar episodic pains for many years, initially unilateral but now affecting multiple joints. He has chronic kidney disease.\n\nOn examination the affected joints are hot, swollen and exquisitely tender. There are multiple firm, irregular, white-yellow nodular deposits over the small joints of the hands and over the olecranon, several of which are visible through thinned overlying skin. Similar chalky white deposits are present in the helix of the external ear. He is afebrile, and joint aspirate shows negatively birefringent needle-shaped crystals with no organisms on Gram stain.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate initial treatment?",
        "15": "A 45-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department with a two-day history of increasing pain and swelling in his left knee. He can no longer weight-bear. He had a cycle race five days ago and does not remember hurting his knee. He has no significant medical history and takes no regular medications. He consumes two glasses of wine a day. On examination, he appears unwell. His pulse rate is 110/min, blood pressure 105/65 mmHg, respiratory rate 20/min, SpO2 98% on room air, and temperature 38.5 C. There is a large, tender and warm effusion around the left knee with a limited range of movement. There are no other abnormal findings. A plain X-ray of the knee shows the swelling, with chondrocalcinosis, and no fracture. Synovial fluid aspiration shows: * Leucocytes: 110,000/mm? (98% neutrophils) * Occasional intracellular rhomboid-shaped crystals, showing some birefringence * Gram stain: ++ (gram-positive cocci in clusters) Which one of the following is the definitive management for this condition?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Musculoskeletal - Septic Arthritis / Osteomyelitis (MCQ)": {
        "3": "A 14-year-old boy is brought to the clinic by his parents because he has been limping for the past two weeks and is complaining of pain in his left hip. There is no history of trauma or fever and he is otherwise well. His weight is on the 90th percentile.\n\nOn examination the left leg is held in external rotation, and there is obligatory external rotation of the hip on passive flexion. A frog-leg lateral X-ray of the pelvis shows the femoral epiphysis displaced posteriorly and inferiorly relative to the femoral neck, with a line drawn along the superior femoral neck failing to intersect the epiphysis. The joint space is preserved and the femoral head is not sclerotic, fragmented or flattened.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "6": "A 52-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department with three weeks of worsening right foot pain and a non-healing ulcer. He has a medical history of type 2 diabetes mellitus with peripheral neuropathy and takes metformin. He first noticed the ulcer on the plantar surface of his right foot two months ago after walking barefoot in his garden. On examination, he appears unwell. His pulse rate is 95/min, blood pressure 125/75 mmHg, respiratory rate 18/min, SpO2 98% on room air, and temperature 38.2 C. His right foot is warm, tender and erythematous with a 2 cm deep ulcer on the plantar surface. Peripheral pulses are palpable. Blood tests reveal a white cell count of 14.2 x 10*9/L (4.0-11.0) and C-reactive protein 156 mg/L (<5). He was initially started on intravenous amoxicillin-clavulanate as empiric therapy. Subsequently, magnetic resonance imaging shows changes consistent with osteomyelitis of the underlying metatarsal. Blood cultures grew gram-positive cocci in clusters, which, on further testing, are found to be methicillin-sensitive. Which one of the following is the most appropriate antibiotic regimen?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Musculoskeletal - Fracture - Upper Limb (MCQ)": {
        "3": "An otherwise well 57-year-old man presents with a painless swelling on the thenar eminence of his right hand. This has been present for three weeks and has grown very rapidly over that time.\n\nOn examination there is a firm, dome-shaped, flesh-coloured nodule approximately 15 mm across, with a rolled, smooth border and a central crater filled with a plug of keratin. It has grown to this size over only three weeks. There is no pigmentation, no bleeding, no surrounding erythema and no puncture mark or necrotic centre.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "4": "A 5-year-old boy is brought to the Emergency Department by his parents because of localised left elbow swelling and pain (8/10). His parents mention that about one hour ago he fell from his bike, landed on an outstretched arm and has not been able to move his elbow since, On examination his range of motion at the elbow is limited by pain throughout, with obvious bruising, swelling, and tenderness on palpation of the elbow. 2 au His. iti. Which one of the following findings would be most common in this type of injury?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Neurology - Seizures / Epilepsy (MCQ)": {
        "7": "A 38-year-old woman presents to the Emergency Department with a two-day history of left-sided facial weakness. She has no significant medical history and takes no regular medications.\n\nOn examination there is weakness of the lower half of the left side of the face, with flattening of the left nasolabial fold and drooping of the left corner of the mouth. She is able to raise both eyebrows symmetrically and can close both eyes fully and forcefully against resistance. There is mild weakness of the left arm. Taste and hearing are normal.\n\nA lesion in which one of the following locations best explains these findings?",
        "8": "A 63-year-old woman presents with a four-month history of painless red lesions gradually spreading over her left arm. Her arm has been chronically swollen since a left total (radical) mastectomy followed by radiotherapy 20 years ago.\n\nOn examination the left arm is grossly oedematous with non-pitting, thickened skin. Over the medial aspect there are multiple raised, violaceous, vascular-appearing nodules and plaques, several of which bleed on light contact. They are coalescing into larger plaques. There is no scaling, no telangiectatic atrophy of the irradiated field alone, and no pigmented asymmetric macule.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "18": "A 20-year-old man presents with painful blisters around and on his lips, and a dusky rash across small areas of his limbs. He reports 5-7 days of feeling generally lethargic and unwell. He was started on a new medication three weeks ago.\n\nOn examination there is haemorrhagic crusting and erosion of the vermillion of the lips, with erosions of the buccal mucosa and conjunctival injection. On the limbs there are dusky, atypical target-like macules with central blistering. Gentle lateral pressure on the skin causes the epidermis to shear away. Less than 10% of the body surface area is detached. The blisters are flaccid rather than tense, and he is systemically unwell and febrile.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "11": "Over the last two years the son has noticed substantial memory impairment in his mother. She often forgets to turn the stove off and cannot always remember what day of the week it is. Yesterday he found her wandering in the street in her nightgown. Her medical history includes asthma, for which she uses an inhaler. She is not on any other medications; does not smoke and drinks only a glass or two of wine at weekends. On examination her blood pressure is 132/84 mmHg, pulse 76/min and temperature 37.2 C. The physical examination, including a neurological and cardiovascular assessment, is unremarkable. She scores 18 out of 30 on the Folstein Mini-Mental Status Examination. Which one of the following is the most appropriate plan of management?",
        "23": "A 28-year-old woman presents to the General Practice clinic with her three-year-old daughter, She is new to the practice, having recently moved to the area. She reports her daughter has had eight episodes of seizures in the past two months, characterised by eye rolling, limb stiffening, and post-ictal drowsiness lasting 30 minutes. Each episode occurred when only the mother was present. The child has been seen at three different general practices and two emergency departments during this period. Previous investigations including electroencephalogram, magnetic resonance imaging of the brain, metabolic studies, and toxicology screens have been normal. The mother is a registered nurse in a paediatric intensive care unit and provides detailed medical descriptions of the episodes, including precise timing and medication doses administered. She appears overly concerned and requests additional tests, specifically genetic testing for rare epilepsy syndromes. The child has no significant medical history and takes no regular medications. Development is age- appropriate. On examination, she appears well with vital signs within normal limits. Neurological examination is normal. Review of previous medical records reveals multiple presentations for various other symptoms including unexplained bleeding, respiratory distress, and fevers, all of which resolved spontaneously. Which one of the following is the most likely underlying cause of the presentations?",
        "27": "A 51-year-old man presents to the Neurosurgery clinic for consideration of surgery for drug- resistant epilepsy. He has a five-year history of focal seizures that have not responded to three different anti-epileptic medications. He has no other significant medical history. His current medications are levetiracetam, carbamazepine and lamotrigine. On examination, he appears well with vital signs within normal limits. Neurological examination is normal. After review of his imaging and electroencephalogram findings, he is offered anterior temporal lobectomy. Which one of the following visual field deficits is most likely to occur following this procedure?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Neurology - Dementia (MCQ)": {
        "11": "An 80-year-old woman presents to the Emergency Department with a one-hour history of shortness of breath, sweating, and nausea. She has a medical history of Alzheimer's dementia, hypertension, hyperlipidaemia, and type 2 diabetes. She denies chest pain.\n\nOn examination her pulse is 96/min and blood pressure 142/88 mmHg. Her ECG shows ST-segment elevation in leads II, III and aVF and in leads V5 and V6, with ST depression in leads V1 to V3 consistent with posterior extension. Her chest is clear and she is not hypotensive.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate initial treatment?",
        "6": "A 75-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department with a two-day history of increasingly aggressive behaviour. He was admitted to the brain injury rehabilitation ward five weeks ago following evacuation of a traumatic subdural haematoma. He had shown a pleasant demeanour until two days ago. He has a medical history of type 2 diabetes mellitus. His only regular medication is metformin. On examination, he appears agitated. His pulse rate is 90/min, blood pressure 134/82 mmHg, respiratory rate 18/min, SpO2 98% on room air, and temperature 37.0 C. His Glasgow Coma Scale score is 15 with impaired attention and worsening cognition. There is generalised hyperreflexia and hypertonia. Urine culture is negative. Complete blood examination and chest X-ray have been arranged. Which one of the following is the most likely explanation for his current behaviour?",
        "8": "A 76-year-old woman presents to her General Practice clinic with her daughter, who has recently become concerned about her mother's ability to cope at home. She reveals that over the last two months, her mother's memory has been \"poor\", often forgetting to shower in the morning or pay her bills on time. On further history, little more information is obtained from the patient herself, as she is withdrawn and quiet. She is unable to recall what she had for breakfast and cannot state the day of the week or month. She is unable to recount any childhood memories, however she is able to talk about the early years of her marriage to her late husband, who died six months ago. Her medical history is notable for hypertension, high cholesterol, osteoporosis, and urinary stress incontinence. Her medications include captopril, simvastatin, and denosumab. On examination, her blood pressure is 152/94 mmHg, pulse 80/min, and temperature 37.2 C. Her facial expression is depressed-looking and poorly responsive, and her movements are slow but not hesitant. Reflexes are all normal, with an absence of primitive reflexes. The mini-mental state examination reveals a score of 18/30, with the patient frequently becoming frustrated and teary during the test. She has a slight tremor of her right hand that only becomes apparent when she writes. Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "16": "A 58-year-old male is brought to the clinic by his spouse with a complaint of significant changes in behaviour and personality over the past 1 year. His spouse reports that he has gradually become more isolated, avoiding social gatherings and even declining invitations to parties he once enjoyed, he has started to neglect showering and grooming, which is a noticeable departure from his previous habits. He is also now unable to solve crosswords, which was one of his favourite hobbies. He frequently forgets important dates and events, which was never a problem before. His spouse also mentions that he used to be quite talkative and articulate, but lately, he has been struggling with finding the right words and often uses vague language. His father had Parkinson's disease, He has a history of smoking 2-pack-year and moderate alcohol consumption. He has hypertension, which is well-controlled with perindopril. On physical examination, his overall demeanour lacks its previous engagement, and he displays subtle yet observable limitations in expressing emotions. He has mild bilateral rigidity but no resting tremors. His muscle strength and reflexes are normal. Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "17": "A 32-year-old man with Down syndrome presents to his General Practitioner with his regular carer. His carer states that the patient has become increasingly agitated in the last two days, and has been becoming violent towards himself and others at the care facility in which he resides. His carer is concerned about the behaviours, although states that he is able to be calmed and redirected easily. The patient is verbal, and able to engage well in conversation. Previous neuropsychological assessment determined (average: 90 - 110), When questioned further, he reports no localising symptoms. His medical history includes myopia, for which he wears corrective spectacles, and a patent ductus arteriosus which was appropriately managed around the time of his birth, On examination, his pulse rate is 85/min, respiratory rate 14/min, blood pressure 152/90mmHg, temperature 38.2 C and SpO 99% on room air. His BMI is 32. He is appropriate and engages well with the doctor whilst being examined. He has poor dentition. His cardiovascular, respiratory and abdominal examinations are unremarkable. Which one of the following should be the priority for preventative health activities to engage in?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Oncology - Breast Cancer (MCQ)": {
        "0": "A 56-year-old woman presents to the General Practice clinic with a three-week history of progressive skin discolouration and swelling of her right breast. There has been warmth and tenderness but no discharge.\n\nOn examination the right breast is diffusely enlarged, firm, warm and erythematous over more than one third of its surface. The skin is thickened and pitted, with prominent, dimpled hair follicles giving an orange-peel texture. There is no discrete palpable lump, no fluctuance and no abscess. She is afebrile, and a course of antibiotics has produced no improvement.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate investigation?",
        "4": "A 52-year-old woman presents to the General Practice clinic with a palpable lump in her left breast. She noticed reddish marks of nipple discharge on her brassiere today. Her last mammogram was performed two years ago.\n\nOn examination there is a 3 cm firm, irregular mass in the upper outer quadrant. The skin over the affected area of the breast is thickened and oedematous, with exaggerated, dimpled hair follicles producing a pitted, orange-peel appearance. There is no ulceration, and the mass moves freely over pectoralis major on contraction.\n\nWhich one of the following physical findings is most useful for staging?",
        "6": "An otherwise well 40-year-old woman presents with a one-month history of a painless lump in her left breast. This first came to her attention after she tripped and fell onto her breast whilst carrying groceries.\n\nOn examination there is a firm, irregular, poorly mobile mass in the infero-lateral quadrant of the left breast. The overlying skin is drawn inwards, with visible tethering and puckering over the mass, which becomes more pronounced when she raises her arms. There is no bruising, no fluctuance, no nipple discharge, and no smooth, highly mobile discrete lump.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "11": "A 67-year-old woman presents to the Emergency Department with a two-day history of increasing pain, swelling, and redness of her right breast. She reports feeling feverish but has not measured her temperature. Earlier in the week she sustained a superficial skin abrasion over the breast.\n\nOn examination she is febrile at 38.4°C. There is a warm, tender, sharply demarcated area of erythema over the lower half of the right breast, with a small crusted abrasion at its centre. The skin is not thickened or pitted, there is no peau d'orange, no discrete underlying mass, no nipple change and no fluctuance. She is not lactating.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "14": "A 58-year-old woman presents to the General Practice clinic with a two-week history of painful and reddened swelling of her right breast. She has no significant medical history and takes no regular medications. She has already completed two courses of oral antibiotics with no improvement.\n\nOn examination the right breast is diffusely swollen, firm and erythematous, with thickened, pitted skin having an orange-peel texture over the lower half. She is afebrile, and there is no fluctuant collection to drain. There is palpable axillary lymphadenopathy.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate next step?",
        "3": "A 52-year-old woman presents with a two-week history of increasing breathlessness. She also reports a chest wall lesion that she has been too embarrassed to seek advice on for the last year.\n\nOn examination of the right chest wall there is a large, fungating, ulcerated mass replacing much of the breast, with raised, everted, necrotic edges and an offensive discharge. The surrounding skin is thickened and nodular, with satellite skin deposits extending across the chest wall, and the mass is fixed to the underlying chest wall. There is dullness to percussion at the right base with reduced breath sounds, and shifting dullness on abdominal examination. There is fixed, matted axillary lymphadenopathy.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate plan of management?",
        "13": "A 28-year-old woman presents to the Emergency Department with a three-day history of right breast pain and redness. She gave birth to her first child four weeks ago and has been breastfeeding. She has been having difficulty with latch and has been using a pump to assist with feeding. She has not seen a lactation consultant. She has no significant medical history and does not take any regular medications. On examination, she appears unwell. Her pulse rate is 100/min, blood pressure 120/80 mmHg, respiratory rate 18/min, SpO2 98% on room air, and temperature 38.5 C. The right breast is tender, warm, and erythematous, particularly in the upper outer quadrant. There fluctuant area felt which is exquisitely tender. Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "19": "A 52-year-old transgender woman presents to the General Practice clinic for advice about breast cancer screening. She has been taking oestrogen therapy for six years as part of her gender- affirming care. She has no significant medical history and takes no other regular medications. She has no family history of breast cancer. On examination, she appears well with vital signs within normal limits. Which one of the following is the most appropriate breast cancer screening recommendation?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Ophthalmology - Glaucoma (MCQ)": {
        "7": "A 58-year-old man presents with a six-hour history of right eye pain, blurred vision and nausea. He is hyperopic and has no medical history. He had a similar episode two months ago with spontaneous resolution of symptoms. He reports seeing haloes around lights.\n\nOn examination the right eye is red with circumcorneal injection. The cornea is hazy and oedematous. The pupil is mid-dilated, oval and non-reactive to light. The globe feels hard on palpation, and intraocular pressure is 52 mmHg. The anterior chamber is shallow. There is no fluorescein-staining epithelial defect and no purulent discharge.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate immediate management?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Ophthalmology - Red Eye (MCQ)": {
        "10": "A 56-year-old man presents to the General Practice clinic with a five-day history of pain and swelling around his right eye. He has a medical history of type 2 diabetes and hypertension. He takes metformin and amlodipine.\n\nOn examination there is a tender, erythematous, fluctuant swelling localised to the inferomedial aspect of the right orbit, below the medial canthus, overlying the lacrimal sac. Pressure over the swelling expresses purulent material from the lower punctum, and there is epiphora. Eye movements are full and painless, there is no proptosis, and visual acuity is normal. The swelling is not centred on the eyelid margin or the tarsal plate.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "12": "A 40-year-old woman presents with a one-week history of pain and redness in her right eye. The pain is severe, penetrating, radiating to the jaw and is associated with photophobia and lacrimation. She wakes at night with the pain. Her medical history includes rheumatoid arthritis.\n\nOn examination the right eye shows a deep, violaceous-blue hue to the injected vessels, which are large, tortuous and do NOT blanch after instillation of topical phenylephrine. The globe is tender to palpation. There is no discharge, no fluorescein uptake, the cornea is clear, the pupil is normal and reactive, and the anterior chamber is deep and quiet.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Ophthalmology - Macular Degeneration (MCQ)": {
        "0": "A 73-year-old woman presents to the General Practice clinic with 12 months of deteriorating vision. She has a medical history of type 2 diabetes mellitus managed with glimepiride and hypertension managed with ramipril. She describes difficulty reading and that straight lines appear wavy, with a blurred patch in the centre of her vision. Her peripheral vision is preserved.\n\nFundoscopy shows multiple small, discrete, pale-yellow deposits scattered beneath the retina at the macula, with associated pigmentary mottling of the retinal pigment epithelium. There are no microaneurysms, no dot-blot haemorrhages, no hard exudates and no neovascularisation. The optic disc is of normal colour with a normal cup, and the retinal arterioles are of normal calibre.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "2": "A 63-year-old man presents with a four month history of deteriorating vision. Fifteen years ago he was started on glibenclamide for type 2 diabetes and he takes perindopril for hypertension. He has a 30 pack-year smoking history and drinks four standard drinks a day. His BMI is 35. The rest of the physical examination is normal. Fundoscopy is performed. Which one of the following is the most likely explanation for his visual changes?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Psychiatry - Depression (MCQ)": {
        "18": "A 65-year-old woman presents to her general practitioner with a three-month history of painless swelling of her left arm. She does not have any current health issues, but had a left mastectomy and axillary clearance followed by radiotherapy three years ago.\n\nOn examination the entire left arm is diffusely swollen from the axilla to the hand, including the dorsum of the hand and the base of the fingers. The swelling is non-pitting, the skin is thickened, and it is not possible to pinch a fold of skin at the base of the second toe-equivalent on the hand. The limb is not tender, not erythematous and not warm, and there is no palpable cord.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate investigation?",
        "15": "A 30-year-old woman presents to the Emergency Department with her husband following ingestion of 24 paracetamol tablets. She lives on a remote cattle station in South Australia. She has a history of major depressive disorder and incomplete suicide by overdose two years ago. She takes sertraline and sees a psychologist monthly via telehealth. She works as a primary school teacher and has two children aged four and six years. Her husband reports she has been more withdrawn over the past month following a recent miscarriage. On examination, she appears withdrawn with minimal eye contact. Her pulse rate is 88/min, blood pressure 125/75 mmHg, respiratory rate 16/min, SpO2 98% on room air, and temperature 36.8 C. She acknowledges feeling hopeless but denies current suicidal thoughts. Mental state examination reveals no evidence of psychosis. Which one of the following factors would most significantly increase her risk of completed suicide?",
        "17": "A 31-year-old woman presents to the General Practice clinic with a five-day history of a painful lesion on her right hand. She works as a sheep farmer and recently assisted with shearing on her property. She has no significant medical history apart from type 2 diabetes mellitus, for which she takes metformin. She returned from a backpacking holiday in Vietnam three weeks ago. On examination, she appears well. Her pulse rate is 78/min, blood pressure 125/80 mmHg, respiratory rate 16/min, SpO2 98% on room air, and temperature 37.4 C. On her right index finger, there erythematous nodule with a central depression, surrounded by a red halo. The lesion is firm and painful to touch. There is mild lymphadenopathy in her right axilla. Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "19": "A 14-year-old presents to the General Practice clinic with their parent expressing feelings of depression. Their parent describes them always being a shy child, but states they reported low mood and anxiety for the past four years, and that this has worsened over the past eight months. They have been taking sertraline for depression for the past year though report minimal effect. They report feeling uncomfortable in their body, and have particularly struggled with the onset of menstruation eight months ago, also reporting distress with recent breast development. They avoid looking in mirrors and wear loose-fitting clothes, though do not restrict their eating or engage in purging behaviour. They have difficulty participating in physical education classes and avoid using public bathrooms due to worrying about being seen by classmates. They deny any suicidal ideation or self-harm. Their parent notes they have become increasingly withdrawn and their school grades have declined. On examination, the patient appears withdrawn but cooperative. Their vital signs are within normal limits. Mental state examination reveals a depressed mood with appropriate affect. There are no signs of psychosis or thought disorder. Which one of the following is the most appropriate initial management?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Renal - Glomerulonephritis / Nephrotic Syndrome (MCQ)": {
        "3": "A 32-year-old woman presents due to episodic flank pain on the left side and blood in the urine. She has had these episodic pains for the last year but recently they have started to recur more frequently. Her father required dialysis in his fifties.\n\nOn examination her blood pressure is 158/96 mmHg, and there are bilateral ballotable masses in the flanks. Urinalysis shows microscopic haematuria with no proteinuria, no red cell casts and no dysmorphic red cells.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate initial investigation?",
        "8": "A 35-year-old man presents with a two-month history of headache, facial swelling, and ankle swelling. On further questioning, he has noticed frothy, but normal coloured, urine, He was diagnosed with type 2 diabetes mellitus 10 years ago, for which he takes metformin. He ceased intravenous drug use six months ago. On examination, his blood pressure 150/90 mmHg, pulse is 82/min, respiratory rate 16/min and SpO2 99% on room air. There is bilateral pitting oedema of his ankles. Chest examination reveals clear breath sounds bilaterally with no signs of respiratory distress. The abdominal examination indicates a soft, non-tender abdomen without hepatomegaly or splenomegaly. Aurine dipstick demonstrates 3+ proteinuria and his blood tests show an albumin level of 22g/L (32-459/L). A renal biopsy is performed which shows: * light microscopy: segmental obliteration of the glomerular tuft * electron microscopy: effacement of foot processes Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "10": "A 54-year-old woman presents to the Emergency Department with two episodes of haematuria and lower bilateral flank pain since this morning. She denies dysuria , difficulty voiding, nausea or vomiting. Five months ago, she was diagnosed with severe bilateral knee osteoarthritis, managed with ibuprofen 400 mg three times a day, She has a 30 pack-year smoking history and drinks 1-2 glasses of red wine a week. On examination her temperature is 38.6 C, pulse 94/min, blood pressure 125/84 mmHg and O2 saturation 99% on room air. Physical examination shows a slight diffuse maculopapular rash across her back and bilateral flank tenderness. Bony nodules are noted over many of the distal and proximal interphalangeal joints of the hands. Urinalysis shows blood +++ and protein +. Moderate renal impairment is present. Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Statistics / Evidence Based Medicine - Biostatistics (MCQ)": {
        "14": "A 63-year-old man presents with a one-day history of new onset severe, dull aching right-sided headache and retro-orbital pain that developed gradually over several hours. He gives a history of being hit on the neck during a martial arts class the day before.\n\nOn examination there is drooping of the right upper eyelid and a constricted right pupil which reacts normally to light. The anisocoria is more pronounced in dim light. Extraocular movements are full and there is no diplopia. The rest of the neurological examination is normal.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate investigation?",
        "11": "A 45-year-old woman is commenced on an intravenous heparin infusion for a pulmonary embolism confirmed on computer tomography pulmonary angiography (CTPA). Five days later, she develops a fever associated with chest pain and dyspnoea. There is no clinically evident bleeding. Her blood tests are repeated which demonstrate a platelet count of 50 x 109/L, a drop from a baseline of 350 x 109/L (150-450). A troponin level is negative. Her electrocardiogram demonstrates sinus rhythm. She has normal renal function. Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step in investigation?",
        "13": "An 18-year-old man presents to his General Practitioner with a one-week history of fatigue, malaise, and sore throat. He denies recent travel, intravenous drug use, or exposure to sick contacts. On examination, his pulse rate is 110/min, blood pressure 120/70 mmHg, respiratory rate 19/min, and temperature is 38.5 C. His chest is clear on auscultation. Exudates are present on both tonsils. Cervical lymphadenopathy is present bilaterally. There is fullness and mild tenderness in the left upper quadrant of his abdomen. Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Urology - Urinary Tract Infection (MCQ)": {
        "0": "A 39-year-old woman presents to the General Practice clinic with a three-day history of urinary frequency and burning on micturition. This is the third episode she has had in the last three months. She has poorly controlled type 2 diabetes.\n\nA CT scan of the abdomen and pelvis shows gas within the lumen and within the wall of the urinary bladder. There is no fistulous tract to the bowel, no colonic diverticula, no bowel wall thickening and no pelvic mass.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely underlying cause?",
        "2": "A 61-year-old man presents with a 24-hour history of increasing redness in his left eye. There is no associated pain. There is no history of eye trauma or insect bites. He does not have any changes to his vision.\n\nOn examination of the left upper eyelid there is a firm, painless, discrete nodule within the tarsal plate, away from the lid margin. On everting the lid, a yellowish collection of retained meibomian gland secretion is visible at the posterior edge of the lid. The overlying skin is not warm or tender. There is no pointing abscess at the lash line, no crusting of the lid margin, and no layered collection of pus in the anterior chamber.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "3": "An 80-year-old woman presents with a tender swelling on the medial aspect of her left eye. It has been present for a few days and occasionally there is a small amount of purulent discharge.\n\nOn examination there is a tender, erythematous swelling at the medial canthus of the left eye, below and medial to the inner corner of the eyelid, sitting over the lacrimal sac. Pressure over the swelling expresses purulent material from the lower punctum. She reports constant watering of the eye. The swelling is not within the tarsal plate, is not centred on the lash line, and there is no keratin-filled central crater. Eye movements are full and visual acuity is normal.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "9": "A 28-year-old woman presents to the Emergency Department with a two-day history of right flank pain, fever, and dysuria. She has a history of renal transplantation four months ago. Her regular medications include tacrolimus, mycophenolate, prednisolone, trimethoprim- sulfamethoxazole, and valganciclovir. On examination, she appears unwell. Her pulse rate is 125/min, blood pressure 100/70 mmHg, respiratory rate 26/min, SpO2 98% on room air, and temperature 38.9 C. She has right renal angle tenderness. Urinalysis is positive for leukocytes and nitrites. Blood tests reveal a white cell count of 19 x109 /L (4-11), C-reactive protein of 160 mg/L (<5) and creatinine of 100 pmol/L (45-90). Which one of the following is the most appropriate empirical antibiotic treatment?",
        "12": "A 45-year-old woman is admitted with a two-day history of fever and right-sided abdominal pain. Her medical history includes type 2 diabetes, hypertension, myasthenia gravis and multiple drug allergies.\n\nOn examination she is febrile at 38.9°C with right upper quadrant tenderness and a positive Murphy's sign. An abdominal ultrasound shows a thick-walled, distended gallbladder with a stone impacted in Hartmann's pouch and a small volume of pericholecystic fluid. The common bile duct is not dilated. She is septic, with a raised white cell count and lactate.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate antibiotic therapy?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Gastroenterology - Gastric Cancer (MCQ)": {
        "6": "A 72-year-old man presents to the General Practice clinic with a two-week history of a painless lump in his left supraclavicular region. He reports increasing fatigue, decreased appetite, and unintentional weight loss over the past few months. He also describes epigastric discomfort.\n\nOn examination there is a firm, non-tender, fixed lymph node palpable in the left supraclavicular fossa, in the angle between the clavicle and the sternocleidomastoid muscle. It does not move on swallowing. The abdomen is soft with no palpable mass. There is no facial plethora or venous distension on raising the arms.\n\nWhich one of the following signs is present?",
        "3": "A 48-year-old man presents with a three month history of frequent episodes of faintness and light-headedness. These symptoms are worse shortly after a meal and may last about an hour. During this time he also experiences abdominal bloating and discomfort. His medical history includes hypertension and obesity. He takes perindopril and underwent a Roux-en-Y gastric bypass for management of his morbid obesity four months ago. On examination his blood pressure is 130/90 mmHg, pulse 90/min and temperature 37.0 C. His physical examination is unremarkable apart from a BM| of 33. Which one of the following is the most appropriate management?",
        "9": "A 60-year-old man presents to the clinic with a three month history of abdominal pain, bloating, and unintentional weight loss of 10 kg. He has also noticed a decrease in his appetite, early satiety and occasional vomiting. He has a medical history of hypertension and chronic gastritis for which he has been taking lisinopril and proton pump inhibitors for several years. He has a 15 pack-year smoking history and occasionally drinks wine. On examination his blood pressure is 135/89 mmHg, pulse 91/min and temperature 37.0 . The rest of the physical examination is unremarkable. A gastrointestinal endoscopy finds a large ulcerating mass in the gastric body and a biopsy confirms gastric adenocarcinoma. A CT and PET scan of the abdomen and pelvis show a locally advanced tumour with involvement of coeliac lymph nodes. A diagnosis of stage Illb gastric adenocarcinoma is made. He is considered to be a candidate for surgical resection and will receive peri-operative chemotherapy. There are several different regimens to consider. Which one of the following is the most important factor in determining the appropriate chemotherapy regimen?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Musculoskeletal - Osteoporosis (MCQ)": {
        "2": "A 79-year-old woman presents to the Emergency Department complaining of a 12-hour history of lower back pain, worse when straightening her back. This pain came on suddenly on lifting a heavy potted plant whilst gardening. The pain does not radiate down her legs.\n\nOn examination there is focal midline tenderness over the lower thoracic spine. Neurological examination of the lower limbs is normal, with intact power, sensation and reflexes, and no saddle anaesthesia or bladder dysfunction. A lateral spinal X-ray shows anterior wedging of a single vertebral body with loss of anterior height, and generalised reduction in bone density. There is no retropulsion of bone fragments into the spinal canal, no lytic lesion and no destruction of the pedicles.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate management?",
        "6": "65-year-old woman presents to her primary care physician with complaints of back pain anda history of recurrent fractures in the past year. She underwent menopause at the age of 52. She has no significant medical history except for mild hypertension for which she is on ramipril 25mg daily. She has never smoked and does not consume alcohol excessively. Her physical examination reveals a loss of height and kyphosis. A plain x-ray of the spine shows a healed compression fracture of the L1 vertebra with 30% loss of height. There is no history of trauma, DEXA scan reveals a T-score of -3.2 at the lumbar spine and -2.8 at the hip. Which one of the following is the most appropriate management of this patient's condition?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Ophthalmology - Retinal Detachment (MCQ)": {
        "2": "A 48-year-old man presents with an 8-hour history of flashing lights in his left eye, associated with a filmy change to his vision. He has not had any previous visual problem but has worn glasses for distance vision (myopia) since childhood.\n\nOn fundoscopy of the left eye there is an elevated, billowing, corrugated sheet of retina extending inferiorly and temporally and encroaching on the macula, with an associated peripheral tear. Visual acuity in the left eye is reduced. There is no vitreous haemorrhage obscuring the view, no cherry-red spot and no widespread haemorrhage.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate immediate advice while awaiting urgent ophthalmology review?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Trauma / Emergency - Major Trauma (MCQ)": {
        "1": "A 25-year-old man is brought into the Emergency Department after being involved in a low-speed motor vehicle crash. He was the front seat passenger when the car crashed into another that was running a red light. He was wearing his seat belt and can recall the entire event. He is otherwise young and well.\n\nOn arrival he is haemodynamically stable with a GCS of 15. He has a seat-belt bruise across the chest but no abdominal tenderness. A CT scan of the head, chest, abdomen and pelvis shows no traumatic injury. He is observed for several hours, during which his observations remain stable, he remains pain-free and he tolerates oral intake without difficulty.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate management?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Endocrinology - Potassium Disorders (MCQ)": {
        "0": "A 50-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department with severe psychotic depression and nihilistic delusions that his chest is empty of organs. He is agitated and tachycardic; his other vital signs are normal. Physical examination produces no significant findings. An ECG demonstrates sinus tachycardia. Full blood count and biochemistry performed in the Emergency Department are unremarkable.\n\nHe is admitted to the psychiatric ward after appropriate investigations rule out any organic cause for his depression. He has no significant medical history and takes no regular medications. After a week of treatment with medication and an inadequate response, the treating team decides that electroconvulsive therapy (ECT) is indicated, and this is approved by the mental health tribunal a week later. He has never had this treatment before.\n\nWhich one of the following investigations is still required before he undergoes ECT?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Gastroenterology - Colorectal Cancer (MCQ)": {
        "1": "A 63-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department with a two-week history of rectal bleeding. He reports occasional tenesmus. He denies any significant weight loss. He has no significant medical history and does not take any regular medications.\n\nOn examination he appears well. His pulse rate is 78/min, blood pressure 130/80 mmHg, respiratory rate 16/min, SpO2 98% on room air, and temperature 36.8°C. Digital rectal examination suggests a fullness in the rectum.\n\nA portal-venous phase CT of the abdomen and pelvis shows asymmetrical thickening of the rectal wall forming a soft-tissue mass, with associated stranding in the perirectal fat. There is no evidence of distant metastatic disease, no bowel obstruction and no perforation. The proximal colon is not assessed.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate next investigation?",
        "11": "73-year-old woman presents to the General Practice clinic with a two-month history of vague upper abdominal pain. She reports anorexia and a change in her bowel habits. She has no significant medical history and takes no regular medications, On examination, she appears well with vital signs within normal limits. Her abdomen is soft and non-tender. Blood tests reveal iron deficiency anaemia. tii Bets, 92 | De. | ath Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis for this patient?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Ophthalmology - General Ophthalmology (MCQ)": {
        "1": "A 68-year-old man with type 1 diabetes mellitus is referred for ophthalmological assessment of his eye disease. He has noticed a gradual deterioration in his vision over the last year.\n\nOn slit-lamp examination there is a fine, disorderly network of new blood vessels growing across the surface of the iris and extending towards the iridocorneal angle. The pupil margin is irregular. Intraocular pressure is raised at 34 mmHg. The lens is clear, the anterior chamber is deep and quiet, and there is no corneal ulcer or epithelial defect.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate management?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Neurology - Motor Neuron Disease (MCQ)": {
        "1": "A 60-year-old man presents to the General Practice clinic with a six-month history of gradual onset progressive weakness in his legs, making it difficult for him to walk. He reports concurrent loss of coordination and stiffness in his arms, making it challenging to get dressed. He denies pain, numbness or tingling. He has lost 15 kg in weight. His medical history includes asthma, for which he uses fluticasone/salmeterol as needed. On examination, he appears unwell. His pulse rate is 60/min and blood pressure is 120/80 mmHg. In his lower limbs, he has fasciculations in his quadriceps bilaterally. He has bilateral leg weakness, left worse than right. He has increased tone with spasticity in the upper limbs bilaterally. He has bilateral arm weakness. His knee jerk and biceps reflexes are hyperreflexic bilaterally, all other reflexes are present in the upper and lower limbs. Light touch, temperature sensation and proprioception are intact in both the upper and lower limbs. Examination of his cranial nerves demonstrates an impaired swallow and tongue fasciculations. He has difficulty rising from a chair. Investigations reveal: * Serum Na+: 140 mEq/L (135-145) Serum K+: 3.7 mEq/L (3.5-5.0) * Serum CK: 100 U/L (30-200) * Nerve conduction studies: normal velocity, normal amplitude, and normal response to repetitive stimulation. Which one of the following has been shown to slow progression of this disease?",
        "2": "A 21-year-old man presents with a gradual onset of weakness in his lower limbs over the past few years. He has noticed that his legs have become progressively weaker, making it difficult for him to walk. Additionally, he has developed stiffness and muscle spasticity in his upper limbs. He has developed difficulty speaking and eating and has had 8kg of unintentional weight loss. He has denies sensory disturbances, such as numbness or tingling, and has no pain. Control over bladder and bowel have remained intact. The patient has no significant medical history and does not take any regular medications. On examination, he has asymmetrical muscle weakness, worse on the left than right, and fasciculations in his quadriceps bilaterally. His reflexes are symmetrically brisk. He has dysarthria and tongue fasciculations. His legs are symmetrical with no visible swelling. Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Neurology - Multiple Sclerosis (MCQ)": {
        "5": "A 28-year-old woman presents to the Emergency Department with new-onset diplopia and ataxia that began three hours earlier. This was accompanied by a shock-like sensation when she flexes her head. Her medical history is unremarkable and she takes no regular medications. On examination, there is a right abducens nerve palsy. Her vision in the right eye is reduced, with poor recognition of the colour red. When light is shone in her right eye, there is no constriction in either eye. When light is shone in her left eye, both pupils constrict. The remainder of her neurological examination is unremarkable. A gadolinium-enhanced MRI of the brain and spinal cord is performed which shows multiple small non-enhancing lesions along the brainstem, and three enhancing lesions along the cervical region. She is hospitalised and receives intravenous methylprednisolone for three days. Upon symptom resolution, she is discharged from hospital. Which one of the following is the most appropriate to offer her?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Paediatrics - Paediatric Neurology / Development (MCQ)": {
        "4": "A two-year-old boy presents to the General Practice clinic with his parent for a routine check-up. He has no significant medical history and does not take any regular medications. His parent reports that he occasionally experiences shortness of breath and has bluish discolouration of the skin. He also experiences constipation. On examination, he appears well. His pulse rate is 110/min, blood pressure 90/60 mmHg, respiratory rate 24/min, SpO2 94% on room air, and temperature 36.8 C. He has low muscle tone. His facial features include upward slanting eyes, a flat nasal bridge, and a protruding tongue, Cardiovascular examination reveals a pansystolic murmur, His ECG shows biventricular hypertrophy. Thyroid function tests show an elevated TSH of 12 mIU/L (0.5-5.0). Which one of the following is the most likely underlying diagnosis?",
        "5": "A six-year-old boy presents to the General Practice clinic with his parent who is concerned about his behaviour at home and school. He has no significant medical history and takes no regular medications. His immunisations are up to date. His parent reports that he has always been an active child but his kindergarten teacher says he is constantly fidgeting, leaves his seat, and interrupts the class. At home, he loses his toys, forgets instructions, and cannot seem to finish tasks. He had some speech delay but otherwise met his developmental milestones. There is a family history of learning difficulties. On examination, he appears well with vital signs within normal limits. He appears restless but cooperative and not dysmorphic. He is in the 50th percentile for height and weight. Which one of the following findings is required to make a diagnosis of attention deficit hyperactivity disorder (ADHD) based on the Australian guidelines?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Vascular - Abdominal Aortic Aneurysm (MCQ)": {
        "4": "A 65-year-old man presents to his General Practitioner after an episode of rapid-onset, sharp right upper quadrant pain that lasted about 20 minutes and then subsided. He has no other symptoms and is now entirely well. He is a long-term smoker with hypertension.\n\nA CT scan of the abdomen shows the infrarenal abdominal aorta to be dilated to 5.2 cm, with a rim of calcification in the vessel wall. There are no gallstones, the gallbladder wall is not thickened, the pancreas is normal, and there is no renal tract calculus and no hepatic cystic lesion.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely cause of the CT finding?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Hepatology - Autoimmune / Cholestatic Liver Disease (MCQ)": {
        "1": "A 45-year-old woman presents with a three-month history of episodic pruritus, upper abdominal pain and jaundice. On these occasions she has noticed dark urine and pale stools. Her medical history includes ulcerative colitis.\n\nLiver function tests show a markedly raised alkaline phosphatase with a disproportionately mild transaminitis. Magnetic resonance cholangiopancreatography shows multifocal short strictures of the intrahepatic and extrahepatic bile ducts with intervening dilated segments, producing a beaded appearance of the biliary tree. There is no single dominant mass lesion and no filling defect within the duct lumen. Anti-mitochondrial antibodies are negative.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Urology - Testicular Pathology (MCQ)": {
        "6": "An otherwise well 54-year-old man presents with a six-month history of a painless swelling in the right side of his scrotum. He has no history of trauma or other symptoms.\n\nOn examination there is a smooth, soft, non-tender swelling in the upper pole of the right scrotum, lying above and separate from the testis, which is itself normal in size and consistency. The swelling transilluminates brightly. It is possible to get above it. There is no cough impulse, no bag-of-worms feel on standing, and no hard irregular testicular mass.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Professional Practice - Communication / Boundaries (MCQ)": {
        "4": "A 42-year-old woman presents to her General Practitioner at a rural practice with symptoms of depression following the recent death of her spouse. During the consultation, she becomes emotional and expresses feelings of loneliness. The General Practitioner feels sympathetic towards her situation. Which one of the following best describes appropriate professional boundary maintenance in this situation?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Psychiatry - Anxiety Disorders (MCQ)": {
        "5": "A 28-year-old woman presents to the General Practice clinic with concerns about her work habits as the insistence of her supervisor. She reports spending long hours at work, often staying late to ensure everything is perfect. She has a strict routine for organizing her desk and files, and becomes distressed if colleagues move her things or don't follow her systems. She believes her high standards are necessary for success and often has difficulty delegating tasks to others. These habits are causing tension with her coworkers and supervisor. She has no significant medical history and takes no regular medications. On examination, she appears well-groomed and composed, with vital signs within normal limits. She speaks precisely and at length about her work methods, emphasizing their importance. She denies any intrusive thoughts or repetitive behaviors that she feels compelled to perform. Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Urology - Renal Cell Carcinoma (MCQ)": {
        "5": "An otherwise well 61-year-old woman presents to her family practitioner after an episode of left iliac fossa pain which resolved spontaneously after three hours. She has no other symptoms. Her medical history is unremarkable She does not use any medications. On examination she looks well, with a blood pressure of 115/75 mmHg, pulse 82/min and temperature 36.7 C. Her abdomen is soft, with no abnormal findings. A CT scan is arranged (images 1, 2). ' gee Bos 4 Bev Un! Jan Pia What does the CT show?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Oncology - Palliative Care (MCQ)": {
        "7": "Practice clinic with his wife following a recent diagnosis of motor neurone disease (MND). He has a medical history of ischaemic heart disease, heart failure with reduced ejection fraction, dyslipidaemia, type 2 diabetes, osteoarthritis, hypertension, depression, and mild cognitive impairment. His medications include aspirin, metoprolol, ramipril, furosemide, atorvastatin, metformin, paracetamol, and sertraline, He lives at home with his wife and has supportive children. On examination, he appears distressed. His pulse rate is 78/min, blood pressure 130/80 mmHg, respiratory rate 16/min, SpO2 98% on room air, and temperature 36.8 C. Respiratory examination is normal with no signs of respiratory distress, and lungs are clear to auscultation, During the consultation, he expresses profound distress about his new diagnosis, stating, \"The specialist said | might have 6 months to live, | would be better off dead now\". Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?",
        "11": "A74-year-old man is admitted to the rehabilitation ward following a left below knee amputation two weeks ago for chronic limb ischaemia. He has a medical history of type 2 diabetes mellitus, hypertension, ischaemic heart disease, and prolonged QT interval. He is a current smoker with a 50 pack-year history. His medications include metformin, gliclazide, aspirin, perindopril, and atorvastatin. He currently requires one assist with mobility and support for all personal care. On examination, he appears uncomfortable. His pulse rate is 78/min, blood pressure 135/85 mmdg, respiratory rate 16/min, SpO2 97% on room air, and temperature 36.7 C. The surgical site is well-healed with no signs of infection. He has significant postural hypotension with a drop of 25 mmHg on standing. He reports severe burning pain and electric shock sensations in his amputated limb, which are interfering with his sleep and rehabilitation exercises. He also reports low mood and anhedonia since the operation. He has requested a referral to a palliative care physician due to his current symptoms and the distress it is causing him. Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?",
        "13": "75-year-old woman is admitted to the palliative care ward for end of life care. She has metastatic breast cancer with bone and liver metastases. Her other medical conditions include hypertension, osteoarthritis, Type 2 diabetes mellitus and anxiety. Her regular medications include oxycodone sustained-release and ondansetron. She reports smoking cannabis daily for the past eight months to manage pain, nausea, and poor appetite. On examination, she appears distressed. Her pulse rate is 95/min, blood pressure 145/85 mmHg, respiratory rate 20/min, SpO2 96% on room air, and temperature 37.2 C. She is agitated, sweating, and reports difficulty sleeping. She describes increased anxiety, irritability, and worsening nausea since admission 24 hours ago when she stopped using cannabis. Which one of the following is the most appropriate management for her cannabis withdrawal symptoms?",
        "15": "An 86-year-old woman presents to the General Practice clinic with pneumonia and is subsequently admitted to hospital. She has a history of metastatic breast cancer. Her regular medications are not mentioned. Following a family discussion her goals of care are changed to comfort care, and all active treatment is stopped. Over the last 24 hours, her breathing has worsened. She has been prescribed subcutaneous morphine 2.5 mg as required every two hours, with the nurses administering 25 mg in total. There have also been several STAT doses of subcutaneous glycopyrrolate 400 mcg given for hypersecretion and one STAT dose of subcutaneous midazolam 5 mg for agitation. On examination, she is not conscious and appears to have some increased work of breathing with extended pauses noted between noisy and rattly breaths, Her respiratory rate is 22/min, oxygen saturation 93% on room air, and temperature 37.3 C, Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Infectious Disease - Gastroenteritis / Food Poisoning (MCQ)": {
        "6": "A five-year-old boy presents to the Emergency Department with his parents with a three-day history of episodic vomiting and watery diarrhoea. He had two episodes of vomiting and three episodes of diarrhoea the night prior to admission. This has been accompanied by a mild fever and poor oral intake. He last vomited 10 hours ago and there are no current ongoing fluid losses. He has no significant medical history and does not take any regular medications. On examination, he appears lethargic but rousable. His pulse rate is 120/min, blood pressure 105/62 mmHg, respiratory rate 36/min, SpO2 98% on room air, and temperature 37.8 C. His mucous membranes are dry and capillary refill time is three seconds. His medical record states that he weighed 12.5 kg last week. Currently he weighs 12.0 kg. The paediatric consultant advises to start prompt, but not rapid, nasogastric rehydration therapy. Which one of the following is the most appropriate rate for the nasogastric fluid regimen (maintenance and replacement)?",
        "10": "A two-year-old boy presents to the Emergency Department with a three-day history of bloody diarrhoea. He has had eight episodes of loose, bloody stools in the past 24 hours. He has no significant medical history and takes no regular medications. His immunisations are up to date. His family recently visited a petting zoo and ate at a fast-food restaurant. On examination, he appears unwell. His pulse rate is 130/min, blood pressure 90/60 mmHg, respiratory rate 28/min, SpO2 98% on room air, and temperature 37.2 C. He has dry mucous membranes and decreased skin turgor. His abdomen is soft but diffusely tender. Investigations reveal: Haemoglobin: 115 g/L (110-140) White cell count: 8.5 x 10*9/L (5.0-15.0) Platelet count: 280 x 10*9/L (150-400) C-reactive protein: 15 mg/L (<5) Stool microscopy: Red blood cells present, minimal white blood cells Which one of the following is the most likely causative organism?",
        "11": "A 30-year-old man presents to the GP wanting to address his chronic lower back pain that has been negatively impacting his sleep. After asking further about his history, you find out that he has had lower back pain for the past year that radiates to his both his buttocks and sometimes wakes him up in the middle of the night. He also has back stiffness in the mornings for approximately an hour before he takes a hot shower, and the pain is relieved. He says his father had similar issues with back pain and stiffness. On examination, there is decreased range of motion on flexion of the lumbar spine due to stiffness and slight pain. His sacroiliac joints are also tender to palpation bilaterally. An X-ray of the lumbar spine showed loss of bone density and bilateral sclerosis of the sacroiliac joints. Which of the following findings is most commonly associated with this patient's condition?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Urology - Renal Colic / Stones (MCQ)": {
        "2": "A 38-year-old man is admitted with a 12 hour history of anorexia and lower right-sided abdominal pain radiating into his groin, He looks unwell, with a blood pressure of 120/70 mmHg, heart rate of 90/min and temperature of 37.4C. His abdomen is soft, apart from some tenderness to deep palpation in the suprapubic region. Urinalysis shows a trace of blood. A CT scan is performed (image, 2). 0 ras ) y ee Bee tiaae heey > \"SP 4 Nes Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "11": "50-year-old man presents to his General Practitioner with pain in his right flank for the past week, The nature of the pain is sharp and colicky and each episode lasts for 20 minutes, with nausea and vomiting associated with the pain. On examination, he looks slightly dehydrated, with dry mucous membrane and decreased skin turgor. His observations show respiratory rate 18/min, pulse rate 88/min, blood pressure 130/80mmHg, and temperature 37.2C. There is tenderness in his right flank. On urinalysis, blood is present. Based on the clinical picture, which one of the following site is the most likely site of pathology?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Gynaecology - Ovarian Pathology (MCQ)": {
        "7": "A 24-year-old woman presents to the General Practice clinic with a two-year history of intermittent pain, swelling and discharge from her armpits. Each episode may last about a week. She smokes and has a BMI of 33.\n\nOn examination both axillae show multiple tender inflammatory nodules and fluctuant abscesses, together with several discharging sinus tracts and thickened, rope-like scarring from previous episodes. There are open comedones with a double-headed appearance in the surrounding skin. Similar changes are present in the groin.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate first-line treatment?",
        "11": "A 28-year-old woman presents to the General Practice clinic for discussion about cancer risk reduction. She has recently tested positive for a BRCA1 gene mutation following genetic counselling. Her mother died of ovarian cancer at age 45, and her maternal aunt had breast cancer at age 38. She has already had prophylactic bilateral mastectomies. She has no children but plans to start a family in the future. She has no significant medical history and takes no regular medications. On examination, she appears well with vital signs within normal limits. A transvaginal ultrasound scan shows normal ovaries. Which one of the following Is the most appropriate management plan to reduce her ovarian cancer risk?",
        "12": "A 28-year-old woman presents to the Emergency Department with a six-hour history of severe left-sided lower abdominal pain and vomiting. The pain began suddenly while jogging. She has no significant medical history and takes no regular medications. She is sexually active with one partner and uses condoms consistently. On examination, she appears distressed. Her pulse rate is 95/min, blood pressure 125/80 mmHg, respiratory rate 20/min, SpO2 99% on room air, and temperature 37.0 C. Her abdomen is tender to light and deep palpation in the left lower quadrant. Her urine beta-human chorionic gonadotropin test is negative. Transvaginal ultrasound shows an empty uterus with a 5x5 cm simple cyst of the left ovary. There is no fluid in the pouch of Douglas and Doppler shows no flow in the left ovarian artery. Which one of the following structures is most likely involved in this presentation?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Endocrinology - Type 1 Diabetes (MCQ)": {
        "4": "A 33-year-old woman presents to the Emergency Department with abdominal pain, nausea and vomiting. She is mildly confused. She has a medical history of type 1 diabetes. On examination her pulse rate is 115/min, blood pressure 105/65 mmHg, respiratory rate 28/min, SpOz 97%, on room air and temperature 36.7 C. She has a fruity smell to her breath and a deep, rapid breathing pattern. She weighs 59 kg. Investigations show a random blood sugar of 19.7 mmol/L (6.0-10.0) and ketones: +++ on urine dipstick. Her serum creatinine is 70 mmol/L (45-90), sodium 140 mmol/L (135-145) and potassium 5.6 mmol/L (3.5-5.0). An arterial blood gas analysis shows: * pH: 7.02 (7.35 - 7.45) * pCO2: 22 mmHg (35-45) * HCO3: 8 mmol/L (22-28) Intravenous (IV) fluids and an insulin infusion are commenced. Which one of the following IV fluid regimens would be the most appropriate initially?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Neurology - Traumatic Brain Injury (MCQ)": {
        "4": "She was assaulted in an attempted home robbery. The Glasgow coma score on admission was 10. A CT head shows a depressed skull fracture with minimal intra-parenchymal injury. She underwent surgical reduction of the fracture and was transferred to the high dependency unit post-operatively. She has no past medical history and is on no regular medications. Which one of the following is the most appropriate to provide post-operatively?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Neurology - General Neurology (MCQ)": {
        "2": "A 67-year-old man is day 3 post an elective right total knee replacement for severe osteoarthritis. He initially made a good post-operative recovery and was able to mobilise with minimal pain. However, he has now become confused, voluble and difficult to control. He is not responding to requests from the nursing staff who have attempted verbal de-escalation and is making threatening comments. He has a medical history of hypertension and type 2 diabetes. His regular medications include atenolol and metformin. He has a 30 pack-year smoking history and consumes four glasses of wine daily. On examination, he appears agitated and is sitting on the edge of the bed, arguing with the nurses. Further attempts at verbal de-escalation are unsuccessful. His vital signs from two hours ago show a blood pressure of 100/70 mmHg, pulse rate 100/min and temperature 38.7 C. Further examination is not possible as he is uncooperative and becomes physically aggressive when approached. Blood tests from this morning were unremarkable. Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?",
        "5": "A 54-year-old man presents to the General Practice clinic with a one-month history of double vision and a drooping right eyelid. He has no significant medical history and takes no regular medications. On examination, he appears well with vital signs within normal limits. His right eyelid is partially drooped, When looking at a single image, he reports that the horizontal images separate further when looking to the left. The left-most (nasal field of vision) image disappears when the right eye is covered when looking at this single image. Which one of the following nerves is most likely to be impaired in this patient?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Neurology - Spinal Cord Injury / Cauda Equina (MCQ)": {
        "5": "A 64-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department with acute back pain. He was doing some home renovations on Sunday evening when he attempted to lift a stack of tiles. While bending forward, he experienced a sharp pain and pressured feeling in his lower back, causing him to fall. He managed to move to a sitting position on the couch with his wife's assistance. The patient reports that he has had intermittent back pain for years, which he manages with over- the-counter analgesics. He has previously been diagnosed with degenerative disc disease and has tried various conservative treatments including physiotherapy and acupuncture with limited success. On examination, he appears uncomfortable. His pulse rate is 78/min, blood pressure 135/85 mmHg, respiratory rate 16/min, SpO2 98% on room air, and temperature 36.7 C. Neurological examination reveals bilateral weakness in hip abduction (4/5) and foot eversion (3/5), with absent ankle reflexes. There is reduced sensation over the perianal region and the dorsum of both feet. Straight leg raise test is positive at 30 degrees bilaterally. Initial blood tests show: White cell count 12 x 10\"9/L (4-11), C-reactive protein 15 mg/L (<5), Erythrocyte sedimentation rate 22 mm/hr (<20). Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?",
        "8": "A 30-year-old woman presents with a two-week history of sensory disturbance in the upper limbs. She also noticed weakness in her hands. She has no relevant medical history.\n\nOn neurological examination there is loss of pain and temperature sensation over both shoulders and the upper arms in a cape-like distribution, with preserved light touch, vibration and joint position sense in the same areas. There is wasting and weakness of the small muscles of both hands with absent upper-limb reflexes. Lower-limb power is normal with brisk reflexes. Sensation below the affected band is intact. There is no unilateral motor loss with contralateral sensory loss.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Urology - General Urology (MCQ)": {
        "3": "A 66-year-old man presents to the General Practice clinic with a two-week history of intermittent painless haematuria. He has no other urinary symptoms. He has no significant medical history and takes no regular medications. He has a 45 pack-year smoking history and currently smokes 10 cigarettes a day. He drinks about two glasses of wine most days. On examination, he appears well with vital signs within normal limits. Abdominal examination reveals no abnormalities. Microscopic examination of urine confirms the presence of red blood cells. Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Obstetrics - Labour and Delivery (MCQ)": {
        "25": "A 28-year-old woman presents to the hospital at 41 weeks gestation for induction of labour. This is her first pregnancy. She has no significant medical history and does not take any regular medications. On examination, she appears well with vital signs within normal limits. A vaginal examination is performed to assess the Bishop score prior to management of prolonged pregnancy. The examination reveals: * Cervical dilation: 1. cm * Cervical effacement: 30% * Cervical consistency: Firm + Cervical position: Posterior Fetal station: -2 The Bishop score is calculated using the following table: Factor 1 2 3 Dilation (cm) Closed yet 3-4 5+ Effacement (%) 0-30 40-50 60-70 80+ Consistency Firm Medium Soft. <i Position Posterior Mid Anterior = Station 3 2: -/0 41/42 Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?",
        "35": "A 28-year-old woman presents to the labour ward with her first pregnancy at 39 weeks of gestation. She reports regular, painful contractions for the past four hours. She has no significant medical history and takes no regular medications. On examination, she appears uncomfortable. Her pulse rate is 88/min, blood pressure 120/75 mmHg, respiratory rate 20/min, SpO2 98% on room air, and temperature 36.7 C. Abdominal examination reveals a term-sized fetus in cephalic presentation. Vaginal examination shows the cervix is 4 cm dilated, 80% effaced, and the fetal head is at station -2. The midwife explains that they will be monitoring her labour progress closely. Over the next eight hours, the labour progresses and using a partograph, the average cervical dilation rate per hour is determined to be 0.22 em/hr. Which of the following correctly describes the current average rate of cervical dilation for this patient?",
        "37": "A 35-year-old, G2P1, at 32 weeks gestation, presents to the local rural hospital with lower abdominal pain and clear vaginal loss. This occurred one hour ago when she was watching television. She reports normal fetal movements. Her 20 week ultrasound demonstrated a low-lying placenta and the pregnancy has otherwise been uncomplicated. Which one of the following medications would be most appropriate to administer first?",
        "39": "A 32-year-old woman presents to the labour ward at 37 weeks gestation with regular uterine contractions every three minutes. This is her first pregnancy, a dichorionic diamniotic twin pregnancy confirmed on early ultrasound scans. She has no significant medical history and takes only pregnancy supplements. Pregnancy has been uncomplicated thus far and the twins are normally grown. Both twins are in cephalic presentation with Twin 1 engaged. On examination, she appears well. Her pulse rate is 85/min, blood pressure 125/75 mmHg, respiratory rate 18/min, SpO2 98% on room air, and temperature 36,8 C, Abdominal examination reveals regular strong contractions. Vaginal examination reveals her cervix is 4 cm dilated, fully effaced, and membranes intact. Cardiotocography shows reassuring traces for both twins. Which one of the following would be the recommended next step in management?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Obstetrics - Pre-eclampsia / HELLP (MCQ)": {
        "5": "A 22-year-old woman presents to her rural hospital with a ten-hour history of constant right upper quadrant pain. She is primigravida at 28 weeks and five days gestation. She denies any trauma, rupture of membranes or vaginal discharge. She has no significant medical history and takes no regular medications. On examination, she appears unwell. Her pulse rate is 88/min, blood pressure 152/96 mmHg, respiratory rate 18/min, SpO2 98% on room air, and temperature 36.8 C. The symphysial fundal height is 28 cm. A cardiotocograph (CTG) is commenced, showing a baseline fetal heart rate of 160/min with normal variability and no decelerations. Urinalysis shows protein ++. Investigations reveal: + Haemoglobin 98 g/L (120-160) * Platelets 80 x 10*9/L (150-400) * ALT 250 U/L (5-40) + AST 280 U/L (5-40) * LDH 600 U/L (100-250) Antihypertensive medication, magnesium sulphate, and corticosteroids have already been commenced. Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Urology - Prostate Cancer (MCQ)": {
        "8": "68-year-old man presents for discussion of treatment options following a recent diagnosis of prostate cancer. His prostate specific antigen (PSA) is 12 ng/mL (0-4). Digital rectal examination reveals a 1.5 cm firm nodule in the right posterior lobe. Prostate biopsy shows adenocarcinoma with Gleason score 7 (4+3). Prostate-specific membrane antigen positron emission tomography (PSMA PET) scan shows disease confined to the prostate with no evidence of metastatic disease. His Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group (ECOG) performance status is 0. After discussion of all treatment options, including radical prostatectomy, he expresses a strong preference for a minimally invasive approach. Which one of the following is the most appropriate treatment?",
        "9": "A 60-year-old man is seen by a Urologist after a firm, nodular prostate is found on digital rectal examination by his General Practitioner. He has a medical history of hypertension and dyslipidaemia. His medications list includes amlodipine and atorvastatin. Staging MRI and PSMA PET scans confirm T2bNOMO prostate cancer. Upon discussion of management options, he expresses concern on the potential long-term side effects of the treatments, particularly the impact any of these might have on his sexual life. He wants to know what will provide the best balance of the risk of his cancer spread and maintenance of sexual function. Which one of the following is the most appropriate management plan given the investigation results and personal priorities?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Infectious Disease - HIV (MCQ)": {
        "5": "A previously well 18-year-old man presents to his General Practitioner with a one-week history of significant fatigue, malaise and sore throat. He denies recent travel, intravenous drug use or exposure to sick contacts. On examination, his pulse rate is 110/min, blood pressure 120/70mmHg, respiratory rate 19/min and temperature 38.5 C. His chest is clear on auscultation. Exudates are present on both tonsils. Cervical lymphadenopathy is present bilaterally. There is fullness and mild tenderness in the left upper quadrant of his abdomen. Which one of the following is the most appropriate plan of management?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Musculoskeletal - Rheumatoid Arthritis (MCQ)": {
        "5": "A 37-year-old woman presents to the General Practice clinic with a two-month history of joint pain. She reports bilateral swelling and stiffness of her knuckles, particularly worse in the mornings lasting approximately two hours before improving. She has not noticed any rashes or eye pain. Her Medical history includes being a 15 pack-year smoker and carpal tunnel release surgery on her left hand one year ago. She does not take any regular medications and does not drink alcohol. On examination, she appears well with vital signs within normal limits. There is symmetrical swelling and tenderness of the metacarpophalangeal and proximal interphalangeal joints of both hands with sparing of the distal interphalangeal joints. Recent blood tests show a raised anti- cyclic citrullinated peptide antibody, negative double-stranded DNA antibody, and liver function tests within normal limits. Which one of the following is the most appropriate disease-modifying agent to initiate alongside short-term glucocorticoids?",
        "8": "A 65-year-old woman visited her general practitioner with a two month history of troublesome joint pains in her wrists and hands. These joints exhibit stiffness, particularly in the early mornings, and this may last for more than an hour, She has a history of chronic alcohol use disorder and has been referred to the Drug and Alcohol Services for further management. She recalls having an allergic reaction to co-trimoxazole when taking it for a urinary tract infection a few years ago. On examination, she has swollen and tender metacarpophalangeal and proximal interphalangeal joints bilaterally. Her blood test results show the following * Hb: 101 g/L (115-165) * MCV: 92 fL (80-100) * WCC: 13.2 x 10/L (4-11) + ESR: 48 mm/h (<12) * GGT: 188 U/L (5-50) * ALT: 94 U/L (<51) * AST: 74 U/L (<36) * Rheumatoid factor (RF): 109 IU/mL (<20) Anti-cyclic citrullinated peptides antibodies (anti-CCP): 133 U/mL (<20) Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step in treatment?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Obstetrics - Antenatal Care (MCQ)": {
        "17": "A 28-year-old woman presents to the General Practice clinic for her first antenatal visit at eight weeks gestation. She has no significant medical history and takes only pregnancy multivitamins. She reports feeling anxious about the pregnancy but denies any current mental health concerns. She works as an accountant and lives with her partner. On examination, she appears well with vital signs within normal limits, As part of routine antenatal screening, she completes the Edinburgh Postnatal Depression Scale and scores 14 out of 30. Her score to question 10 is negative. Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?",
        "19": "A 28-year-old woman presents to the General Practice clinic for routine antenatal care. She is 20 weeks pregnant with her second child. Her morphology scan reveals a healthy fetus, but an abnormality is noted in the lower limb.\n\nThe radiograph of the lower limb shows the fibula to be present but markedly shortened and thin, with a normal-length femur and tibia. There is no anterolateral bowing of the tibia, no absent or deficient proximal femur, and no pseudarthrosis or false joint.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "20": "A 41-year-old woman presents for genetic counselling regarding her pregnancy. She is currently 12 weeks pregnant and received a result today that her First Trimester Screening has placed her at high risk of trisomy 21 (1 in 30). She is now considering if she wants to continue the pregnancy and she wishes to have further diagnostic testing to determine if the fetus will have Down syndrome. Which one of the following is the most appropriate diagnostic test to offer this patient?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Gynaecology - Menopause (MCQ)": {
        "3": "A 55-year-old woman presents to the General Practice clinic with an 11-month history of amenorrhoea, accompanied by hot flushes, night sweats, and difficulty sleeping. She reports that these symptoms are significantly affecting her quality of life. She has a medical history of hypertension, which is well-controlled on three medications, and cholelithiasis. She underwent a lumpectomy for stage | oestrogen receptor-positive breast cancer eight years ago, followed by radiotherapy and five years of tamoxifen. She completed her tamoxifen course three years ago and has been cancer-free since then. She does not take any regular medications apart from her antihypertensives. On examination, she appears well with vital signs within normal limits. She asks about the possibility of starting hormone replacement therapy (HRT) to alleviate her menopausal symptoms. Which one of the following is the most appropriate recommendation regarding hormone replacement therapy for this patient?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Haematology - Myeloma (MCQ)": {
        "3": "A 60-year-old man is referred to a haematology clinic following a finding of elevated globulins on his liver function tests. He has not noticed any bony pain, fatigue, infections, or easy bruising/bleeding. Physical examination is unremarkable, with no lymphadenopathy, organomegaly or splenomegaly.\n\nSerum protein electrophoresis shows a discrete, narrow-based paraprotein band in the gamma region, measuring 12 g/L. Bone marrow biopsy shows 6% clonal plasma cells. Full blood count, serum calcium, creatinine and skeletal survey are all normal, with no lytic lesions and no anaemia.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Dermatology - Psoriasis (MCQ)": {
        "4": "A 28-year-old man presents to the General Practice clinic for review of quitting smoking. You notice a rash on his forearms which he then notes has been there for three weeks. He reports feeling generally unwell about six weeks ago and recalls a painless ulcer on his penis that healed on its own.\n\nOn examination there is a symmetrical, non-itchy, coppery-red maculopapular eruption over the trunk and forearms, extending onto the palms and soles, with fine scale. There is generalised painless lymphadenopathy. The lesions are not teardrop-shaped, there is no herald patch, and there is no Christmas-tree distribution along the skin cleavage lines.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Musculoskeletal - Soft Tissue Injury (MCQ)": {
        "3": "A 58-year-old man presents with a three month history of left wrist pain. He cannot recall any history of injury or trauma and works as a car mechanic. He plays golf at weekends. He denies neck pain. Examination reveals normal sensation throughout bilateral upper extremities. Neck flexion/extension/rotation does not reproduce his symptoms. Strength testing is normal in bilateral upper extremities. With the patient making a fist surrounding the thumb, ulnar deviation of the wrist produces pain over the distal radial styloid. Prolonged wrist flexion does not elicit pain. Palpation over the flexor surface of the wrist base does not reproduce the patient's symptoms. Pain is not elicited with resisted wrist extension or flexion. Which one of the following Is the most appropriate next step in management?",
        "4": "A 40-year-old competitive runner presents with a three-week history of worsening left foot pain. The pain is particularly worse when he gets out of bed in the morning and improves after walking for a few minutes, only to worsen again at the end of the day.\n\nOn examination there is focal tenderness on palpation at the medial tuberosity of the calcaneus, at the origin of the plantar aponeurosis. Passive dorsiflexion of the toes and foot reproduces and increases the pain. There is no midfoot tenderness, no swelling and no neurological deficit.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate initial management?",
        "5": "A 32-year-old man presents to the emergency department with an acutely painful right knee. He was playing football and tried to perform a sliding tackle, but collided with another player. The pain is a 8/10, constant and sharp in nature, with localised swelling and inability to weight bear on the injured leg. He has no significant medical history and has been taking protein powder supplements for the past two years to build muscle strength. On examination, his vital signs are within normal limits and his knee is tender to palpation anteriorly, with no erythema or bruising. He is also unable to extend his knee actively, and passive flexion and extension of the knee elicits pain. X-ray of the right knee shows the patella located proximal to the femoral condyles and there is no fracture detected. Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Gastroenterology - Diverticular Disease (MCQ)": {
        "3": "A 48-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department with a two-day history of constant, dull lower abdominal pain. He has no associated symptoms and no significant medical history.\n\nOn examination he is afebrile with a soft abdomen and mild localised tenderness in the left iliac fossa. There is no guarding and no rebound. A CT scan of the abdomen shows a small ovoid lesion adjacent to the descending colon with a thin ring of enhancement and a central density equivalent to fat, with a little surrounding stranding. There is no diverticular wall thickening, no abscess collection, no free gas and no bowel obstruction.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate management?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Gastroenterology - Pancreatitis (MCQ)": {
        "2": "A 49-year-old woman presents to the emergency department in a regional hospital with a three day history of vomiting. Over the last two weeks she has developed anorexia and early satiety. Two months ago she was discharged from hospital after an episode of severe gallstone-related acute pancreatitis. She is dehydrated with a blood pressure of 110/70 mmHg and pulse rate of 90/min. There is a fullness in the epigastrium but no succussion splash. Which one of the following would be the most appropriate investigation?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Trauma / Emergency - Burns (MCQ)": {
        "6": "A 35-year-old man is brought to the emergency department with burns sustained in a house fire. His blood pressure is 92/58 mmHg, pulse 118/min, temperature 37.0 C, respiratory rate 22/min and SpO2 94% on room air. His body weight is 60 kg. He has superficial burns covering 20% and partial-thickness burns covering 30% of his total body surface area. He is going to be given fluid resuscitation with Lactated Ringer's solution based on the Parkland Formula. Which of the following would be the most appropriate initial fluid therapy?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Psychiatry - Eating Disorders (MCQ)": {
        "2": "A 15-year-old girl presents to the General Practice clinic with her parent due to fatigue and dizziness, Over the past six months, she has lost 15 kg through restricted eating and excessive exercise, though her body mass index remains 24 kg/m2. She describes intense fear of weight gain and body image disturbance. She has withdrawn from social activities and become preoccupied with counting calories. She has no significant medical history and takes no regular medications. On examination, she appears tired. Her pulse rate is 48/min, blood pressure 90/60 mmHg, respiratory rate 14/min, SpO2 99% on room air, and temperature 36.5 C. She has cold peripheries and fine lanugo hair on her arms. Her heart sounds are normal with no murmur. Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Infectious Disease - Sepsis (MCQ)": {
        "7": "A 68-year-old man with type 1 diabetes mellitus is referred for ophthalmological assessment of his eye disease. He has noticed a gradual deterioration in his vision over the last year.\n\nOn slit-lamp examination there is a fine, disorderly network of new blood vessels growing across the surface of the iris and extending towards the iridocorneal angle. The pupil margin is irregular. Intraocular pressure is raised at 34 mmHg. The lens is clear, the anterior chamber is deep and quiet, and there is no corneal ulcer or epithelial defect.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate management?",
        "9": "A 27-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department with a four-day history of increasing pain in his left hand. The pain has worsened to the point of being unbearable despite paracetamol and ibuprofen. He describes a history of a recent puncture wound to the palm of his hand while using a sharp instrument at his workbench. He has no significant medical history and does not take any regular medications. On examination, he appears unwell. His pulse rate is 95/min, blood pressure 120/80 mmHg, respiratory rate 16/min, SpO2 98% on room air, and temperature 37.2 C. There is fusiform swelling of the digits of the left hand, and the fingers are held in slight flexion. Any attempt to passively move his fingers is limited by pain. In addition to commencing antibiotics, which one of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Obstetrics - Ectopic Pregnancy / Early Pregnancy Loss (MCQ)": {
        "16": "A 29-year-old woman presents to the Emergency Department with a two-day history of severe nausea and vomiting. She is 14 weeks pregnant, G2P1, and has a history of hyperemesis in her previous pregnancy. She has had eight episodes of vomiting daily for the past two days and has had very little oral intake today. She developed nausea and vomiting early in this pregnancy; however, her symptoms have steadily worsened to the extent that she is having difficulty sleeping, working, and maintaining normal oral intake. She has no significant medical history other than her previous hyperemesis gravidarum. She does not take any regular medications. On examination, she appears unwell. Her pulse rate is 105/min, blood pressure 105/70 mmHg, respiratory rate 18/min, SpO2 98% on room air, and temperature 37.0 C. Her capillary refill time is three seconds, and her mouth appears dry. The rest of the examination is unremarkable. Investigations reveal a blood glucose level of 5.1 mmol/L (3.3-5.5) and urine ketones of 0.4 mmol/L (<0.6). Other investigations are still pending. Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?",
        "17": "A 28-year-old woman attends her General Practice clinic reporting a seven-week history of amenorrhoea and nausea. Urine b-hCG in the clinic is positive and a bedside ultrasound confirms an intrauterine pregnancy. The patient is G4P3, has no significant medical history and takes no regular medications. The patient decides that she does not want to continue with the pregnancy, and requests a referral to a family planning clinic which the General Practitioner provides. At the clinic one week later, ultrasound confirms a live intrauterine pregnancy. The patient is provided with medication for a medical termination of pregnancy. Which one of the following is the most appropriate choice for medical termination of pregnancy for this patient?",
        "20": "A 27-year-old woman presents to the Emergency Department with intermittent vaginal bleeding. She Is gravida 2, para 0, with one previous miscarriage at eight weeks gestation two years ago. She is currently in her second pregnancy and was due for her 12-week dating scan next week. She reports passing large clots and tissue yesterday, accompanied by severe cramping pain. The bleeding has since reduced but continues intermittently. She has no significant medical history and takes no regular medications. The couple have been trying to conceive for three years. On examination, she appears well. Her pulse rate is 82/min, blood pressure 110/70 mmHg, respiratory rate 16/min, SpO2 99% on room air, and temperature 36.7 C. Her abdomen is soft with mild tenderness in the suprapubic region. On speculum examination, the cervical os is closed with no bleeding or tissue visible. Transvaginal ultrasound reveals a thickened endometrium measuring 23 mm with heterogeneous echogenic material measuring within the uterine cavity. No gestational sac is visualised. Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Renal - Acute Kidney Injury (MCQ)": {
        "7": "A 60-year-old man presents to the General Practice clinic and is diagnosed with essential hypertension. His only regular medication is rosuvastatin. He is a lifetime non-smoker and drinks one to two alcoholic drinks per week. On examination, he appears well with vital signs within normal limits, His General Practitioner decides to start him on perindopril 5 mg once daily and order some blood tests. Two weeks later, blood tests reveal that his creatinine has increased from 120 mol/L to 150 ymol/L (50-120). Which one of the following is the most appropriate management?",
        "8": "A 28-year-old man presents to the hospital for an elective laparoscopic appendicectomy. He has no significant medical history and takes no regular medications. He is admitted and taken to the operating theatre Twenty minutes after induction of general anaesthesia with propofol and maintenance with sevoflurane, the anaesthetist notices the patient's end-tidal CO2 is rising rapidly. On examination, his pulse rate is 120/min, blood pressure 110/75 mmHg, and temperature 38.5 C. The surgeon reports difficulty with the laparoscopy due to abdominal wall rigidity. The anaesthetist immediately performs several key interventions and calls for assistance. Which one of the following is the most important step in management?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Trauma / Emergency - Toxicology (MCQ)": {
        "9": "A 48-year-old woman attends the Emergency Department stating that she is concerned about a foreign body she inadvertently swallowed four days ago. She feels well but thinks that by now it should have passed. She has no abdominal pain, no vomiting and is tolerating a normal diet.\n\nAn abdominal X-ray shows a radio-opaque, butterfly-shaped metallic object projected over the left upper quadrant, lying within the gastric shadow. It has not progressed beyond the stomach in four days. There is no free gas, no bowel obstruction and no perforation.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most appropriate management?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Musculoskeletal - Fracture - Lower Limb / Pelvis (MCQ)": {
        "5": "A 75-year-old woman presents to the Emergency Department with pain in her right hip after a fall She was unable to get up off the floor and her husband called an ambulance. She is normally in good health and the only medication she takes is alendronate, together with calcium supplements. On examination there is limited movement because of the pain and the right leg appears shortened and externally rotated. An X-ray is performed (Image Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Renal - Chronic Kidney Disease (MCQ)": {
        "4": "A 30-year-old woman presents with a two-week history of sensory disturbance in the upper limbs. She also noticed weakness in her hands. She has no relevant medical history.\n\nOn neurological examination there is loss of pain and temperature sensation over both shoulders and the upper arms in a cape-like distribution, with preserved light touch, vibration and joint position sense in the same areas. There is wasting and weakness of the small muscles of both hands with absent upper-limb reflexes. Lower-limb power is normal with brisk reflexes. Sensation below the affected band is intact. There is no unilateral motor loss with contralateral sensory loss.\n\nWhich one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Hepatology - Hepatocellular Carcinoma (MCQ)": {
        "7": "A 40-year-old Aboriginal woman presents to the General Practice clinic for management advice following a recent diagnosis of hepatitis B. She was tested as part of a community program and has received counselling, education and support around the diagnosis. She has no significant medical history and takes no regular medications. She reports feeling well and denies any symptoms. On examination, she appears well with vital signs within normal limits. There are no signs of chronic liver disease. Recent investigations reveal: * Hepatitis B surface antigen (HBsAg): Positive Hepatitis B e antigen (HBeAg): Negative + Hepatitis B viral DNA: 10,000 IU/mL * ALT: 35 U/L (5-40) * AST: 30 U/L (5-40) * Albumin: 40 g/L (35-50) * Bilirubin: 15 mol/L (3-20) + INR: 1.0 (0.9-1.2) + Transient elastography demonstrates liver stiffness of 4kPa (2-7) Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Neurology - Parkinson Disease (MCQ)": {
        "9": "65-year-old man presents to a neurology clinic with a six-month history of recurrent falls. He also reports a resting tremor in both hands and difficulty moving. His wife noticed he has difficulty closing his eyes, particularly when blinking. His medical history includes hypertension, hyperlipidaemia, and type 2 diabetes mellitus. His medications include verapamil, perindopril, atorvastatin, metformin, and empagliflozin. He has a 40 pack-year smoking history. The patient returns four weeks later. He has noticed no improvement in any of his symptoms. Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
        "10": "A 75-year-old woman is brought to see her general practitioner by her son, who is concerned about his mother's tremors. These have been gradually worsening over the last six months and are most noticeable when she extends her arms. The tremors are jerky and irregular. The son has also noticed a decline in his mother's balance and changes in her speech. Her medical history includes type 2 diabetes and hypertension, for which she takes metformin and perindopril. She drinks a few glasses of wine most nights and occasionally more on weekends. She was recently on a short course of metoclopramide for vomiting associated with a bout of gastroenteritis. Her sister was recently diagnosed with Parkinson's disease and had to relocate to anursing home due to dementia. On examination, the patient displays an unsteady, broad-based gait, breaks words into syllables while speaking (\"chocolate brownie\" pronounced as \"cho-co-late brow-nie\"), and exhibits difficulty in quickly flipping one hand over the other. Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Respiratory - Obstructive Sleep Apnoea (MCQ)": {
        "2": "A 62-year-old woman presents to the General Practice clinic with a six-month history of progressive shortness of breath and daytime sleepiness. She has a medical history of hypertension, type 2 diabetes, and multiple sclerosis. Her multiple sclerosis has been well controlled, although she has some left hand weakness and lower limb sensory changes. She takes metformin, lisinopril, and cladribine. She has a 10 pack-year smoking history but quit five years ago. On examination, she appears drowsy with a respiratory rate of 22/min. Her pulse rate is 88/min, blood pressure 145/90 mmHg, SpO2 88% on room air, and temperature 36.8 C. Her weight is 135 kg and height is 165 cm (BMI 49.6 kg/m2). Chest examination reveals decreased breath sounds bilaterally. She has mild pedal oedema. Arterial blood gas on room air shows: pH 7.35 (7.35-7.45), PaCO2 52 mmHg (35-45), PaO2 55 mmHg (80-100), HCO3- 28 mEq/L (22-26). Lung function tests show: * FEV1: 65% predicted (>80%) * FVC: 70% predicted (>80%) + FEV1/FVC ratio: 0.75 (>0.7) * TLC: 75% predicted (80-120) + RV: 80% predicted (75-120) * DLCO: 85% predicted (80-120) Polysomnography results: * Apnoea-Hypopnoea Index (AHI): 4 events/hour (<5) * Oxygen desaturation index: 70 events/hour (<5) + Lowest SpO2: 72% (>90%) Time spent with Sp02 <90%: 65% of total sleep time (<5%) * End-tidal CO2 >45 mmHg for 70% of total sleep time (<25%) Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Cardiology - Deep Vein Thrombosis (MCQ)": {
        "7": "A70-year-old man is day 6 post elective right total hip arthroplasty. His operation was uneventful. His warfarin, taken for atrial fibrillation, was temporarily ceased prior to the operation and has since been re-commenced. He has been bridged on an unfractionated heparin infusion during this time while the INR is still sub-therapeutic. Today, he is complaining of intense right calf pain. He denies chest pain or dyspnoea. His right calf appears swollen and is warm and tender on palpation. His blood tests show: Haemoglobin 130 g/L [115-155] Platelets 30 x 10 9/L [150-450] + White cell count 10.2 x 10*9/L [4.0-10.0] + INR 1.8 [0.9-1.2] * aPTT 90 seconds [24-38] Which one of the following is the most appropriate initial management approach?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Musculoskeletal - Back Pain (MCQ)": {
        "5": "A 45-year-old man in an urban setting presents to his general practitioner with a two-day history of low back pain. The pain came on suddenly when he was lifting a heavy piece of furniture and has now started to radiate down his left leg. He has never had pain of this severity before and has had to stop his work as a bus driver. His medical history includes mild anxiety. He is not currently taking any medications. On examination, he has a high steppage gait, is tender over the L4/5 region, and has a reduced range of motion with lumbar flexion, extension, and rotation because of pain. He also exhibits loss of power in dorsiflexion of the left foot. His knee jerk and ankle jerk reflexes are present, but the ankle Jerk is difficult to elicit. He has a normal plantar reflex. Routine bloods as part of his annual wellness check one month ago showed a normal renal function. It was noted that the patient has a history of anaphylactic reactions to ketorolac. A same day MRI was conducted, and showed a disc prolapse. Which one of the following is the most important immediate step?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Professional Practice - Ethics / Consent (MCQ)": {
        "11": "A 42-year-old Aboriginal woman presents to the General Practice clinic where you are undertaking placement as a medical student. She is a community health worker who has been invited to participate in a research project examining diabetes prevalence in her remote community. The research team has approached her because of her connections within the community. She explains that while she believes the research could benefit her community, she is concerned about how the data will be used, who will have access to it, and whether the community will have any control over the findings. She asks for your opinion on the matter. Which one of the following best addresses the principles of Indigenous data sovereignty in this context?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Endocrinology - Sodium Disorders (MCQ)": {
        "17": "A 64-year-old woman presents to the Emergency Department with a two-day history of nausea, confusion and headache. She had seen her GP yesterday for dysuria and increased urinary frequency, and was prescribed trimethoprim for three days. She has a medical history of epilepsy controlled on carbamazepine and has been seizure-free for eight years. She does not take any other regular medications. On examination, she appears euvolaemic with vital signs showing blood pressure 118/70 mmHg, pulse rate 90/min, respiratory rate 16/min, SpO2 98% on room air, and temperature 37.2 C. Neurological examination reveals mild confusion but no focal deficits. Laboratory investigations show: Sodium: 126 mmol/L (135-145) Serum osmolality: 265 mmol/kg (285-295) Urine osmolality: 100 mOsm/kg (500-800) Urine sodium: 35 mmol/L Other electrolytes: within normal limits Which one of the following is the most likely cause of the hyponatraemia?"
    },
    "UQ Critical Care Module :: EMED - Cardiology - Aortic Dissection (MCQ)": {
        "2": "An 82-year-old man presents to the Emergency Department overnight with a one-hour history of sudden onset chest pain. The pain is tearing in character, felt in the anterior chest, and is currently 10/10 in severity. It is associated with dizziness and shortness of breath. He has a medical history of hypertension, bicuspid aortic valve, and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease. His medications include amlodipine and tiotropium. On examination, he appears unwell. His pulse rate is 130/min, blood pressure 84/40 mmHg, respiratory rate 24/min, SpO2 94% on room air, and temperature 36.5 C. There is a pulse deficit in both femoral arteries. Auscultation of the praecordium reveals an early diastolic decrescendo murmur heard maximally along the right sternal border. An electrocardiogram shows sinus tachycardia but is otherwise unremarkable. Which one of the following is the most appropriate diagnostic investigation?"
    }
}

